const PRACTICE_DATA = {"version":1,"reviewed":"2026-10-22","paths":{"applied-ai":{"revision":1,"title":"Invalid extractions reach the queue","brief":"A ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"schema_errors","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","schema_errors":0},{"id":"case-2","schema_errors":1},{"id":"case-3","schema_errors":3},{"id":"case-4","schema_errors":0},{"id":"case-5","schema_errors":2}],"policy":{"field":"schema_errors","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"schema_errors","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when schema_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three of five outputs violate the contract; HTTP success alone concealed invalid extraction.","repair":"Put deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay.","verify":"Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors.","deliverable":"Validation report and API contract","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and validation report and api contract template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using schema_errors in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Put deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as validation report and api contract. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Support-ticket triage API — Create 30 synthetic tickets with labels.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Validation report and API contract\n\nCase: Invalid extractions reach the queue\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Validation report and API contract\nRole: GenAI application engineer\nCase: Invalid extractions reach the queue\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | schema_errors (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree of five outputs violate the contract; HTTP success alone concealed invalid extraction.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPut deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in genai application engineer.\nBefore release: Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate outputs before side effects; preserve invalid examples for regression tests.\nA stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSupport-ticket triage API: Report confusion matrix, refusals and manual-review rate.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# GenAI application engineer — Invalid extractions reach the queue\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nA ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.\n\n## Contract\nReview when schema_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and validation report and api contract template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using schema_errors in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Put deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as validation report and api contract. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nValid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Support-ticket triage API — Create 30 synthetic tickets with labels.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- Amazon Bedrock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,schema_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"schema_errors\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"schema_errors\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Validation report and API contract\n\nCase: Invalid extractions reach the queue\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Validation report and API contract\nRole: GenAI application engineer\nCase: Invalid extractions reach the queue\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | schema_errors (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree of five outputs violate the contract; HTTP success alone concealed invalid extraction.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPut deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in genai application engineer.\nBefore release: Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate outputs before side effects; preserve invalid examples for regression tests.\nA stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSupport-ticket triage API: Report confusion matrix, refusals and manual-review rate.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"7cf8509dd2148c27326ef5f9c01eedfb4bb147194e74b2fd2ea3249e76998ce2","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Invalid extractions reach the queue","scenario":"A ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Invalid extractions reach the queue\nid,schema_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: schema_errors >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when schema_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three of five outputs violate the contract; HTTP success alone concealed invalid extraction.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three of five outputs violate the contract; HTTP success alone concealed invalid extraction. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Invalid extractions reach the queue","scenario":"A ticket extractor must send any result with one or more schema violations to human review. The demo currently forwards every output.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Invalid extractions reach the queue\nid,schema_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: schema_errors >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when schema_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Put deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay.","correct":true,"feedback":"Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Put deterministic schema validation before queue publication; repair the review threshold in the local replay. Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of GenAI application engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Validate outputs before side effects; preserve invalid examples for regression tests.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Validate outputs before side effects; preserve invalid examples for regression tests. A stricter schema reduces malformed data but may increase manual review; schema validity does not establish factual accuracy. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your validation report and api contract is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the validation report and api contract with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Valid tickets proceed; each invalid output is held with its field errors. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]}]},"ai-product-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"Cancellation that users cannot trust","brief":"A draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"chunks_after_cancel","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","chunks_after_cancel":0},{"id":"case-2","chunks_after_cancel":4},{"id":"case-3","chunks_after_cancel":0},{"id":"case-4","chunks_after_cancel":2},{"id":"case-5","chunks_after_cancel":1}],"policy":{"field":"chunks_after_cancel","operator":">=","threshold":5},"expected":{"field":"chunks_after_cancel","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when chunks_after_cancel >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three cancelled sessions still append text, so the visible interface disagrees with user intent.","repair":"Reject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately.","verify":"Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID.","deliverable":"UI state specification and cancellation test report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and ui state specification and cancellation test report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using chunks_after_cancel in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Reject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as ui state specification and cancellation test report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Discarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI document workspace — Build editable generated drafts.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — UI state specification and cancellation test report\n\nCase: Cancellation that users cannot trust\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — UI state specification and cancellation test report\nRole: AI product / full-stack engineer\nCase: Cancellation that users cannot trust\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | chunks_after_cancel (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 4 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree cancelled sessions still append text, so the visible interface disagrees with user intent.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai product / full-stack engineer.\nBefore release: Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nModel loading, streaming, cancelled and failed states explicitly; associate chunks with request identity.\nDiscarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI document workspace: Edits survive reload and users can distinguish drafts from approved text.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI product / full-stack engineer — Cancellation that users cannot trust\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nA draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.\n\n## Contract\nReview when chunks_after_cancel >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and ui state specification and cancellation test report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using chunks_after_cancel in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Reject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as ui state specification and cancellation test report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Discarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReplay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI document workspace — Build editable generated drafts.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials: https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n","evidence.csv":"id,chunks_after_cancel\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,4\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"chunks_after_cancel\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 5\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"chunks_after_cancel\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — UI state specification and cancellation test report\n\nCase: Cancellation that users cannot trust\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — UI state specification and cancellation test report\nRole: AI product / full-stack engineer\nCase: Cancellation that users cannot trust\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | chunks_after_cancel (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 4 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree cancelled sessions still append text, so the visible interface disagrees with user intent.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai product / full-stack engineer.\nBefore release: Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nModel loading, streaming, cancelled and failed states explicitly; associate chunks with request identity.\nDiscarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI document workspace: Edits survive reload and users can distinguish drafts from approved text.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"106f88e04c486576af6904ff755411826d13342f30da6123b7522b36cf888245","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Cancellation that users cannot trust","scenario":"A draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Cancellation that users cannot trust\nid,chunks_after_cancel\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,4\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: chunks_after_cancel >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when chunks_after_cancel >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three cancelled sessions still append text, so the visible interface disagrees with user intent.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three cancelled sessions still append text, so the visible interface disagrees with user intent. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Cancellation that users cannot trust","scenario":"A draft editor contract requires cancelled sessions to discard every subsequent streamed chunk.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Cancellation that users cannot trust\nid,chunks_after_cancel\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,4\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: chunks_after_cancel >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when chunks_after_cancel >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Reject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately.","correct":true,"feedback":"Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 5 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Reject chunks with a cancelled request ID and reconcile server cancellation separately. Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI product / full-stack engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Model loading, streaming, cancelled and failed states explicitly; associate chunks with request identity.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Discarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Model loading, streaming, cancelled and failed states explicitly; associate chunks with request identity. Discarding stale UI chunks protects interaction but may not stop provider billing; measure both boundaries. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your ui state specification and cancellation test report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the ui state specification and cancellation test report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Replay normal completion, cancel, late arrival and retry with a new request ID. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]}]},"agent-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"The duplicate booking retry","brief":"The booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"commits_for_request","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","commits_for_request":1},{"id":"case-2","commits_for_request":2},{"id":"case-3","commits_for_request":1},{"id":"case-4","commits_for_request":3},{"id":"case-5","commits_for_request":1}],"policy":{"field":"commits_for_request","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"commits_for_request","operator":">=","threshold":2},"rule":"Review when commits_for_request >= 2 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two request IDs produced multiple bookings; retries were treated as new business actions.","repair":"Add an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects.","verify":"Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization.","deliverable":"Action-state diagram and retry proof","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and action-state diagram and retry proof template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using commits_for_request in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as action-state diagram and retry proof. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Travel approval agent — Use synthetic travel inventory.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Action-state diagram and retry proof\n\nCase: The duplicate booking retry\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Action-state diagram and retry proof\nRole: Agent / agentic AI engineer\nCase: The duplicate booking retry\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | commits_for_request (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | review |\n| case-3 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4.\nTwo request IDs produced multiple bookings; retries were treated as new business actions.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent / agentic ai engineer.\nBefore release: Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate proposed and approved actions; use durable state and idempotent external operations.\nA durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTravel approval agent: Retries and interrupted sessions preserve one approved booking.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Agent / agentic AI engineer — The duplicate booking retry\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.\n\n## Contract\nReview when commits_for_request >= 2 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and action-state diagram and retry proof template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using commits_for_request in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as action-state diagram and retry proof. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRetry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Travel approval agent — Use synthetic travel inventory.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- Temporal: https://docs.temporal.io/\n- Open Policy Agent: https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs\n","evidence.csv":"id,commits_for_request\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"commits_for_request\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"commits_for_request\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 2\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Action-state diagram and retry proof\n\nCase: The duplicate booking retry\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Action-state diagram and retry proof\nRole: Agent / agentic AI engineer\nCase: The duplicate booking retry\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | commits_for_request (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | review |\n| case-3 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4.\nTwo request IDs produced multiple bookings; retries were treated as new business actions.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent / agentic ai engineer.\nBefore release: Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate proposed and approved actions; use durable state and idempotent external operations.\nA durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTravel approval agent: Retries and interrupted sessions preserve one approved booking.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"de0561d6fe879499f66a1298716d6dd760dbd666f65038af27e03cd478352cfd","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The duplicate booking retry","scenario":"The booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The duplicate booking retry\nid,commits_for_request\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: commits_for_request >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when commits_for_request >= 2 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two request IDs produced multiple bookings; retries were treated as new business actions.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two request IDs produced multiple bookings; retries were treated as new business actions. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Temporal","url":"https://docs.temporal.io/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The duplicate booking retry","scenario":"The booking workflow permits exactly one committed booking for each approved request ID.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The duplicate booking retry\nid,commits_for_request\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: commits_for_request >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when commits_for_request >= 2 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects.","correct":true,"feedback":"Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Add an idempotency record keyed by approved action and request ID before external effects. Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Temporal","url":"https://docs.temporal.io/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Agent / agentic AI engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Separate proposed and approved actions; use durable state and idempotent external operations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 2 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Separate proposed and approved actions; use durable state and idempotent external operations. A durable workflow can retry safely only when the external operation has a reliable deduplication boundary. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Temporal","url":"https://docs.temporal.io/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your action-state diagram and retry proof is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the action-state diagram and retry proof with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Retry and resume the same approved action; a different action must require new authorization. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Temporal","url":"https://docs.temporal.io/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"}]}]},"retrieval-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"Deleted policies still retrieved","brief":"The knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"deletion_lag","unit":"minutes","rows":[{"id":"case-1","deletion_lag":0},{"id":"case-2","deletion_lag":10},{"id":"case-3","deletion_lag":18},{"id":"case-4","deletion_lag":45},{"id":"case-5","deletion_lag":5}],"policy":{"field":"deletion_lag","operator":">","threshold":60},"expected":{"field":"deletion_lag","operator":">","threshold":15},"rule":"Review when deletion_lag > 15 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two deleted sources remain discoverable beyond the stated freshness limit.","repair":"Propagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval.","verify":"Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient.","deliverable":"Freshness audit and deletion runbook","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and freshness audit and deletion runbook template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using deletion_lag in minutes. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 60 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Propagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as freshness audit and deletion runbook. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Faster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Public handbook search — Collect versioned public documents.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Freshness audit and deletion runbook\n\nCase: Deleted policies still retrieved\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Freshness audit and deletion runbook\nRole: RAG / retrieval engineer\nCase: Deleted policies still retrieved\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | deletion_lag (minutes) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 10 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 18 | review |\n| case-4 | 45 | review |\n| case-5 | 5 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo deleted sources remain discoverable beyond the stated freshness limit.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPropagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in rag / retrieval engineer.\nBefore release: Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCarry source version and access metadata through indexing; test removal and permission changes.\nFaster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPublic handbook search: Publish retrieval metrics and examples where each baseline fails.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# RAG / retrieval engineer — Deleted policies still retrieved\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.\n\n## Contract\nReview when deletion_lag > 15 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and freshness audit and deletion runbook template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using deletion_lag in minutes. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 60 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Propagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as freshness audit and deletion runbook. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Faster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCheck deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Public handbook search — Collect versioned public documents.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,deletion_lag\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,18\r\ncase-4,45\r\ncase-5,5\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"deletion_lag\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 60\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"deletion_lag\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 15\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Freshness audit and deletion runbook\n\nCase: Deleted policies still retrieved\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Freshness audit and deletion runbook\nRole: RAG / retrieval engineer\nCase: Deleted policies still retrieved\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | deletion_lag (minutes) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 10 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 18 | review |\n| case-4 | 45 | review |\n| case-5 | 5 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo deleted sources remain discoverable beyond the stated freshness limit.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPropagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in rag / retrieval engineer.\nBefore release: Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCarry source version and access metadata through indexing; test removal and permission changes.\nFaster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPublic handbook search: Publish retrieval metrics and examples where each baseline fails.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"828dbd9a8833dfc3a406530d3716f95e8ddc92add751beefa000d18b1027a7ca","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Deleted policies still retrieved","scenario":"The knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Deleted policies still retrieved\nid,deletion_lag\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,18\r\ncase-4,45\r\ncase-5,5\r\n\nCurrent review rule: deletion_lag > 60\nRequired contract: Review when deletion_lag > 15 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two deleted sources remain discoverable beyond the stated freshness limit.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Two deleted sources remain discoverable beyond the stated freshness limit. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Deleted policies still retrieved","scenario":"The knowledge pilot requires a tombstoned source to disappear from results within 15 minutes.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Deleted policies still retrieved\nid,deletion_lag\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,18\r\ncase-4,45\r\ncase-5,5\r\n\nCurrent review rule: deletion_lag > 60\nRequired contract: Review when deletion_lag > 15 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Propagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval.","correct":true,"feedback":"Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 60 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Propagate tombstones through document, chunk and cache indexes, then replay permission-aware retrieval. Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of RAG / retrieval engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Faster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 15 minutes as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Carry source version and access metadata through indexing; test removal and permission changes.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Carry source version and access metadata through indexing; test removal and permission changes. Faster reindexing reduces stale results but increases processing cost; deletion needs its own propagation guarantee. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your freshness audit and deletion runbook is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the freshness audit and deletion runbook with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Check deleted and active sources using normal user identities; a fresh ingestion job alone is insufficient. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"voice-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"Barge-in arrives after stale audio","brief":"The prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"stale_playback","unit":"milliseconds","rows":[{"id":"case-1","stale_playback":80},{"id":"case-2","stale_playback":250},{"id":"case-3","stale_playback":260},{"id":"case-4","stale_playback":620},{"id":"case-5","stale_playback":90}],"policy":{"field":"stale_playback","operator":">","threshold":700},"expected":{"field":"stale_playback","operator":">","threshold":250},"rule":"Review when stale_playback > 250 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two interrupted calls play stale audio beyond the declared bound.","repair":"Cancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption.","verify":"Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields.","deliverable":"Voice timing trace and repair dialogue","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and voice timing trace and repair dialogue template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using stale_playback in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 700 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Cancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as voice timing trace and repair dialogue. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Aggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Voice appointment prototype — Define a constrained appointment schema.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Voice timing trace and repair dialogue\n\nCase: Barge-in arrives after stale audio\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Voice timing trace and repair dialogue\nRole: Conversational / voice AI engineer\nCase: Barge-in arrives after stale audio\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | stale_playback (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 80 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 250 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 260 | review |\n| case-4 | 620 | review |\n| case-5 | 90 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo interrupted calls play stale audio beyond the declared bound.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in conversational / voice ai engineer.\nBefore release: Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack each audio generation separately; distinguish transcript revisions from confirmed dialogue state.\nAggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nVoice appointment prototype: Dates and names are explicitly confirmed and interruptions work.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Conversational / voice AI engineer — Barge-in arrives after stale audio\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.\n\n## Contract\nReview when stale_playback > 250 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and voice timing trace and repair dialogue template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using stale_playback in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 700 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Cancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as voice timing trace and repair dialogue. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Aggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Voice appointment prototype — Define a constrained appointment schema.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- OpenTelemetry: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/\n","evidence.csv":"id,stale_playback\r\ncase-1,80\r\ncase-2,250\r\ncase-3,260\r\ncase-4,620\r\ncase-5,90\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"stale_playback\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 700\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"stale_playback\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 250\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Voice timing trace and repair dialogue\n\nCase: Barge-in arrives after stale audio\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Voice timing trace and repair dialogue\nRole: Conversational / voice AI engineer\nCase: Barge-in arrives after stale audio\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | stale_playback (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 80 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 250 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 260 | review |\n| case-4 | 620 | review |\n| case-5 | 90 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo interrupted calls play stale audio beyond the declared bound.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in conversational / voice ai engineer.\nBefore release: Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack each audio generation separately; distinguish transcript revisions from confirmed dialogue state.\nAggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nVoice appointment prototype: Dates and names are explicitly confirmed and interruptions work.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"44fae58526278c321d3daefef40e94f92d7a4204a5cf81350aa289f77e59c55c","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Barge-in arrives after stale audio","scenario":"The prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Barge-in arrives after stale audio\nid,stale_playback\r\ncase-1,80\r\ncase-2,250\r\ncase-3,260\r\ncase-4,620\r\ncase-5,90\r\n\nCurrent review rule: stale_playback > 700\nRequired contract: Review when stale_playback > 250 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two interrupted calls play stale audio beyond the declared bound.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two interrupted calls play stale audio beyond the declared bound. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Barge-in arrives after stale audio","scenario":"The prototype declares a failure if cancelled speech keeps playing for more than 250 ms.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Barge-in arrives after stale audio\nid,stale_playback\r\ncase-1,80\r\ncase-2,250\r\ncase-3,260\r\ncase-4,620\r\ncase-5,90\r\n\nCurrent review rule: stale_playback > 700\nRequired contract: Review when stale_playback > 250 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 700 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Cancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Cancel the active playback generation and flush its buffer on confirmed interruption. Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Conversational / voice AI engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 250 milliseconds as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Track each audio generation separately; distinguish transcript revisions from confirmed dialogue state.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Aggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Track each audio generation separately; distinguish transcript revisions from confirmed dialogue state. Aggressive interruption improves responsiveness but may react to noise; evaluate false interruptions alongside latency. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your voice timing trace and repair dialogue is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the voice timing trace and repair dialogue with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Test interruption during synthesis and playback while preserving confirmed appointment fields. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]}]},"multimodal-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"Receipt totals without evidence","brief":"The receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unlinked_fields","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unlinked_fields":0},{"id":"case-2","unlinked_fields":2},{"id":"case-3","unlinked_fields":1},{"id":"case-4","unlinked_fields":0},{"id":"case-5","unlinked_fields":3}],"policy":{"field":"unlinked_fields","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unlinked_fields","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unlinked_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three receipts contain accepted fields with no traceable page evidence.","repair":"Retain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review.","verify":"Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations.","deliverable":"Evidence-linked extraction worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and evidence-linked extraction worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unlinked_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Retain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as evidence-linked extraction worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Evidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Receipt review workbench — Use synthetic receipts.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Evidence-linked extraction worksheet\n\nCase: Receipt totals without evidence\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Evidence-linked extraction worksheet\nRole: Multimodal application engineer\nCase: Receipt totals without evidence\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unlinked_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | review |\n| case-3 | 1 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree receipts contain accepted fields with no traceable page evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRetain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in multimodal application engineer.\nBefore release: Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nBind extracted fields to source regions; separate visual extraction from arithmetic validation.\nEvidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nReceipt review workbench: Scanned and rotated receipts are evaluated separately.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Multimodal application engineer — Receipt totals without evidence\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unlinked_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and evidence-linked extraction worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unlinked_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Retain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as evidence-linked extraction worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Evidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nValidate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Receipt review workbench — Use synthetic receipts.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Computer Vision Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,unlinked_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unlinked_fields\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unlinked_fields\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Evidence-linked extraction worksheet\n\nCase: Receipt totals without evidence\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Evidence-linked extraction worksheet\nRole: Multimodal application engineer\nCase: Receipt totals without evidence\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unlinked_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | review |\n| case-3 | 1 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree receipts contain accepted fields with no traceable page evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRetain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in multimodal application engineer.\nBefore release: Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nBind extracted fields to source regions; separate visual extraction from arithmetic validation.\nEvidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nReceipt review workbench: Scanned and rotated receipts are evaluated separately.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"a4d05d06b5366cb5d7855ecfc7bdabd1f1ad2dd2f89b782a5333c3b1415ce933","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Receipt totals without evidence","scenario":"The receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Receipt totals without evidence\nid,unlinked_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unlinked_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unlinked_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three receipts contain accepted fields with no traceable page evidence.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three receipts contain accepted fields with no traceable page evidence. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Receipt totals without evidence","scenario":"The receipt reviewer requires every accepted extracted total to have a resolvable page-evidence reference.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Receipt totals without evidence\nid,unlinked_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,1\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unlinked_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unlinked_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Retain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review.","correct":true,"feedback":"Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Retain page and region references through extraction and route unlinked fields to review. Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Multimodal application engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Bind extracted fields to source regions; separate visual extraction from arithmetic validation.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Evidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Bind extracted fields to source regions; separate visual extraction from arithmetic validation. Evidence links help review but do not prove the OCR text is right; retain the original page. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your evidence-linked extraction worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the evidence-linked extraction worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Validate referenced regions, arithmetic and rotated-page cases, not just nonempty citations. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"workflow-automation":{"revision":1,"title":"Invoices bypass approval","brief":"The simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unapproved_writes","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unapproved_writes":0},{"id":"case-2","unapproved_writes":1},{"id":"case-3","unapproved_writes":0},{"id":"case-4","unapproved_writes":2},{"id":"case-5","unapproved_writes":1}],"policy":{"field":"unapproved_writes","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"unapproved_writes","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unapproved_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three workflow runs reach the ledger without a recorded approval.","repair":"Enforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence.","verify":"Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent.","deliverable":"Workflow map and exception-handling checklist","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and workflow map and exception-handling checklist template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unapproved_writes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as workflow map and exception-handling checklist. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Human review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Invoice intake workflow — Create synthetic invoice messages.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Workflow map and exception-handling checklist\n\nCase: Invoices bypass approval\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Workflow map and exception-handling checklist\nRole: AI workflow automation engineer\nCase: Invoices bypass approval\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unapproved_writes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree workflow runs reach the ledger without a recorded approval.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai workflow automation engineer.\nBefore release: Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate extraction, approval and write permissions; route exceptions to an accountable reviewer.\nHuman review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nInvoice intake workflow: Duplicates are detected and no payment is executed.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI workflow automation engineer — Invoices bypass approval\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unapproved_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and workflow map and exception-handling checklist template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unapproved_writes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as workflow map and exception-handling checklist. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Human review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Invoice intake workflow — Create synthetic invoice messages.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Microsoft Power Automate: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,unapproved_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unapproved_writes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unapproved_writes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Workflow map and exception-handling checklist\n\nCase: Invoices bypass approval\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Workflow map and exception-handling checklist\nRole: AI workflow automation engineer\nCase: Invoices bypass approval\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unapproved_writes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree workflow runs reach the ledger without a recorded approval.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai workflow automation engineer.\nBefore release: Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate extraction, approval and write permissions; route exceptions to an accountable reviewer.\nHuman review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nInvoice intake workflow: Duplicates are detected and no payment is executed.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"0d48ffaa0bae6f870d235709851b96bb00cc584ec66ad579e9eb601c48bec1a3","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Invoices bypass approval","scenario":"The simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Invoices bypass approval\nid,unapproved_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unapproved_writes >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when unapproved_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three workflow runs reach the ledger without a recorded approval.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three workflow runs reach the ledger without a recorded approval. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Invoices bypass approval","scenario":"The simulated ledger must contain zero unapproved writes; no real payments are involved.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Invoices bypass approval\nid,unapproved_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unapproved_writes >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when unapproved_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Enforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence.","correct":true,"feedback":"Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Enforce an approval state before the connector action; do not rely on a generated approval sentence. Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI workflow automation engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Separate extraction, approval and write permissions; route exceptions to an accountable reviewer.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Human review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Separate extraction, approval and write permissions; route exceptions to an accountable reviewer. Human review adds delay but bounds high-impact mistakes; automate preparation before automating commitment. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your workflow map and exception-handling checklist is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the workflow map and exception-handling checklist with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Reject missing, denied and stale approvals; approved retries must remain idempotent. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"ai-devtools":{"revision":1,"title":"Green tests hide changed tests","brief":"The coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"verifier_edits","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","verifier_edits":0},{"id":"case-2","verifier_edits":1},{"id":"case-3","verifier_edits":0},{"id":"case-4","verifier_edits":3},{"id":"case-5","verifier_edits":2}],"policy":{"field":"verifier_edits","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"verifier_edits","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when verifier_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three apparently successful patches changed the checks used to judge them.","repair":"Run independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths.","verify":"A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score.","deliverable":"Patch-review record and independent test manifest","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and patch-review record and independent test manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using verifier_edits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Run independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as patch-review record and independent test manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Hidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Repository explainer — Index one public project.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Patch-review record and independent test manifest\n\nCase: Green tests hide changed tests\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Patch-review record and independent test manifest\nRole: AI developer-tools engineer\nCase: Green tests hide changed tests\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | verifier_edits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree apparently successful patches changed the checks used to judge them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRun independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai developer-tools engineer.\nBefore release: A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep evaluation code outside the editable workspace; test behavior on a clean checkout.\nHidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRepository explainer: References resolve and unsupported claims are marked.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI developer-tools engineer — Green tests hide changed tests\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.\n\n## Contract\nReview when verifier_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and patch-review record and independent test manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using verifier_edits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Run independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as patch-review record and independent test manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Hidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nA legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Repository explainer — Index one public project.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n","evidence.csv":"id,verifier_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"verifier_edits\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"verifier_edits\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Patch-review record and independent test manifest\n\nCase: Green tests hide changed tests\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Patch-review record and independent test manifest\nRole: AI developer-tools engineer\nCase: Green tests hide changed tests\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | verifier_edits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree apparently successful patches changed the checks used to judge them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRun independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai developer-tools engineer.\nBefore release: A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep evaluation code outside the editable workspace; test behavior on a clean checkout.\nHidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRepository explainer: References resolve and unsupported claims are marked.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"0df86c16c017cd74528fb15118a80cc464e526802f19fc265b317caa5341aa76","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Green tests hide changed tests","scenario":"The coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Green tests hide changed tests\nid,verifier_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: verifier_edits >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when verifier_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three apparently successful patches changed the checks used to judge them.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three apparently successful patches changed the checks used to judge them. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Green tests hide changed tests","scenario":"The coding-agent trial fails if an agent modifies any verifier file outside its allowed patch surface.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Green tests hide changed tests\nid,verifier_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: verifier_edits >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when verifier_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Run independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths.","correct":true,"feedback":"A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Run independent verifiers from a protected checkout and restrict patch paths. A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI developer-tools engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Hidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Keep evaluation code outside the editable workspace; test behavior on a clean checkout.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Keep evaluation code outside the editable workspace; test behavior on a clean checkout. Hidden tests improve independence but need maintenance and clear task scope to avoid unfair failures. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your patch-review record and independent test manifest is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the patch-review record and independent test manifest with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"A legitimate fix passes; deleting or weakening a verifier does not change the score. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]}]},"forward-deployed":{"revision":1,"title":"A demo that misses the customer","brief":"The customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"critical_failures","unit":"out of ten","rows":[{"id":"case-1","critical_failures":0},{"id":"case-2","critical_failures":2},{"id":"case-3","critical_failures":3},{"id":"case-4","critical_failures":6},{"id":"case-5","critical_failures":1}],"policy":{"field":"critical_failures","operator":">=","threshold":7},"expected":{"field":"critical_failures","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two sessions exceed the customer-defined failure allowance despite a successful sales demo.","repair":"Reproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate.","verify":"Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access.","deliverable":"Pilot acceptance memo and handover plan","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and pilot acceptance memo and handover plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_failures in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Reproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as pilot acceptance memo and handover plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Narrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Customer support pilot — Define a support team brief.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Pilot acceptance memo and handover plan\n\nCase: A demo that misses the customer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Pilot acceptance memo and handover plan\nRole: Forward-deployed AI engineer\nCase: A demo that misses the customer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_failures (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo sessions exceed the customer-defined failure allowance despite a successful sales demo.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in forward-deployed ai engineer.\nBefore release: Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nBuild around the customer workflow and integration boundary; make acceptance criteria jointly reviewable.\nNarrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nCustomer support pilot: Show time saved, wrong-answer cases and escalation behavior.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Forward-deployed AI engineer — A demo that misses the customer\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.\n\n## Contract\nReview when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and pilot acceptance memo and handover plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_failures in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Reproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as pilot acceptance memo and handover plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Narrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRepeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Customer support pilot — Define a support team brief.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- Amazon Bedrock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html\n- OpenTelemetry: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/\n","evidence.csv":"id,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"critical_failures\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 7\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"critical_failures\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Pilot acceptance memo and handover plan\n\nCase: A demo that misses the customer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Pilot acceptance memo and handover plan\nRole: Forward-deployed AI engineer\nCase: A demo that misses the customer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_failures (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo sessions exceed the customer-defined failure allowance despite a successful sales demo.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in forward-deployed ai engineer.\nBefore release: Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nBuild around the customer workflow and integration boundary; make acceptance criteria jointly reviewable.\nNarrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nCustomer support pilot: Show time saved, wrong-answer cases and escalation behavior.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"2897f86fc0b863e6a8754ab0a5df2a278f78ae53dfe7a5ea5c0d17169b205c79","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A demo that misses the customer","scenario":"The customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A demo that misses the customer\nid,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_failures >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two sessions exceed the customer-defined failure allowance despite a successful sales demo.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two sessions exceed the customer-defined failure allowance despite a successful sales demo. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A demo that misses the customer","scenario":"The customer pilot allows at most two failed critical tasks in a ten-task acceptance session.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A demo that misses the customer\nid,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_failures >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 7 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Reproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate.","correct":true,"feedback":"Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Reproduce failed customer workflows, narrow pilot scope and agree a revised acceptance gate. Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Forward-deployed AI engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Build around the customer workflow and integration boundary; make acceptance criteria jointly reviewable.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Narrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Build around the customer workflow and integration boundary; make acceptance criteria jointly reviewable. Narrowing a pilot limits breadth but creates more credible delivery evidence than a polished unrestricted demo. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your pilot acceptance memo and handover plan is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the pilot acceptance memo and handover plan with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Repeat the same representative tasks with the customer owner before expanding access. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]}]},"ai-architect":{"revision":1,"title":"Capacity assumptions fail review","brief":"The architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"capacity_percent","unit":"percent","rows":[{"id":"case-1","capacity_percent":40},{"id":"case-2","capacity_percent":80},{"id":"case-3","capacity_percent":92},{"id":"case-4","capacity_percent":115},{"id":"case-5","capacity_percent":65}],"policy":{"field":"capacity_percent","operator":">","threshold":120},"expected":{"field":"capacity_percent","operator":">","threshold":80},"rule":"Review when capacity_percent > 80 (percent). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two designs have insufficient tested headroom; a cost estimate used average traffic.","repair":"Recalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size.","verify":"Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route.","deliverable":"Architecture decision record and capacity worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and architecture decision record and capacity worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using capacity_percent in percent. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 120 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Recalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as architecture decision record and capacity worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Reserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Enterprise AI decision pack — Gather a synthetic customer brief.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Architecture decision record and capacity worksheet\n\nCase: Capacity assumptions fail review\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Architecture decision record and capacity worksheet\nRole: AI solutions architect\nCase: Capacity assumptions fail review\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | capacity_percent (percent) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 40 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 80 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 92 | review |\n| case-4 | 115 | review |\n| case-5 | 65 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo designs have insufficient tested headroom; a cost estimate used average traffic.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRecalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai solutions architect.\nBefore release: Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMake traffic, quality, identity and recovery assumptions explicit; validate the riskiest dependency first.\nReserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nEnterprise AI decision pack: Decisions link to requirements and measured evidence.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI solutions architect — Capacity assumptions fail review\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.\n\n## Contract\nReview when capacity_percent > 80 (percent). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and architecture decision record and capacity worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using capacity_percent in percent. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 120 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Recalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as architecture decision record and capacity worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Reserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nMeasure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Enterprise AI decision pack — Gather a synthetic customer brief.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Amazon Bedrock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html\n- Microsoft Foundry: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry\n- Google Cloud AI platform documentation: https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,capacity_percent\r\ncase-1,40\r\ncase-2,80\r\ncase-3,92\r\ncase-4,115\r\ncase-5,65\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"capacity_percent\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 120\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"capacity_percent\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 80\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Architecture decision record and capacity worksheet\n\nCase: Capacity assumptions fail review\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Architecture decision record and capacity worksheet\nRole: AI solutions architect\nCase: Capacity assumptions fail review\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | capacity_percent (percent) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 40 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 80 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 92 | review |\n| case-4 | 115 | review |\n| case-5 | 65 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo designs have insufficient tested headroom; a cost estimate used average traffic.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRecalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai solutions architect.\nBefore release: Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMake traffic, quality, identity and recovery assumptions explicit; validate the riskiest dependency first.\nReserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nEnterprise AI decision pack: Decisions link to requirements and measured evidence.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"882a856fa3bb2431c47b3edcebb2e49ed40b3c8a1774cbc69d862d25d329810b","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Capacity assumptions fail review","scenario":"The architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Capacity assumptions fail review\nid,capacity_percent\r\ncase-1,40\r\ncase-2,80\r\ncase-3,92\r\ncase-4,115\r\ncase-5,65\r\n\nCurrent review rule: capacity_percent > 120\nRequired contract: Review when capacity_percent > 80 (percent). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two designs have insufficient tested headroom; a cost estimate used average traffic.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Two designs have insufficient tested headroom; a cost estimate used average traffic. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"Google Cloud AI platform documentation","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Capacity assumptions fail review","scenario":"The architecture brief requires projected peak traffic to remain at or below 80% of tested sustainable capacity.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Capacity assumptions fail review\nid,capacity_percent\r\ncase-1,40\r\ncase-2,80\r\ncase-3,92\r\ncase-4,115\r\ncase-5,65\r\n\nCurrent review rule: capacity_percent > 120\nRequired contract: Review when capacity_percent > 80 (percent). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Recalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size.","correct":true,"feedback":"Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 120 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Recalculate with peak request shape and test capacity before choosing deployment size. Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"Google Cloud AI platform documentation","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI solutions architect. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 80 percent as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Make traffic, quality, identity and recovery assumptions explicit; validate the riskiest dependency first.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Reserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Make traffic, quality, identity and recovery assumptions explicit; validate the riskiest dependency first. Reserved headroom costs money but reduces overload risk; capacity must be measured for the intended workload. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"Google Cloud AI platform documentation","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your architecture decision record and capacity worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the architecture decision record and capacity worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Measure latency and error rates at the proposed peak, then exercise the fallback route. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"Google Cloud AI platform documentation","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"platform":{"revision":1,"title":"A tenant exhausts the shared budget","brief":"The gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"accepted_requests","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","accepted_requests":4},{"id":"case-2","accepted_requests":10},{"id":"case-3","accepted_requests":11},{"id":"case-4","accepted_requests":25},{"id":"case-5","accepted_requests":8}],"policy":{"field":"accepted_requests","operator":">=","threshold":30},"expected":{"field":"accepted_requests","operator":">=","threshold":11},"rule":"Review when accepted_requests >= 11 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two tenants exceed the configured admission contract.","repair":"Enforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue.","verify":"Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget.","deliverable":"Tenant-isolation test report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and tenant-isolation test report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using accepted_requests in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 30 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as tenant-isolation test report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Shared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build a tenant-aware AI gateway — Open Gateway.route in project.py. Follow the order: key lookup → input validation → tenant/model authorization → atomic quota → mock response..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Tenant-isolation test report\n\nCase: A tenant exhausts the shared budget\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Tenant-isolation test report\nRole: AI platform engineer\nCase: A tenant exhausts the shared budget\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | accepted_requests (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 4 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 10 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 11 | review |\n| case-4 | 25 | review |\n| case-5 | 8 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo tenants exceed the configured admission contract.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai platform engineer.\nBefore release: Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAuthenticate the caller before quota checks; bound queues and retain redacted usage evidence.\nShared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild a tenant-aware AI gateway: Show cross-tenant denial before model execution. Explain the in-memory quota limitation and how a shared atomic store changes the design. Record the failure drill and include the passing tests.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI platform engineer — A tenant exhausts the shared budget\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.\n\n## Contract\nReview when accepted_requests >= 11 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and tenant-isolation test report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using accepted_requests in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 30 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as tenant-isolation test report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Shared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nAllowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build a tenant-aware AI gateway — Open Gateway.route in project.py. Follow the order: key lookup → input validation → tenant/model authorization → atomic quota → mock response..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n- OpenTelemetry: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/\n- Amazon Bedrock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,accepted_requests\r\ncase-1,4\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,11\r\ncase-4,25\r\ncase-5,8\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"accepted_requests\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 30\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"accepted_requests\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 11\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Tenant-isolation test report\n\nCase: A tenant exhausts the shared budget\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Tenant-isolation test report\nRole: AI platform engineer\nCase: A tenant exhausts the shared budget\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | accepted_requests (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 4 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 10 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 11 | review |\n| case-4 | 25 | review |\n| case-5 | 8 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo tenants exceed the configured admission contract.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai platform engineer.\nBefore release: Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAuthenticate the caller before quota checks; bound queues and retain redacted usage evidence.\nShared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild a tenant-aware AI gateway: Show cross-tenant denial before model execution. Explain the in-memory quota limitation and how a shared atomic store changes the design. Record the failure drill and include the passing tests.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"c0385a70e18d5f777e203a9659c92fb365071a34e16e58b59e4b93b670d133cb","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A tenant exhausts the shared budget","scenario":"The gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A tenant exhausts the shared budget\nid,accepted_requests\r\ncase-1,4\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,11\r\ncase-4,25\r\ncase-5,8\r\n\nCurrent review rule: accepted_requests >= 30\nRequired contract: Review when accepted_requests >= 11 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two tenants exceed the configured admission contract.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two tenants exceed the configured admission contract. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A tenant exhausts the shared budget","scenario":"The gateway lab permits at most ten accepted requests per tenant per window.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A tenant exhausts the shared budget\nid,accepted_requests\r\ncase-1,4\r\ncase-2,10\r\ncase-3,11\r\ncase-4,25\r\ncase-5,8\r\n\nCurrent review rule: accepted_requests >= 30\nRequired contract: Review when accepted_requests >= 11 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Enforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue.","correct":true,"feedback":"Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 30 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Enforce identity-scoped limits atomically before work enters the shared queue. Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI platform engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Authenticate the caller before quota checks; bound queues and retain redacted usage evidence.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Shared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 11 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Authenticate the caller before quota checks; bound queues and retain redacted usage evidence. Shared capacity improves utilization but needs fairness controls and explicit overload behavior. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your tenant-isolation test report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the tenant-isolation test report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Allowed traffic succeeds; excess requests fail without consuming another tenant’s budget. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"}]}]},"mlops":{"revision":1,"title":"Training results cannot be reproduced","brief":"Every promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_lineage","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_lineage":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_lineage":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_lineage":2},{"id":"case-4","missing_lineage":0},{"id":"case-5","missing_lineage":1}],"policy":{"field":"missing_lineage","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"missing_lineage","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_lineage >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three candidates lack sufficient lineage for reproducible promotion.","repair":"Block promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked.","verify":"Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance.","deliverable":"Model promotion evidence manifest","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and model promotion evidence manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_lineage in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Block promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as model promotion evidence manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Train, version and promote a small model — Read dataset, fit and train. The label is x > 0; seed 42 and disjoint 90/30 rows make the exercise reproducible..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Model promotion evidence manifest\n\nCase: Training results cannot be reproduced\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Model promotion evidence manifest\nRole: MLOps / ML platform engineer\nCase: Training results cannot be reproduced\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_lineage (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree candidates lack sufficient lineage for reproducible promotion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nBlock promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in mlops / ml platform engineer.\nBefore release: Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nVersion training inputs and evaluation datasets; promote immutable artifacts after independent checks.\nMore lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTrain, version and promote a small model: Record data and code lineage plus the split strategy. Demonstrate a rejected promotion. Describe why repeated tuning on this holdout would contaminate evaluation.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# MLOps / ML platform engineer — Training results cannot be reproduced\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nEvery promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_lineage >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and model promotion evidence manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_lineage in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Block promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as model promotion evidence manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Train, version and promote a small model — Read dataset, fit and train. The label is x > 0; seed 42 and disjoint 90/30 rows make the exercise reproducible..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_lineage\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_lineage\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_lineage\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Model promotion evidence manifest\n\nCase: Training results cannot be reproduced\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Model promotion evidence manifest\nRole: MLOps / ML platform engineer\nCase: Training results cannot be reproduced\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_lineage (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree candidates lack sufficient lineage for reproducible promotion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nBlock promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in mlops / ml platform engineer.\nBefore release: Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nVersion training inputs and evaluation datasets; promote immutable artifacts after independent checks.\nMore lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTrain, version and promote a small model: Record data and code lineage plus the split strategy. Demonstrate a rejected promotion. Describe why repeated tuning on this holdout would contaminate evaluation.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"5936ea385a6deac62829e2fbbb0dcbe6b20b4524a9e26956b2715e12501de405","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Training results cannot be reproduced","scenario":"Every promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Training results cannot be reproduced\nid,missing_lineage\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_lineage >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when missing_lineage >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three candidates lack sufficient lineage for reproducible promotion.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three candidates lack sufficient lineage for reproducible promotion. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Training results cannot be reproduced","scenario":"Every promoted model must reference both its dataset version and code revision. Count missing references.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Training results cannot be reproduced\nid,missing_lineage\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_lineage >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when missing_lineage >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Block promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked.","correct":true,"feedback":"Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Block promotion until immutable code, data and model artifacts are linked. Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of MLOps / ML platform engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Version training inputs and evaluation datasets; promote immutable artifacts after independent checks.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Version training inputs and evaluation datasets; promote immutable artifacts after independent checks. More lineage metadata requires discipline but is cheaper than reconstructing an unexplained production model. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your model promotion evidence manifest is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the model promotion evidence manifest with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Rebuild a candidate from its recorded inputs and compare declared evaluation tolerance. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]}]},"llmops":{"revision":1,"title":"Prompt changes miss a language slice","brief":"The pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"critical_failures","unit":"out of ten","rows":[{"id":"case-1","critical_failures":1},{"id":"case-2","critical_failures":2},{"id":"case-3","critical_failures":3},{"id":"case-4","critical_failures":5},{"id":"case-5","critical_failures":0}],"policy":{"field":"critical_failures","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"critical_failures","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two language slices fail the declared gate; a pooled average hid the regression.","repair":"Add slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved.","verify":"Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions.","deliverable":"Prompt release scorecard","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and prompt release scorecard template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_failures in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as prompt release scorecard. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Small slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build an evaluation release gate — Open cases.json and baseline.json. Match each output ID to its test case; the evaluator rejects incomplete or duplicate output sets..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Prompt release scorecard\n\nCase: Prompt changes miss a language slice\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Prompt release scorecard\nRole: LLMOps / GenAIOps engineer\nCase: Prompt changes miss a language slice\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_failures (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo language slices fail the declared gate; a pooled average hid the regression.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in llmops / genaiops engineer.\nBefore release: Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack prompt, retrieval and model versions together; inspect consequential slices independently.\nSmall slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild an evaluation release gate: Preserve baseline and rejected-candidate reports. Explain each failure independently of the aggregate score. Version dataset, evaluator and candidate configuration together.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# LLMOps / GenAIOps engineer — Prompt changes miss a language slice\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.\n\n## Contract\nReview when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and prompt release scorecard template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_failures in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as prompt release scorecard. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Small slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRe-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build an evaluation release gate — Open cases.json and baseline.json. Match each output ID to its test case; the evaluator rejects incomplete or duplicate output sets..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- OpenTelemetry: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/\n","evidence.csv":"id,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,0\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"critical_failures\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"critical_failures\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Prompt release scorecard\n\nCase: Prompt changes miss a language slice\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Prompt release scorecard\nRole: LLMOps / GenAIOps engineer\nCase: Prompt changes miss a language slice\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_failures (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo language slices fail the declared gate; a pooled average hid the regression.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in llmops / genaiops engineer.\nBefore release: Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack prompt, retrieval and model versions together; inspect consequential slices independently.\nSmall slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild an evaluation release gate: Preserve baseline and rejected-candidate reports. Explain each failure independently of the aggregate score. Version dataset, evaluator and candidate configuration together.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"43aea7e74a15a22c4edb2d864b59765c42b5f68f9c9a62f7bb61595d1c88be9f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Prompt changes miss a language slice","scenario":"The pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Prompt changes miss a language slice\nid,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,0\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_failures >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two language slices fail the declared gate; a pooled average hid the regression.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two language slices fail the declared gate; a pooled average hid the regression. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Prompt changes miss a language slice","scenario":"The pilot permits at most two critical failures per ten reviewed multilingual tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Prompt changes miss a language slice\nid,critical_failures\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,0\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_failures >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when critical_failures >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved.","correct":true,"feedback":"Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Add slice-specific evaluation gates and hold the changed prompt until failed cases are resolved. Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of LLMOps / GenAIOps engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Track prompt, retrieval and model versions together; inspect consequential slices independently.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Small slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Track prompt, retrieval and model versions together; inspect consequential slices independently. Small slices produce noisy scores; require adequate review rather than silently dropping difficult languages. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your prompt release scorecard is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the prompt release scorecard with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Re-evaluate frozen and unseen cases, recording rubric and prompt versions. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}]}]},"inference":{"revision":1,"title":"A longer prompt overloads serving","brief":"The workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"ttft","unit":"milliseconds","rows":[{"id":"case-1","ttft":300},{"id":"case-2","ttft":900},{"id":"case-3","ttft":1001},{"id":"case-4","ttft":2400},{"id":"case-5","ttft":750}],"policy":{"field":"ttft","operator":">","threshold":2500},"expected":{"field":"ttft","operator":">","threshold":1000},"rule":"Review when ttft > 1000 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two long-context requests miss the interactive latency bound.","repair":"Inspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries.","verify":"Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency.","deliverable":"Serving benchmark and overload policy","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and serving benchmark and overload policy template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using ttft in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 2500 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as serving benchmark and overload policy. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Larger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Measure a bounded inference queue — Read benchmark: each request records queue, service and completion latency from one burst start time..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Serving benchmark and overload policy\n\nCase: A longer prompt overloads serving\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Serving benchmark and overload policy\nRole: Inference / model-serving engineer\nCase: A longer prompt overloads serving\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | ttft (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 300 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 900 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 1001 | review |\n| case-4 | 2400 | review |\n| case-5 | 750 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo long-context requests miss the interactive latency bound.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in inference / model-serving engineer.\nBefore release: Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate admission, queue and generation timing; benchmark realistic prompt/output shapes.\nLarger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMeasure a bounded inference queue: Retain raw records for baseline, concurrency change and failure drill. Separate successful throughput from attempted request rate. Label every mock result and explain which real serving metrics remain unmeasured.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Inference / model-serving engineer — A longer prompt overloads serving\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.\n\n## Contract\nReview when ttft > 1000 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and serving benchmark and overload policy template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using ttft in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 2500 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as serving benchmark and overload policy. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Larger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReplay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Measure a bounded inference queue — Read benchmark: each request records queue, service and completion latency from one burst start time..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- vLLM: https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- Prometheus: https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\n","evidence.csv":"id,ttft\r\ncase-1,300\r\ncase-2,900\r\ncase-3,1001\r\ncase-4,2400\r\ncase-5,750\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"ttft\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 2500\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"ttft\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 1000\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Serving benchmark and overload policy\n\nCase: A longer prompt overloads serving\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Serving benchmark and overload policy\nRole: Inference / model-serving engineer\nCase: A longer prompt overloads serving\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | ttft (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 300 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 900 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 1001 | review |\n| case-4 | 2400 | review |\n| case-5 | 750 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo long-context requests miss the interactive latency bound.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in inference / model-serving engineer.\nBefore release: Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate admission, queue and generation timing; benchmark realistic prompt/output shapes.\nLarger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMeasure a bounded inference queue: Retain raw records for baseline, concurrency change and failure drill. Separate successful throughput from attempted request rate. Label every mock result and explain which real serving metrics remain unmeasured.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"d1f83ece96e869e5011f1070548518afd1d493fa345856534c57e1e328da5497","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A longer prompt overloads serving","scenario":"The workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A longer prompt overloads serving\nid,ttft\r\ncase-1,300\r\ncase-2,900\r\ncase-3,1001\r\ncase-4,2400\r\ncase-5,750\r\n\nCurrent review rule: ttft > 2500\nRequired contract: Review when ttft > 1000 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two long-context requests miss the interactive latency bound.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Two long-context requests miss the interactive latency bound. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A longer prompt overloads serving","scenario":"The workload contract flags time to first token above 1,000 ms for interactive requests.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A longer prompt overloads serving\nid,ttft\r\ncase-1,300\r\ncase-2,900\r\ncase-3,1001\r\ncase-4,2400\r\ncase-5,750\r\n\nCurrent review rule: ttft > 2500\nRequired contract: Review when ttft > 1000 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Inspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries.","correct":true,"feedback":"Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 2500 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Inspect queue delay and KV-cache pressure; bound admission before adding indiscriminate retries. Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Inference / model-serving engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1000 milliseconds as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate admission, queue and generation timing; benchmark realistic prompt/output shapes.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Larger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Separate admission, queue and generation timing; benchmark realistic prompt/output shapes. Larger batches improve throughput but can harm interactive tail latency; define which objective governs. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your serving benchmark and overload policy is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the serving benchmark and overload policy with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Replay the same prompt-length and concurrency distribution and verify tail latency. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]}]},"gpu":{"revision":1,"title":"A job requests more memory than it has","brief":"For this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"requested_memory","unit":"GiB","rows":[{"id":"case-1","requested_memory":12},{"id":"case-2","requested_memory":24},{"id":"case-3","requested_memory":25},{"id":"case-4","requested_memory":40},{"id":"case-5","requested_memory":18}],"policy":{"field":"requested_memory","operator":">","threshold":48},"expected":{"field":"requested_memory","operator":">","threshold":24},"rule":"Review when requested_memory > 24 (GiB). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two jobs cannot fit the specified device memory, even if utilization looks low.","repair":"Choose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone.","verify":"Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency.","deliverable":"GPU placement worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and gpu placement worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using requested_memory in GiB. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 48 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Choose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as gpu placement worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Smaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Plan GPU memory from a device snapshot — Open inventory.csv and identify total versus free MiB. Compute weights + cache + overhead for the default one-billion-parameter case..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — GPU placement worksheet\n\nCase: A job requests more memory than it has\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — GPU placement worksheet\nRole: GPU infrastructure engineer\nCase: A job requests more memory than it has\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nFor this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | requested_memory (GiB) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 12 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 24 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 25 | review |\n| case-4 | 40 | review |\n| case-5 | 18 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo jobs cannot fit the specified device memory, even if utilization looks low.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nChoose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in gpu infrastructure engineer.\nBefore release: Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nModel device memory and compatibility explicitly; record peak rather than only idle memory.\nSmaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPlan GPU memory from a device snapshot: Show every unit conversion and assumption. Use free memory rather than installed capacity in the admission check. List runtime overhead, fragmentation, model format and backend compatibility as further validation work.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# GPU infrastructure engineer — A job requests more memory than it has\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nFor this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.\n\n## Contract\nReview when requested_memory > 24 (GiB). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and gpu placement worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using requested_memory in GiB. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 48 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Choose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as gpu placement worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Smaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCheck weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Plan GPU memory from a device snapshot — Open inventory.csv and identify total versus free MiB. Compute weights + cache + overhead for the default one-billion-parameter case..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n- Prometheus: https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\n","evidence.csv":"id,requested_memory\r\ncase-1,12\r\ncase-2,24\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,40\r\ncase-5,18\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"requested_memory\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 48\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"requested_memory\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 24\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — GPU placement worksheet\n\nCase: A job requests more memory than it has\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — GPU placement worksheet\nRole: GPU infrastructure engineer\nCase: A job requests more memory than it has\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nFor this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | requested_memory (GiB) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 12 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 24 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 25 | review |\n| case-4 | 40 | review |\n| case-5 | 18 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo jobs cannot fit the specified device memory, even if utilization looks low.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nChoose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in gpu infrastructure engineer.\nBefore release: Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nModel device memory and compatibility explicitly; record peak rather than only idle memory.\nSmaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPlan GPU memory from a device snapshot: Show every unit conversion and assumption. Use free memory rather than installed capacity in the admission check. List runtime overhead, fragmentation, model format and backend compatibility as further validation work.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"605c2cd59a33f008cd73e51c08cfde5e119c6e4e28f438abacfbb3f6babb1802","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A job requests more memory than it has","scenario":"For this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A job requests more memory than it has\nid,requested_memory\r\ncase-1,12\r\ncase-2,24\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,40\r\ncase-5,18\r\n\nCurrent review rule: requested_memory > 48\nRequired contract: Review when requested_memory > 24 (GiB). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two jobs cannot fit the specified device memory, even if utilization looks low.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two jobs cannot fit the specified device memory, even if utilization looks low. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A job requests more memory than it has","scenario":"For this synthetic scheduling exercise, requests above 24 GiB cannot fit the available device partition.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A job requests more memory than it has\nid,requested_memory\r\ncase-1,12\r\ncase-2,24\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,40\r\ncase-5,18\r\n\nCurrent review rule: requested_memory > 48\nRequired contract: Review when requested_memory > 24 (GiB). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Choose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone.","correct":true,"feedback":"Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 48 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Choose a compatible partition or reduce verified memory use; avoid scheduling on utilization alone. Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of GPU infrastructure engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Model device memory and compatibility explicitly; record peak rather than only idle memory.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Smaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 24 GiB as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Model device memory and compatibility explicitly; record peak rather than only idle memory. Smaller precision or context may reduce memory but can change quality or supported behavior. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your gpu placement worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the gpu placement worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Check weights, runtime and cache memory under the intended concurrency. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]}]},"sre":{"revision":1,"title":"HTTP success conceals failed tasks","brief":"The service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"wrong_critical_fields","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","wrong_critical_fields":0},{"id":"case-2","wrong_critical_fields":1},{"id":"case-3","wrong_critical_fields":0},{"id":"case-4","wrong_critical_fields":2},{"id":"case-5","wrong_critical_fields":1}],"policy":{"field":"wrong_critical_fields","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"wrong_critical_fields","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when wrong_critical_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three successful HTTP responses violate the user-task contract.","repair":"Add an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation.","verify":"Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route.","deliverable":"Outcome SLO proposal and incident note","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and outcome slo proposal and incident note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using wrong_critical_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as outcome slo proposal and incident note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Deep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build availability and quality indicators — Read indicators and the explicit good-event definition (status \u003c 500). Decide whether that policy matches the service you would own..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Outcome SLO proposal and incident note\n\nCase: HTTP success conceals failed tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Outcome SLO proposal and incident note\nRole: AI site reliability engineer\nCase: HTTP success conceals failed tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | wrong_critical_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree successful HTTP responses violate the user-task contract.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai site reliability engineer.\nBefore release: Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure user-visible outcomes and dependency health separately; tie alerts to actionable ownership.\nDeep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild availability and quality indicators: Retain three scenario reports and metric definitions. Separate availability, latency and quality evidence. Write a short runbook for the degraded scenario with an owner and rollback trigger.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI site reliability engineer — HTTP success conceals failed tasks\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.\n\n## Contract\nReview when wrong_critical_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and outcome slo proposal and incident note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using wrong_critical_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as outcome slo proposal and incident note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Deep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nVerify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Build availability and quality indicators — Read indicators and the explicit good-event definition (status \u003c 500). Decide whether that policy matches the service you would own..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google SRE books: https://sre.google/books/\n- OpenTelemetry: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/\n- Prometheus: https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\n","evidence.csv":"id,wrong_critical_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"wrong_critical_fields\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"wrong_critical_fields\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Outcome SLO proposal and incident note\n\nCase: HTTP success conceals failed tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Outcome SLO proposal and incident note\nRole: AI site reliability engineer\nCase: HTTP success conceals failed tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | wrong_critical_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree successful HTTP responses violate the user-task contract.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai site reliability engineer.\nBefore release: Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure user-visible outcomes and dependency health separately; tie alerts to actionable ownership.\nDeep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBuild availability and quality indicators: Retain three scenario reports and metric definitions. Separate availability, latency and quality evidence. Write a short runbook for the degraded scenario with an owner and rollback trigger.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"2a7df7c80851a48f0d100e9066d251c7bfe63d2499af4e704c663e23d49b81b3","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"HTTP success conceals failed tasks","scenario":"The service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — HTTP success conceals failed tasks\nid,wrong_critical_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: wrong_critical_fields >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when wrong_critical_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three successful HTTP responses violate the user-task contract.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three successful HTTP responses violate the user-task contract. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google SRE books","url":"https://sre.google/books/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: HTTP success conceals failed tasks","scenario":"The service’s synthetic probe flags any response containing a wrong critical field, even with status 200.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — HTTP success conceals failed tasks\nid,wrong_critical_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: wrong_critical_fields >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when wrong_critical_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Add an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation.","correct":true,"feedback":"Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Add an outcome probe alongside availability metrics and define an owned mitigation. Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google SRE books","url":"https://sre.google/books/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI site reliability engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Deep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Measure user-visible outcomes and dependency health separately; tie alerts to actionable ownership.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Measure user-visible outcomes and dependency health separately; tie alerts to actionable ownership. Deep probes cost more and sample only selected behaviors; pair them with broader sampled review. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google SRE books","url":"https://sre.google/books/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your outcome slo proposal and incident note is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the outcome slo proposal and incident note with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Verify good and deliberately incorrect outputs through the ordinary caller route. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google SRE books","url":"https://sre.google/books/"},{"title":"OpenTelemetry","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]}]},"finops":{"revision":1,"title":"Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes","brief":"The trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"cost_per_accepted","unit":"synthetic units","rows":[{"id":"case-1","cost_per_accepted":0.04},{"id":"case-2","cost_per_accepted":0.1},{"id":"case-3","cost_per_accepted":0.11},{"id":"case-4","cost_per_accepted":0.25},{"id":"case-5","cost_per_accepted":0.08}],"policy":{"field":"cost_per_accepted","operator":">","threshold":0.3},"expected":{"field":"cost_per_accepted","operator":">","threshold":0.1},"rule":"Review when cost_per_accepted > 0.1 (synthetic units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two routes exceed the outcome budget after retries and rejected outputs are included.","repair":"Allocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes.","verify":"Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window.","deliverable":"Reconciled AI unit-cost ledger","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and reconciled ai unit-cost ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using cost_per_accepted in synthetic units. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 0.3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Allocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as reconciled ai unit-cost ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Reconcile AI cost per successful outcome — Read ledger and summarize. The unowned row is retained in an unallocated bucket, and failed attempts still cost money..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reconciled AI unit-cost ledger\n\nCase: Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Reconciled AI unit-cost ledger\nRole: AI FinOps / capacity engineer\nCase: Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | cost_per_accepted (synthetic units) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0.04 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 0.1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 0.11 | review |\n| case-4 | 0.25 | review |\n| case-5 | 0.08 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo routes exceed the outcome budget after retries and rejected outputs are included.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAllocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai finops / capacity engineer.\nBefore release: Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure cost per accepted outcome with quality constraints; disclose shared-cost allocation.\nA cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nReconcile AI cost per successful outcome: Reconcile every request cost, including failures and unknown owners. Publish the outcome definition and denominator. Keep illustrative numbers clearly separate from actual provider bills and rates.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI FinOps / capacity engineer — Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.\n\n## Contract\nReview when cost_per_accepted > 0.1 (synthetic units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and reconciled ai unit-cost ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using cost_per_accepted in synthetic units. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 0.3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Allocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as reconciled ai unit-cost ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Reconcile AI cost per successful outcome — Read ledger and summarize. The unowned row is retained in an unallocated bucket, and failed attempts still cost money..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- FinOps Foundation: AI: https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- Prometheus: https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\n","evidence.csv":"id,cost_per_accepted\r\ncase-1,0.04\r\ncase-2,0.1\r\ncase-3,0.11\r\ncase-4,0.25\r\ncase-5,0.08\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"cost_per_accepted\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 0.3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"cost_per_accepted\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 0.1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reconciled AI unit-cost ledger\n\nCase: Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Reconciled AI unit-cost ledger\nRole: AI FinOps / capacity engineer\nCase: Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | cost_per_accepted (synthetic units) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0.04 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 0.1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 0.11 | review |\n| case-4 | 0.25 | review |\n| case-5 | 0.08 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo routes exceed the outcome budget after retries and rejected outputs are included.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAllocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai finops / capacity engineer.\nBefore release: Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure cost per accepted outcome with quality constraints; disclose shared-cost allocation.\nA cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nReconcile AI cost per successful outcome: Reconcile every request cost, including failures and unknown owners. Publish the outcome definition and denominator. Keep illustrative numbers clearly separate from actual provider bills and rates.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"f1a6e989e998f0151a613bdb057f56719d3d3105e5be22f6b0ed3b5cca0ccf4f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes","scenario":"The trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\nid,cost_per_accepted\r\ncase-1,0.04\r\ncase-2,0.1\r\ncase-3,0.11\r\ncase-4,0.25\r\ncase-5,0.08\r\n\nCurrent review rule: cost_per_accepted > 0.3\nRequired contract: Review when cost_per_accepted > 0.1 (synthetic units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two routes exceed the outcome budget after retries and rejected outputs are included.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two routes exceed the outcome budget after retries and rejected outputs are included. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes","scenario":"The trial budget caps cost per accepted task at 0.10 synthetic currency units.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Cheap tokens, expensive outcomes\nid,cost_per_accepted\r\ncase-1,0.04\r\ncase-2,0.1\r\ncase-3,0.11\r\ncase-4,0.25\r\ncase-5,0.08\r\n\nCurrent review rule: cost_per_accepted > 0.3\nRequired contract: Review when cost_per_accepted > 0.1 (synthetic units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 0.3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Allocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes.","correct":true,"feedback":"Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Allocate retries and review overhead before comparing model routes. Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI FinOps / capacity engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 0.1 synthetic units as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Measure cost per accepted outcome with quality constraints; disclose shared-cost allocation.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Measure cost per accepted outcome with quality constraints; disclose shared-cost allocation. A cheaper model can cost more per useful result if errors and review increase. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your reconciled ai unit-cost ledger is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the reconciled ai unit-cost ledger with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Reconcile total spend and accepted outcomes using the same reporting window. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]}]},"devplatform":{"revision":1,"title":"Templates create ownerless services","brief":"Every generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_service_fields","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_service_fields":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_service_fields":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_service_fields":2},{"id":"case-4","missing_service_fields":0},{"id":"case-5","missing_service_fields":3}],"policy":{"field":"missing_service_fields","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"missing_service_fields","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_service_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three scaffolded services lack required ownership evidence.","repair":"Validate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path.","verify":"A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors.","deliverable":"Service template acceptance report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and service template acceptance report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_service_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Validate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as service template acceptance report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Too many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Generate a tested golden-path project — Read scaffold and templates/. The strict name grammar prevents path traversal, and existing destinations are refused..","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Service template acceptance report\n\nCase: Templates create ownerless services\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Service template acceptance report\nRole: AI developer-platform / research-productivity engineer\nCase: Templates create ownerless services\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_service_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree scaffolded services lack required ownership evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nValidate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai developer-platform / research-productivity engineer.\nBefore release: A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine self-service defaults with tested contracts; retain an accountable owner for template changes.\nToo many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGenerate a tested golden-path project: A fresh folder can run the generated app and its tests. Existing work cannot be silently overwritten. The service contract accurately states ownership and scope.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI developer-platform / research-productivity engineer — Templates create ownerless services\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nEvery generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_service_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and service template acceptance report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_service_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Validate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as service template acceptance report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Too many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nA complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Generate a tested golden-path project — Read scaffold and templates/. The strict name grammar prevents path traversal, and existing destinations are refused..\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Backstage: https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage/\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_service_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_service_fields\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_service_fields\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Service template acceptance report\n\nCase: Templates create ownerless services\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Service template acceptance report\nRole: AI developer-platform / research-productivity engineer\nCase: Templates create ownerless services\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_service_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree scaffolded services lack required ownership evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nValidate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai developer-platform / research-productivity engineer.\nBefore release: A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine self-service defaults with tested contracts; retain an accountable owner for template changes.\nToo many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGenerate a tested golden-path project: A fresh folder can run the generated app and its tests. Existing work cannot be silently overwritten. The service contract accurately states ownership and scope.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"84895d5f3d7ad1092eb7ccc410adfb94714fcd186d82d8b514e2b14351721e6d","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Templates create ownerless services","scenario":"Every generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Templates create ownerless services\nid,missing_service_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_service_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_service_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three scaffolded services lack required ownership evidence.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three scaffolded services lack required ownership evidence. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Backstage","url":"https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Templates create ownerless services","scenario":"Every generated service must declare an owner, runbook and lifecycle policy. Count missing fields.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Templates create ownerless services\nid,missing_service_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_service_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_service_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Validate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path.","correct":true,"feedback":"A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Validate service metadata during template generation and provide a clear repair path. A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Backstage","url":"https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI developer-platform / research-productivity engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Combine self-service defaults with tested contracts; retain an accountable owner for template changes.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Too many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Combine self-service defaults with tested contracts; retain an accountable owner for template changes. Too many gates slow onboarding; require evidence that reduces concrete operational risk. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Backstage","url":"https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your service template acceptance report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the service template acceptance report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"A complete template succeeds; incomplete metadata blocks publication with actionable errors. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Backstage","url":"https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]}]},"distributed-training":{"revision":1,"title":"One worker determines the step","brief":"The toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"step_time","unit":"milliseconds","rows":[{"id":"case-1","step_time":90},{"id":"case-2","step_time":120},{"id":"case-3","step_time":121},{"id":"case-4","step_time":220},{"id":"case-5","step_time":95}],"policy":{"field":"step_time","operator":">","threshold":250},"expected":{"field":"step_time","operator":">","threshold":120},"rule":"Review when step_time > 120 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two workers exceed the step bound; synchronized training waits for slow participants.","repair":"Compare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism.","verify":"Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence.","deliverable":"Scaling efficiency and straggler report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and scaling efficiency and straggler report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using step_time in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 250 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Compare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as scaling efficiency and straggler report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Adding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Restartable training job — Train a tiny model on two workers.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scaling efficiency and straggler report\n\nCase: One worker determines the step\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Scaling efficiency and straggler report\nRole: Distributed-training engineer\nCase: One worker determines the step\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | step_time (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 90 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 120 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 121 | review |\n| case-4 | 220 | review |\n| case-5 | 95 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo workers exceed the step bound; synchronized training waits for slow participants.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCompare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in distributed-training engineer.\nBefore release: Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate compute, input and communication timing; preserve optimizer and random state on restart.\nAdding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRestartable training job: Report lost work, recovery time and numerical differences.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Distributed-training engineer — One worker determines the step\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.\n\n## Contract\nReview when step_time > 120 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and scaling efficiency and straggler report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using step_time in milliseconds. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 250 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Compare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as scaling efficiency and straggler report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Adding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Restartable training job — Train a tiny model on two workers.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html\n- Ray: https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,step_time\r\ncase-1,90\r\ncase-2,120\r\ncase-3,121\r\ncase-4,220\r\ncase-5,95\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"step_time\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 250\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"step_time\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 120\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scaling efficiency and straggler report\n\nCase: One worker determines the step\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Scaling efficiency and straggler report\nRole: Distributed-training engineer\nCase: One worker determines the step\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | step_time (milliseconds) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 90 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 120 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 121 | review |\n| case-4 | 220 | review |\n| case-5 | 95 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo workers exceed the step bound; synchronized training waits for slow participants.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCompare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in distributed-training engineer.\nBefore release: Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate compute, input and communication timing; preserve optimizer and random state on restart.\nAdding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRestartable training job: Report lost work, recovery time and numerical differences.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"541bca8d58ebab09c8578e4480d248d0f3a39047d0da7f2e47514b12b69d97b2","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"One worker determines the step","scenario":"The toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — One worker determines the step\nid,step_time\r\ncase-1,90\r\ncase-2,120\r\ncase-3,121\r\ncase-4,220\r\ncase-5,95\r\n\nCurrent review rule: step_time > 250\nRequired contract: Review when step_time > 120 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two workers exceed the step bound; synchronized training waits for slow participants.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two workers exceed the step bound; synchronized training waits for slow participants. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Ray","url":"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: One worker determines the step","scenario":"The toy training run flags worker step times above 120 ms under a fixed workload.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — One worker determines the step\nid,step_time\r\ncase-1,90\r\ncase-2,120\r\ncase-3,121\r\ncase-4,220\r\ncase-5,95\r\n\nCurrent review rule: step_time > 250\nRequired contract: Review when step_time > 120 (milliseconds). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Compare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism.","correct":true,"feedback":"Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 250 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Compare data loading and collective timing per worker before changing parallelism. Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Ray","url":"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Distributed-training engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Separate compute, input and communication timing; preserve optimizer and random state on restart.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Adding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 120 milliseconds as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Separate compute, input and communication timing; preserve optimizer and random state on restart. Adding workers can increase synchronization cost; report useful speedup under comparable training conditions. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Ray","url":"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your scaling efficiency and straggler report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the scaling efficiency and straggler report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Rerun identical data and batch settings and compare step distribution and convergence. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Ray","url":"https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/"}]}]},"training-data":{"revision":1,"title":"Clean rows with missing provenance","brief":"Every admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_provenance","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_provenance":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_provenance":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_provenance":0},{"id":"case-4","missing_provenance":2},{"id":"case-5","missing_provenance":3}],"policy":{"field":"missing_provenance","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"missing_provenance","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_provenance >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three records are structurally valid but lack required provenance fields.","repair":"Quarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review.","verify":"Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits.","deliverable":"Dataset contract and quarantine register","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and dataset contract and quarantine register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_provenance in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Quarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as dataset contract and quarantine register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Traceable corpus pipeline — Ingest a small licensed text dataset.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Dataset contract and quarantine register\n\nCase: Clean rows with missing provenance\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Dataset contract and quarantine register\nRole: AI data / training-data infrastructure engineer\nCase: Clean rows with missing provenance\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_provenance (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree records are structurally valid but lack required provenance fields.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nQuarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai data / training-data infrastructure engineer.\nBefore release: Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack immutable source manifests; use group-aware splits and reproducible sampling.\nStrict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTraceable corpus pipeline: A dataset version can be recreated from recorded inputs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI data / training-data infrastructure engineer — Clean rows with missing provenance\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nEvery admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_provenance >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and dataset contract and quarantine register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_provenance in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Quarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as dataset contract and quarantine register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Traceable corpus pipeline — Ingest a small licensed text dataset.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Apache Spark: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_provenance\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_provenance\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_provenance\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Dataset contract and quarantine register\n\nCase: Clean rows with missing provenance\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Dataset contract and quarantine register\nRole: AI data / training-data infrastructure engineer\nCase: Clean rows with missing provenance\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nEvery admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_provenance (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree records are structurally valid but lack required provenance fields.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nQuarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai data / training-data infrastructure engineer.\nBefore release: Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack immutable source manifests; use group-aware splits and reproducible sampling.\nStrict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nTraceable corpus pipeline: A dataset version can be recreated from recorded inputs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"c0e3b1ded4185e5e46f966dc4c1adfda3da6df08103dbd6fb41befe6fe6df783","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Clean rows with missing provenance","scenario":"Every admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Clean rows with missing provenance\nid,missing_provenance\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_provenance >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_provenance >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three records are structurally valid but lack required provenance fields.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three records are structurally valid but lack required provenance fields. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Clean rows with missing provenance","scenario":"Every admitted training record must have known source, allowed use and split-group identity.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Clean rows with missing provenance\nid,missing_provenance\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_provenance >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_provenance >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Quarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review.","correct":true,"feedback":"Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Quarantine unknown provenance before dataset assembly; retain source identifiers for review. Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI data / training-data infrastructure engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Strict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Track immutable source manifests; use group-aware splits and reproducible sampling.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Track immutable source manifests; use group-aware splits and reproducible sampling. Strict provenance gates reduce available data but make later deletion and contamination review possible. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your dataset contract and quarantine register is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the dataset contract and quarantine register with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Rebuild the manifest and prove disallowed records do not reach training or evaluation splits. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"ml-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"The classifier fails high-cost cases","brief":"A toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"false_negatives","unit":"out of ten positives","rows":[{"id":"case-1","false_negatives":0},{"id":"case-2","false_negatives":1},{"id":"case-3","false_negatives":2},{"id":"case-4","false_negatives":4},{"id":"case-5","false_negatives":0}],"policy":{"field":"false_negatives","operator":">=","threshold":5},"expected":{"field":"false_negatives","operator":">=","threshold":2},"rule":"Review when false_negatives >= 2 (out of ten positives). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two batches exceed the stated missed-positive tolerance; accuracy alone hid the failure cost.","repair":"Review threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases.","verify":"Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice.","deliverable":"Model evaluation and threshold decision report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and model evaluation and threshold decision report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_negatives in out of ten positives. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Review threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as model evaluation and threshold decision report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Raising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Churn prediction service — Use public or synthetic subscription data.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Model evaluation and threshold decision report\n\nCase: The classifier fails high-cost cases\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Model evaluation and threshold decision report\nRole: Machine learning engineer\nCase: The classifier fails high-cost cases\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_negatives (out of ten positives) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 4 | review |\n| case-5 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the stated missed-positive tolerance; accuracy alone hid the failure cost.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReview threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in machine learning engineer.\nBefore release: Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine the cost of errors before selecting metrics; separate training, validation and test decisions.\nRaising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nChurn prediction service: Compare calibration and recall against a simple baseline.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Machine learning engineer — The classifier fails high-cost cases\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nA toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.\n\n## Contract\nReview when false_negatives >= 2 (out of ten positives). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and model evaluation and threshold decision report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_negatives in out of ten positives. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Review threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as model evaluation and threshold decision report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Raising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCompare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Churn prediction service — Use public or synthetic subscription data.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google ML Crash Course: https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,false_negatives\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,4\r\ncase-5,0\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"false_negatives\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 5\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"false_negatives\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 2\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Model evaluation and threshold decision report\n\nCase: The classifier fails high-cost cases\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Model evaluation and threshold decision report\nRole: Machine learning engineer\nCase: The classifier fails high-cost cases\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_negatives (out of ten positives) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 4 | review |\n| case-5 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the stated missed-positive tolerance; accuracy alone hid the failure cost.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReview threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in machine learning engineer.\nBefore release: Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine the cost of errors before selecting metrics; separate training, validation and test decisions.\nRaising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nChurn prediction service: Compare calibration and recall against a simple baseline.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"4ddd898d03f6d76549f4e9c0e3b155a9b3f199570c34196b261485c5c7e2904e","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The classifier fails high-cost cases","scenario":"A toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The classifier fails high-cost cases\nid,false_negatives\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,4\r\ncase-5,0\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_negatives >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when false_negatives >= 2 (out of ten positives). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two batches exceed the stated missed-positive tolerance; accuracy alone hid the failure cost.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two batches exceed the stated missed-positive tolerance; accuracy alone hid the failure cost. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The classifier fails high-cost cases","scenario":"A toy risk-screening pilot escalates any batch with more than one missed positive in ten labeled positives.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The classifier fails high-cost cases\nid,false_negatives\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,4\r\ncase-5,0\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_negatives >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when false_negatives >= 2 (out of ten positives). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 5 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Review threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases.","correct":true,"feedback":"Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Review threshold and features on validation data, then evaluate once on untouched test cases. Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Machine learning engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 2 out of ten positives as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Define the cost of errors before selecting metrics; separate training, validation and test decisions.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Raising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Define the cost of errors before selecting metrics; separate training, validation and test decisions. Raising recall can increase false positives and review workload; make the operational cost visible. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your model evaluation and threshold decision report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the model evaluation and threshold decision report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Compare recall, false positives and calibration by meaningful slice. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"applied-scientist":{"revision":1,"title":"A gain smaller than run variability","brief":"The predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"seed_spread","unit":"percentage points","rows":[{"id":"case-1","seed_spread":1},{"id":"case-2","seed_spread":3},{"id":"case-3","seed_spread":4},{"id":"case-4","seed_spread":7},{"id":"case-5","seed_spread":2}],"policy":{"field":"seed_spread","operator":">","threshold":8},"expected":{"field":"seed_spread","operator":">","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when seed_spread > 3 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two experiments are too variable for the current single-run improvement claim.","repair":"Repeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain.","verify":"Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs.","deliverable":"Ablation table and scientific limitations note","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and ablation table and scientific limitations note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using seed_spread in percentage points. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 8 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Repeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as ablation table and scientific limitations note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Repeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Ranking-method ablation — Select a public relevance dataset.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Ablation table and scientific limitations note\n\nCase: A gain smaller than run variability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Ablation table and scientific limitations note\nRole: Applied scientist\nCase: A gain smaller than run variability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | seed_spread (percentage points) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 3 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 4 | review |\n| case-4 | 7 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo experiments are too variable for the current single-run improvement claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRepeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in applied scientist.\nBefore release: Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nPredeclare the baseline and primary metric; preserve negative and inconclusive experiments.\nRepeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRanking-method ablation: Report variation and failure cases, not only the best run.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Applied scientist — A gain smaller than run variability\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.\n\n## Contract\nReview when seed_spread > 3 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and ablation table and scientific limitations note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using seed_spread in percentage points. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 8 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Repeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as ablation table and scientific limitations note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Repeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nUse matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Ranking-method ablation — Select a public relevance dataset.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- Google Research careers: https://research.google/careers/\n","evidence.csv":"id,seed_spread\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,3\r\ncase-3,4\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"seed_spread\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 8\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"seed_spread\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Ablation table and scientific limitations note\n\nCase: A gain smaller than run variability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Ablation table and scientific limitations note\nRole: Applied scientist\nCase: A gain smaller than run variability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | seed_spread (percentage points) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 3 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 4 | review |\n| case-4 | 7 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo experiments are too variable for the current single-run improvement claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRepeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in applied scientist.\nBefore release: Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nPredeclare the baseline and primary metric; preserve negative and inconclusive experiments.\nRepeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRanking-method ablation: Report variation and failure cases, not only the best run.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"c909fb91a7d9850c09eb1011826b19e1b2713db3ecb7086fab267dd5b93e200d","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A gain smaller than run variability","scenario":"The predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A gain smaller than run variability\nid,seed_spread\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,3\r\ncase-3,4\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: seed_spread > 8\nRequired contract: Review when seed_spread > 3 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two experiments are too variable for the current single-run improvement claim.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Two experiments are too variable for the current single-run improvement claim. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A gain smaller than run variability","scenario":"The predeclared study gate flags run-to-run accuracy spreads above three percentage points for further analysis.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A gain smaller than run variability\nid,seed_spread\r\ncase-1,1\r\ncase-2,3\r\ncase-3,4\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: seed_spread > 8\nRequired contract: Review when seed_spread > 3 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Repeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain.","correct":true,"feedback":"Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 8 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Repeat controlled seeds and report uncertainty and effect sizes before claiming a gain. Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Applied scientist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 percentage points as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Predeclare the baseline and primary metric; preserve negative and inconclusive experiments.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Repeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Predeclare the baseline and primary metric; preserve negative and inconclusive experiments. Repeated runs consume compute but distinguish robust improvements from favorable random seeds. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your ablation table and scientific limitations note is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the ablation table and scientific limitations note with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Use matched budgets and untouched evaluation data; include all planned runs. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]}]},"data-scientist":{"revision":1,"title":"A forecast judged on familiar dates","brief":"The forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"future_rows","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","future_rows":0},{"id":"case-2","future_rows":1},{"id":"case-3","future_rows":0},{"id":"case-4","future_rows":5},{"id":"case-5","future_rows":2}],"policy":{"field":"future_rows","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"future_rows","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when future_rows >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three splits include future data and invalidate the out-of-time evaluation claim.","repair":"Rebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only.","verify":"Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline.","deliverable":"Rolling-validation notebook report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and rolling-validation notebook report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using future_rows in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Rebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as rolling-validation notebook report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Longer training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Demand forecasting report — Use public time-series data.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Rolling-validation notebook report\n\nCase: A forecast judged on familiar dates\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Rolling-validation notebook report\nRole: Data scientist / decision scientist\nCase: A forecast judged on familiar dates\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | future_rows (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree splits include future data and invalidate the out-of-time evaluation claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in data scientist / decision scientist.\nBefore release: Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAlign split boundaries with the decision time; preserve uncertainty in business recommendations.\nLonger training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDemand forecasting report: Publish forecast errors by horizon and actionable limitations.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Data scientist / decision scientist — A forecast judged on familiar dates\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.\n\n## Contract\nReview when future_rows >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and rolling-validation notebook report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using future_rows in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Rebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as rolling-validation notebook report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Longer training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRecompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Demand forecasting report — Use public time-series data.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google ML Crash Course: https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,future_rows\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"future_rows\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"future_rows\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Rolling-validation notebook report\n\nCase: A forecast judged on familiar dates\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Rolling-validation notebook report\nRole: Data scientist / decision scientist\nCase: A forecast judged on familiar dates\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | future_rows (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree splits include future data and invalidate the out-of-time evaluation claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in data scientist / decision scientist.\nBefore release: Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAlign split boundaries with the decision time; preserve uncertainty in business recommendations.\nLonger training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDemand forecasting report: Publish forecast errors by horizon and actionable limitations.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"daefe55b27cd1fb927ce96f476b46e35dd4ec4535ffe27dbc074fff2c3fe319f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A forecast judged on familiar dates","scenario":"The forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A forecast judged on familiar dates\nid,future_rows\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: future_rows >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when future_rows >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three splits include future data and invalidate the out-of-time evaluation claim.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three splits include future data and invalidate the out-of-time evaluation claim. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A forecast judged on familiar dates","scenario":"The forecasting protocol flags any training window containing observations from its future evaluation interval.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A forecast judged on familiar dates\nid,future_rows\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: future_rows >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when future_rows >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Rebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only.","correct":true,"feedback":"Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Rebuild rolling time splits and fit transformations on training windows only. Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Data scientist / decision scientist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Align split boundaries with the decision time; preserve uncertainty in business recommendations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Longer training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Align split boundaries with the decision time; preserve uncertainty in business recommendations. Longer training windows add data but may blur regime changes; evaluate on the periods that match deployment. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your rolling-validation notebook report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the rolling-validation notebook report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Recompute errors by horizon against a seasonal-naive baseline. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]}]},"recommendation":{"revision":1,"title":"The candidate stage loses the answer","brief":"The search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_relevant","unit":"out of ten","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_relevant":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_relevant":2},{"id":"case-3","missing_relevant":3},{"id":"case-4","missing_relevant":6},{"id":"case-5","missing_relevant":1}],"policy":{"field":"missing_relevant","operator":">=","threshold":7},"expected":{"field":"missing_relevant","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when missing_relevant >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two candidate sets fall below the stated recall requirement before reranking begins.","repair":"Improve candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker.","verify":"Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups.","deliverable":"Retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_relevant in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Improve candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Content recommender — Build a public article catalog.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis\n\nCase: The candidate stage loses the answer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis\nRole: Recommendation / ranking engineer\nCase: The candidate stage loses the answer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_relevant (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo candidate sets fall below the stated recall requirement before reranking begins.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nImprove candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in recommendation / ranking engineer.\nBefore release: Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate candidate recall, rank quality and exposure effects; log the stage that removed useful items.\nMore candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nContent recommender: Evaluate cold-start users without using future interactions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Recommendation / ranking engineer — The candidate stage loses the answer\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_relevant >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_relevant in out of ten. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Improve candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nMeasure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Content recommender — Build a public article catalog.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_relevant\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_relevant\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 7\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_relevant\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis\n\nCase: The candidate stage loses the answer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis\nRole: Recommendation / ranking engineer\nCase: The candidate stage loses the answer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_relevant (out of ten) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo candidate sets fall below the stated recall requirement before reranking begins.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nImprove candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in recommendation / ranking engineer.\nBefore release: Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate candidate recall, rank quality and exposure effects; log the stage that removed useful items.\nMore candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nContent recommender: Evaluate cold-start users without using future interactions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"e8a5a2964228497607fac34e9b01d1c7c2fb0ba8556ad53135b167c5bb2f31a3","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The candidate stage loses the answer","scenario":"The search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The candidate stage loses the answer\nid,missing_relevant\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_relevant >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when missing_relevant >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two candidate sets fall below the stated recall requirement before reranking begins.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Two candidate sets fall below the stated recall requirement before reranking begins. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The candidate stage loses the answer","scenario":"The search pilot flags candidate sets with fewer than eight of ten labeled relevant items. Use missing items as the signal.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The candidate stage loses the answer\nid,missing_relevant\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_relevant >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when missing_relevant >= 3 (out of ten). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Improve candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker.","correct":true,"feedback":"Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 7 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Improve candidate generation and evaluate it separately from the reranker. Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Recommendation / ranking engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate candidate recall, rank quality and exposure effects; log the stage that removed useful items.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Separate candidate recall, rank quality and exposure effects; log the stage that removed useful items. More candidates can improve recall while increasing reranking cost and latency. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the retrieval-versus-ranking error analysis with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Measure recall at fixed k on the same time-held-out user groups. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]}]},"computer-vision":{"revision":1,"title":"A model sees the same scene twice","brief":"The evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"shared_scenes","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","shared_scenes":0},{"id":"case-2","shared_scenes":1},{"id":"case-3","shared_scenes":3},{"id":"case-4","shared_scenes":0},{"id":"case-5","shared_scenes":2}],"policy":{"field":"shared_scenes","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"shared_scenes","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when shared_scenes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three reported evaluations leak scene identity across the split.","repair":"Split by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection.","verify":"Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms.","deliverable":"Scene-split audit and vision model card","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and scene-split audit and vision model card template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_scenes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Split by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as scene-split audit and vision model card. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Visual quality inspection — Create synthetic defect images.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scene-split audit and vision model card\n\nCase: A model sees the same scene twice\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Scene-split audit and vision model card\nRole: Computer-vision engineer\nCase: A model sees the same scene twice\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_scenes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree reported evaluations leak scene identity across the split.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nSplit by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in computer-vision engineer.\nBefore release: Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep correlated images grouped; evaluate deployment-relevant capture conditions.\nA harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nVisual quality inspection: Report missed defects and false alarms by defect type.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Computer-vision engineer — A model sees the same scene twice\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.\n\n## Contract\nReview when shared_scenes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and scene-split audit and vision model card template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_scenes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Split by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as scene-split audit and vision model card. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nEvaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Visual quality inspection — Create synthetic defect images.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Computer Vision Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- ONNX Runtime: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/\n","evidence.csv":"id,shared_scenes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"shared_scenes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"shared_scenes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scene-split audit and vision model card\n\nCase: A model sees the same scene twice\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Scene-split audit and vision model card\nRole: Computer-vision engineer\nCase: A model sees the same scene twice\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_scenes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree reported evaluations leak scene identity across the split.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nSplit by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in computer-vision engineer.\nBefore release: Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep correlated images grouped; evaluate deployment-relevant capture conditions.\nA harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nVisual quality inspection: Report missed defects and false alarms by defect type.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"c6bdd0d5c3f9aa2a0e80794643565212fd6c88415b82b4497875c7b3a2687264","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A model sees the same scene twice","scenario":"The evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A model sees the same scene twice\nid,shared_scenes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_scenes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_scenes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three reported evaluations leak scene identity across the split.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three reported evaluations leak scene identity across the split. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A model sees the same scene twice","scenario":"The evaluation protocol forbids any scene identity from appearing in both training and test data.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A model sees the same scene twice\nid,shared_scenes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_scenes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_scenes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Split by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Split by scene or object group before augmentation and model selection. Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Computer-vision engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Keep correlated images grouped; evaluate deployment-relevant capture conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Keep correlated images grouped; evaluate deployment-relevant capture conditions. A harder grouped split may lower scores but better reflects generalization to new scenes. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your scene-split audit and vision model card is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the scene-split audit and vision model card with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Evaluate unseen cameras and lighting conditions; report per-class misses and false alarms. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]}]},"speech-ml":{"revision":1,"title":"A transcript metric hides number errors","brief":"The voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"wrong_digits","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","wrong_digits":0},{"id":"case-2","wrong_digits":1},{"id":"case-3","wrong_digits":0},{"id":"case-4","wrong_digits":2},{"id":"case-5","wrong_digits":3}],"policy":{"field":"wrong_digits","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"wrong_digits","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when wrong_digits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three utterances fail the critical-field check despite plausible overall transcription.","repair":"Report critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number.","verify":"Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows.","deliverable":"Audio evaluation worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and audio evaluation worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using wrong_digits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Report critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as audio evaluation worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Confirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Accent robustness study — Select licensed multi-speaker clips.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Audio evaluation worksheet\n\nCase: A transcript metric hides number errors\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Audio evaluation worksheet\nRole: Speech / audio ML engineer\nCase: A transcript metric hides number errors\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | wrong_digits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree utterances fail the critical-field check despite plausible overall transcription.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReport critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in speech / audio ml engineer.\nBefore release: Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate linguistic transcription quality from task-critical fields; preserve speaker-disjoint evaluation.\nConfirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAccent robustness study: Explain sample sizes and avoid population-wide claims.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Speech / audio ML engineer — A transcript metric hides number errors\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.\n\n## Contract\nReview when wrong_digits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and audio evaluation worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using wrong_digits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Report critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as audio evaluation worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Confirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nEvaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Accent robustness study — Select licensed multi-speaker clips.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- ONNX Runtime: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/\n","evidence.csv":"id,wrong_digits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"wrong_digits\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"wrong_digits\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Audio evaluation worksheet\n\nCase: A transcript metric hides number errors\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Audio evaluation worksheet\nRole: Speech / audio ML engineer\nCase: A transcript metric hides number errors\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | wrong_digits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree utterances fail the critical-field check despite plausible overall transcription.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReport critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in speech / audio ml engineer.\nBefore release: Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate linguistic transcription quality from task-critical fields; preserve speaker-disjoint evaluation.\nConfirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAccent robustness study: Explain sample sizes and avoid population-wide claims.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"1ceb063acf8a653449dc106f60357da98b5d506b41e2c4cff3932e6885dcfbd0","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A transcript metric hides number errors","scenario":"The voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A transcript metric hides number errors\nid,wrong_digits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: wrong_digits >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when wrong_digits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three utterances fail the critical-field check despite plausible overall transcription.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three utterances fail the critical-field check despite plausible overall transcription. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A transcript metric hides number errors","scenario":"The voice workflow treats any wrong account digit in a reviewed utterance as a critical error.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A transcript metric hides number errors\nid,wrong_digits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: wrong_digits >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when wrong_digits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Report critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Report critical-field accuracy separately and require confirmation before using an account number. Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Speech / audio ML engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate linguistic transcription quality from task-critical fields; preserve speaker-disjoint evaluation.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Confirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Separate linguistic transcription quality from task-critical fields; preserve speaker-disjoint evaluation. Confirmation adds conversational turns but reduces the cost of silently accepting a wrong identifier. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your audio evaluation worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the audio evaluation worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Evaluate unseen speakers, noise conditions and corrected read-back flows. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"}]}]},"post-training":{"revision":1,"title":"The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks","brief":"The adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"regression_drop","unit":"percentage points","rows":[{"id":"case-1","regression_drop":0},{"id":"case-2","regression_drop":2},{"id":"case-3","regression_drop":3},{"id":"case-4","regression_drop":8},{"id":"case-5","regression_drop":1}],"policy":{"field":"regression_drop","operator":">","threshold":9},"expected":{"field":"regression_drop","operator":">","threshold":2},"rule":"Review when regression_drop > 2 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two protected tasks regress beyond the declared allowance after tuning.","repair":"Hold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline.","verify":"Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations.","deliverable":"Adaptation decision and regression report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and adaptation decision and regression report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using regression_drop in percentage points. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 9 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Hold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as adaptation decision and regression report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Specialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Domain style adapter — Use rights-cleared synthetic support examples.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Adaptation decision and regression report\n\nCase: The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Adaptation decision and regression report\nRole: LLM customization / post-training engineer\nCase: The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | regression_drop (percentage points) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 8 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo protected tasks regress beyond the declared allowance after tuning.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nHold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in llm customization / post-training engineer.\nBefore release: Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCompare tuning against retrieval and prompting; preserve the base model and rollback path.\nSpecialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDomain style adapter: Blind review finds gains without hidden evaluation overlap.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# LLM customization / post-training engineer — The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.\n\n## Contract\nReview when regression_drop > 2 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and adaptation decision and regression report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using regression_drop in percentage points. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 9 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Hold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as adaptation decision and regression report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Specialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nUse separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Domain style adapter — Use rights-cleared synthetic support examples.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,regression_drop\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,8\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"regression_drop\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 9\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"regression_drop\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 2\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Adaptation decision and regression report\n\nCase: The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Adaptation decision and regression report\nRole: LLM customization / post-training engineer\nCase: The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | regression_drop (percentage points) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 8 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo protected tasks regress beyond the declared allowance after tuning.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nHold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in llm customization / post-training engineer.\nBefore release: Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCompare tuning against retrieval and prompting; preserve the base model and rollback path.\nSpecialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDomain style adapter: Blind review finds gains without hidden evaluation overlap.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"abd397190339d5a48afbf96d6691719d3cf3133c94e2fb5f47fc8ef31b86ae35","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks","scenario":"The adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\nid,regression_drop\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,8\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: regression_drop > 9\nRequired contract: Review when regression_drop > 2 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two protected tasks regress beyond the declared allowance after tuning.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two protected tasks regress beyond the declared allowance after tuning. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks","scenario":"The adaptation gate flags a capability drop greater than two points on protected regression tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The tuned model forgets adjacent tasks\nid,regression_drop\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,8\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: regression_drop > 9\nRequired contract: Review when regression_drop > 2 (percentage points). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Hold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline.","correct":true,"feedback":"Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 9 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Hold promotion, inspect the tuning data mix and compare with a strong prompt baseline. Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of LLM customization / post-training engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Compare tuning against retrieval and prompting; preserve the base model and rollback path.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Specialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 2 percentage points as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Compare tuning against retrieval and prompting; preserve the base model and rollback path. Specialization can improve target behavior while degrading other capabilities; make that trade-off explicit. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your adaptation decision and regression report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the adaptation decision and regression report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Use separate adaptation and general-capability tests, with provenance and repeated evaluations. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"research-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"Reproduction without the data version","brief":"The reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_run_fields","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_run_fields":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_run_fields":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_run_fields":2},{"id":"case-4","missing_run_fields":0},{"id":"case-5","missing_run_fields":3}],"policy":{"field":"missing_run_fields","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"missing_run_fields","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_run_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three runs cannot be independently reconstructed from their recorded metadata.","repair":"Capture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments.","verify":"Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance.","deliverable":"Reproducibility manifest and deviation log","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and reproducibility manifest and deviation log template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_run_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Capture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as reproducibility manifest and deviation log. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Small-paper reproduction — Choose a tractable public method.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reproducibility manifest and deviation log\n\nCase: Reproduction without the data version\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Reproducibility manifest and deviation log\nRole: AI research engineer\nCase: Reproduction without the data version\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_run_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree runs cannot be independently reconstructed from their recorded metadata.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCapture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai research engineer.\nBefore release: Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAutomate configuration validation and artifact capture; preserve failed runs for diagnosis.\nStrict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSmall-paper reproduction: Document deviations from published settings.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI research engineer — Reproduction without the data version\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_run_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and reproducibility manifest and deviation log template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_run_fields in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Capture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as reproducibility manifest and deviation log. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRecreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Small-paper reproduction — Choose a tractable public method.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- Google Research careers: https://research.google/careers/\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_run_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_run_fields\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_run_fields\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reproducibility manifest and deviation log\n\nCase: Reproduction without the data version\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Reproducibility manifest and deviation log\nRole: AI research engineer\nCase: Reproduction without the data version\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_run_fields (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree runs cannot be independently reconstructed from their recorded metadata.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCapture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai research engineer.\nBefore release: Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nAutomate configuration validation and artifact capture; preserve failed runs for diagnosis.\nStrict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSmall-paper reproduction: Document deviations from published settings.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"1820cc27fa7fb697999127d56b1b07d1e2dd36262c2a0a02ff4261b2285509d0","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Reproduction without the data version","scenario":"The reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Reproduction without the data version\nid,missing_run_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_run_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_run_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three runs cannot be independently reconstructed from their recorded metadata.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three runs cannot be independently reconstructed from their recorded metadata. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Reproduction without the data version","scenario":"The reproduction contract requires code commit, dataset version and configuration hash for each run.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Reproduction without the data version\nid,missing_run_fields\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_run_fields >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_run_fields >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Capture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments.","correct":true,"feedback":"Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Capture immutable input and configuration references before launching experiments. Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI research engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Strict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Automate configuration validation and artifact capture; preserve failed runs for diagnosis.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Automate configuration validation and artifact capture; preserve failed runs for diagnosis. Strict reproducibility has storage and setup costs but reduces ambiguous research conclusions. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your reproducibility manifest and deviation log is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the reproducibility manifest and deviation log with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Recreate one run from a fresh environment and explain numerical tolerance. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"}]}]},"research-scientist":{"revision":1,"title":"Only winning seeds are in the paper","brief":"The study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"omitted_seeds","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","omitted_seeds":0},{"id":"case-2","omitted_seeds":1},{"id":"case-3","omitted_seeds":0},{"id":"case-4","omitted_seeds":3},{"id":"case-5","omitted_seeds":2}],"policy":{"field":"omitted_seeds","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"omitted_seeds","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when omitted_seeds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three result summaries omit planned trials, so the reported evidence is selectively presented.","repair":"Restore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results.","verify":"Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan.","deliverable":"Reproducible research note and claim-evidence table","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and reproducible research note and claim-evidence table template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using omitted_seeds in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Restore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as reproducible research note and claim-evidence table. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Focused research proposal — Survey one narrow question.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reproducible research note and claim-evidence table\n\nCase: Only winning seeds are in the paper\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Reproducible research note and claim-evidence table\nRole: AI research scientist\nCase: Only winning seeds are in the paper\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | omitted_seeds (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree result summaries omit planned trials, so the reported evidence is selectively presented.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRestore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai research scientist.\nBefore release: Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMake hypotheses falsifiable and comparisons fair; separate exploratory results from confirmatory claims.\nA narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFocused research proposal: Proposal explains novelty and a falsification criterion.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI research scientist — Only winning seeds are in the paper\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.\n\n## Contract\nReview when omitted_seeds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and reproducible research note and claim-evidence table template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using omitted_seeds in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Restore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as reproducible research note and claim-evidence table. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReport effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Focused research proposal — Survey one narrow question.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google Research careers: https://research.google/careers/\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,omitted_seeds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"omitted_seeds\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"omitted_seeds\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Reproducible research note and claim-evidence table\n\nCase: Only winning seeds are in the paper\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Reproducible research note and claim-evidence table\nRole: AI research scientist\nCase: Only winning seeds are in the paper\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | omitted_seeds (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree result summaries omit planned trials, so the reported evidence is selectively presented.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRestore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai research scientist.\nBefore release: Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMake hypotheses falsifiable and comparisons fair; separate exploratory results from confirmatory claims.\nA narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFocused research proposal: Proposal explains novelty and a falsification criterion.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"67ab41cf0aff2284a541e21d75240f16c72e57f392d5c8f7c3ad03bcc8d0d4c7","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Only winning seeds are in the paper","scenario":"The study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Only winning seeds are in the paper\nid,omitted_seeds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: omitted_seeds >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when omitted_seeds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three result summaries omit planned trials, so the reported evidence is selectively presented.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three result summaries omit planned trials, so the reported evidence is selectively presented. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Only winning seeds are in the paper","scenario":"The study plan requires reporting every planned seed, including failed or unfavorable outcomes.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Only winning seeds are in the paper\nid,omitted_seeds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: omitted_seeds >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when omitted_seeds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Restore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results.","correct":true,"feedback":"Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Restore the full trial ledger and revise the claim to match the complete results. Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI research scientist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Make hypotheses falsifiable and comparisons fair; separate exploratory results from confirmatory claims.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Make hypotheses falsifiable and comparisons fair; separate exploratory results from confirmatory claims. A narrower, defensible claim is more valuable than an impressive result dependent on selective reporting. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your reproducible research note and claim-evidence table is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the reproducible research note and claim-evidence table with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Report effect sizes, variability, negative results and deviations from the original plan. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google Research careers","url":"https://research.google/careers/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"kernel-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"The fast kernel changes the answer","brief":"The numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"absolute_error","unit":"absolute units","rows":[{"id":"case-1","absolute_error":0.0001},{"id":"case-2","absolute_error":0.001},{"id":"case-3","absolute_error":0.002},{"id":"case-4","absolute_error":0.01},{"id":"case-5","absolute_error":0.0005}],"policy":{"field":"absolute_error","operator":">","threshold":0.02},"expected":{"field":"absolute_error","operator":">","threshold":0.001},"rule":"Review when absolute_error > 0.001 (absolute units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two optimized cases violate the declared numerical tolerance; speed alone cannot justify the kernel.","repair":"Inspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again.","verify":"Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests.","deliverable":"Numerical correctness and performance matrix","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and numerical correctness and performance matrix template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using absolute_error in absolute units. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 0.02 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as numerical correctness and performance matrix. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Reduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Fused tensor operator — Implement a reference operation.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Numerical correctness and performance matrix\n\nCase: The fast kernel changes the answer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Numerical correctness and performance matrix\nRole: ML compiler / GPU kernel engineer\nCase: The fast kernel changes the answer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | absolute_error (absolute units) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0.0001 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 0.001 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 0.002 | review |\n| case-4 | 0.01 | review |\n| case-5 | 0.0005 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo optimized cases violate the declared numerical tolerance; speed alone cannot justify the kernel.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ml compiler / gpu kernel engineer.\nBefore release: Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate semantics before optimization; benchmark warmup and synchronization consistently.\nReduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFused tensor operator: Report regressions and hardware/software versions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# ML compiler / GPU kernel engineer — The fast kernel changes the answer\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.\n\n## Contract\nReview when absolute_error > 0.001 (absolute units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and numerical correctness and performance matrix template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using absolute_error in absolute units. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 0.02 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as numerical correctness and performance matrix. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Reduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRun correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Fused tensor operator — Implement a reference operation.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html\n- Triton language: https://triton-lang.org/main/index.html\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,absolute_error\r\ncase-1,0.0001\r\ncase-2,0.001\r\ncase-3,0.002\r\ncase-4,0.01\r\ncase-5,0.0005\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"absolute_error\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 0.02\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"absolute_error\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 0.001\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Numerical correctness and performance matrix\n\nCase: The fast kernel changes the answer\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Numerical correctness and performance matrix\nRole: ML compiler / GPU kernel engineer\nCase: The fast kernel changes the answer\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | absolute_error (absolute units) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0.0001 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 0.001 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 0.002 | review |\n| case-4 | 0.01 | review |\n| case-5 | 0.0005 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo optimized cases violate the declared numerical tolerance; speed alone cannot justify the kernel.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ml compiler / gpu kernel engineer.\nBefore release: Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate semantics before optimization; benchmark warmup and synchronization consistently.\nReduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFused tensor operator: Report regressions and hardware/software versions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"ec1c0be63acf7b270801bec020096a272b6519c9299b652c91d1bae34e01135d","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The fast kernel changes the answer","scenario":"The numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The fast kernel changes the answer\nid,absolute_error\r\ncase-1,0.0001\r\ncase-2,0.001\r\ncase-3,0.002\r\ncase-4,0.01\r\ncase-5,0.0005\r\n\nCurrent review rule: absolute_error > 0.02\nRequired contract: Review when absolute_error > 0.001 (absolute units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two optimized cases violate the declared numerical tolerance; speed alone cannot justify the kernel.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Two optimized cases violate the declared numerical tolerance; speed alone cannot justify the kernel. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Triton language","url":"https://triton-lang.org/main/index.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The fast kernel changes the answer","scenario":"The numerical contract flags absolute error above 0.001 against the reference implementation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The fast kernel changes the answer\nid,absolute_error\r\ncase-1,0.0001\r\ncase-2,0.001\r\ncase-3,0.002\r\ncase-4,0.01\r\ncase-5,0.0005\r\n\nCurrent review rule: absolute_error > 0.02\nRequired contract: Review when absolute_error > 0.001 (absolute units). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Inspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again.","correct":true,"feedback":"Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 0.02 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Inspect accumulation precision and edge shapes before benchmarking the candidate again. Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Triton language","url":"https://triton-lang.org/main/index.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of ML compiler / GPU kernel engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 0.001 absolute units as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Validate semantics before optimization; benchmark warmup and synchronization consistently.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Reduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Validate semantics before optimization; benchmark warmup and synchronization consistently. Reduced precision may improve throughput but must respect task-specific error tolerance. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Triton language","url":"https://triton-lang.org/main/index.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your numerical correctness and performance matrix is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the numerical correctness and performance matrix with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Run correctness across irregular shapes and extreme values before synchronized timing tests. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"},{"title":"Triton language","url":"https://triton-lang.org/main/index.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]}]},"evals-engineer":{"revision":1,"title":"A grader accepts plausible mistakes","brief":"The calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"false_passes","unit":"out of ten incorrect answers","rows":[{"id":"case-1","false_passes":0},{"id":"case-2","false_passes":1},{"id":"case-3","false_passes":2},{"id":"case-4","false_passes":5},{"id":"case-5","false_passes":1}],"policy":{"field":"false_passes","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"false_passes","operator":">=","threshold":2},"rule":"Review when false_passes >= 2 (out of ten incorrect answers). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two grader configurations accept too many known errors to support the release claim.","repair":"Calibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording.","verify":"Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set.","deliverable":"Grader calibration report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and grader calibration report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_passes in out of ten incorrect answers. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Calibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as grader calibration report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Answer-quality harness — Write 40 cases across clear and ambiguous tasks.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Grader calibration report\n\nCase: A grader accepts plausible mistakes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Grader calibration report\nRole: AI evaluation / evals engineer\nCase: A grader accepts plausible mistakes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_passes (out of ten incorrect answers) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo grader configurations accept too many known errors to support the release claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCalibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai evaluation / evals engineer.\nBefore release: Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep grader evaluation separate from model evaluation; preserve rubric versions and human adjudication.\nA stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAnswer-quality harness: Report grader error alongside model quality.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI evaluation / evals engineer — A grader accepts plausible mistakes\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.\n\n## Contract\nReview when false_passes >= 2 (out of ten incorrect answers). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and grader calibration report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_passes in out of ten incorrect answers. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Calibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as grader calibration report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nMeasure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Answer-quality harness — Write 40 cases across clear and ambiguous tasks.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,false_passes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"false_passes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"false_passes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 2\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Grader calibration report\n\nCase: A grader accepts plausible mistakes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Grader calibration report\nRole: AI evaluation / evals engineer\nCase: A grader accepts plausible mistakes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_passes (out of ten incorrect answers) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo grader configurations accept too many known errors to support the release claim.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nCalibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai evaluation / evals engineer.\nBefore release: Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep grader evaluation separate from model evaluation; preserve rubric versions and human adjudication.\nA stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAnswer-quality harness: Report grader error alongside model quality.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"1a62c86c6ca919f98ad637ed59139ffaf6ed08d6e651ddbc11a4b962f7060167","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A grader accepts plausible mistakes","scenario":"The calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A grader accepts plausible mistakes\nid,false_passes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_passes >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when false_passes >= 2 (out of ten incorrect answers). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two grader configurations accept too many known errors to support the release claim.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two grader configurations accept too many known errors to support the release claim. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A grader accepts plausible mistakes","scenario":"The calibration gate flags more than one false pass in ten deliberately incorrect answers.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A grader accepts plausible mistakes\nid,false_passes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_passes >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when false_passes >= 2 (out of ten incorrect answers). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Calibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording.","correct":true,"feedback":"Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Calibrate against independently labeled examples and repair ambiguous rubric wording. Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI evaluation / evals engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Keep grader evaluation separate from model evaluation; preserve rubric versions and human adjudication.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 2 out of ten incorrect answers as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Keep grader evaluation separate from model evaluation; preserve rubric versions and human adjudication. A stricter grader can reject useful answers; calibrate both sides rather than maximizing agreement with itself. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your grader calibration report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the grader calibration report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Measure false passes and false failures separately on a new calibration set. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"agent-benchmarks":{"revision":1,"title":"The verifier believes the agent","brief":"The benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"false_successes","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","false_successes":0},{"id":"case-2","false_successes":1},{"id":"case-3","false_successes":2},{"id":"case-4","false_successes":0},{"id":"case-5","false_successes":3}],"policy":{"field":"false_successes","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"false_successes","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when false_successes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three verifiers accept a success message even when the required final state is absent.","repair":"Validate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace.","verify":"Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification.","deliverable":"Agent benchmark integrity report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and agent benchmark integrity report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_successes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Validate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as agent benchmark integrity report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Agent task arena — Create five disposable repository tasks.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Agent benchmark integrity report\n\nCase: The verifier believes the agent\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Agent benchmark integrity report\nRole: Agent evaluation / benchmark engineer\nCase: The verifier believes the agent\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_successes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree verifiers accept a success message even when the required final state is absent.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nValidate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent evaluation / benchmark engineer.\nBefore release: Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate evaluated actions from scoring authority; record trajectories and independent outcome checks.\nA verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAgent task arena: Scores reflect final behavior and include failed or timed-out runs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Agent evaluation / benchmark engineer — The verifier believes the agent\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.\n\n## Contract\nReview when false_successes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and agent benchmark integrity report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_successes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Validate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as agent benchmark integrity report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReplay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Agent task arena — Create five disposable repository tasks.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n","evidence.csv":"id,false_successes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"false_successes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"false_successes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Agent benchmark integrity report\n\nCase: The verifier believes the agent\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Agent benchmark integrity report\nRole: Agent evaluation / benchmark engineer\nCase: The verifier believes the agent\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_successes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree verifiers accept a success message even when the required final state is absent.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nValidate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent evaluation / benchmark engineer.\nBefore release: Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate evaluated actions from scoring authority; record trajectories and independent outcome checks.\nA verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAgent task arena: Scores reflect final behavior and include failed or timed-out runs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"1e62fd548a0c19f6e70cfadd2c46ef0922f6927100e195fc6254d1eaa5381fa6","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The verifier believes the agent","scenario":"The benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The verifier believes the agent\nid,false_successes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_successes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when false_successes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three verifiers accept a success message even when the required final state is absent.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three verifiers accept a success message even when the required final state is absent. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The verifier believes the agent","scenario":"The benchmark requires zero false successes when final task state is deliberately wrong.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The verifier believes the agent\nid,false_successes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_successes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when false_successes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Validate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace.","correct":true,"feedback":"Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Validate final state using independent checks outside the editable agent workspace. Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Agent evaluation / benchmark engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate evaluated actions from scoring authority; record trajectories and independent outcome checks.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Separate evaluated actions from scoring authority; record trajectories and independent outcome checks. A verifier must allow different valid solutions without trusting self-reported completion. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your agent benchmark integrity report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the agent benchmark integrity report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Replay genuine success, fake success text and attempted verifier modification. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"}]}]},"ai-security":{"revision":1,"title":"Injected context gains write access","brief":"The sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unauthorized_writes","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unauthorized_writes":0},{"id":"case-2","unauthorized_writes":1},{"id":"case-3","unauthorized_writes":0},{"id":"case-4","unauthorized_writes":2},{"id":"case-5","unauthorized_writes":3}],"policy":{"field":"unauthorized_writes","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unauthorized_writes","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unauthorized_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three isolated trials show that content can bypass the intended action boundary.","repair":"Enforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution.","verify":"Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects.","deliverable":"Scoped security finding and retest record","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and scoped security finding and retest record template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unauthorized_writes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as scoped security finding and retest record. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Filtering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Prompt-injection test bench — Seed hostile retrieved text.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scoped security finding and retest record\n\nCase: Injected context gains write access\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Scoped security finding and retest record\nRole: AI security / red-team engineer\nCase: Injected context gains write access\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unauthorized_writes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree isolated trials show that content can bypass the intended action boundary.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai security / red-team engineer.\nBefore release: Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTreat retrieved instructions as untrusted data; minimize tool privileges and isolate testing.\nFiltering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPrompt-injection test bench: Show before/after outcomes with no real secret exposure.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI security / red-team engineer — Injected context gains write access\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unauthorized_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and scoped security finding and retest record template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unauthorized_writes in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Enforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as scoped security finding and retest record. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Filtering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nAuthorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Prompt-injection test bench — Seed hostile retrieved text.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project: https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\n- Open Policy Agent: https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,unauthorized_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unauthorized_writes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unauthorized_writes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Scoped security finding and retest record\n\nCase: Injected context gains write access\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Scoped security finding and retest record\nRole: AI security / red-team engineer\nCase: Injected context gains write access\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unauthorized_writes (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree isolated trials show that content can bypass the intended action boundary.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEnforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai security / red-team engineer.\nBefore release: Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTreat retrieved instructions as untrusted data; minimize tool privileges and isolate testing.\nFiltering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPrompt-injection test bench: Show before/after outcomes with no real secret exposure.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"90f222109cc77d6bb4dde9d66faf4621151a3de1924ac806f4101aa1fe107b49","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Injected context gains write access","scenario":"The sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Injected context gains write access\nid,unauthorized_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unauthorized_writes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unauthorized_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three isolated trials show that content can bypass the intended action boundary.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three isolated trials show that content can bypass the intended action boundary. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Injected context gains write access","scenario":"The sandbox policy allows no writes authorized only by retrieved or generated text.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Injected context gains write access\nid,unauthorized_writes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unauthorized_writes >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unauthorized_writes >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Enforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution.","correct":true,"feedback":"Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Enforce user, resource and action authorization in deterministic code before tool execution. Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI security / red-team engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Treat retrieved instructions as untrusted data; minimize tool privileges and isolate testing.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Filtering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Treat retrieved instructions as untrusted data; minimize tool privileges and isolate testing. Filtering suspicious text can help but cannot replace an independent authorization boundary. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your scoped security finding and retest record is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the scoped security finding and retest record with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Authorized actions pass; injected and replayed unauthorized actions cause no side effects. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"ai-safety":{"revision":1,"title":"The safeguard blocks legitimate requests","brief":"The trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"false_blocks","unit":"out of ten permitted tasks","rows":[{"id":"case-1","false_blocks":0},{"id":"case-2","false_blocks":2},{"id":"case-3","false_blocks":3},{"id":"case-4","false_blocks":7},{"id":"case-5","false_blocks":1}],"policy":{"field":"false_blocks","operator":">=","threshold":8},"expected":{"field":"false_blocks","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when false_blocks >= 3 (out of ten permitted tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two configurations overblock legitimate tasks beyond the agreed pilot tolerance.","repair":"Inspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples.","verify":"Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks.","deliverable":"Safeguard calibration and escalation plan","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and safeguard calibration and escalation plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_blocks in out of ten permitted tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 8 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as safeguard calibration and escalation plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Safeguard evaluation pack — Define safe synthetic policy cases.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Safeguard calibration and escalation plan\n\nCase: The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Safeguard calibration and escalation plan\nRole: AI safety / safeguards engineer\nCase: The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_blocks (out of ten permitted tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 7 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo configurations overblock legitimate tasks beyond the agreed pilot tolerance.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai safety / safeguards engineer.\nBefore release: Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine allowed and prohibited behavior together; keep a review path for ambiguous cases.\nMore aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSafeguard evaluation pack: Report false positives by legitimate task type.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI safety / safeguards engineer — The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.\n\n## Contract\nReview when false_blocks >= 3 (out of ten permitted tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and safeguard calibration and escalation plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using false_blocks in out of ten permitted tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 8 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Inspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as safeguard calibration and escalation plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nMeasure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Safeguard evaluation pack — Define safe synthetic policy cases.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project: https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,false_blocks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"false_blocks\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 8\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"false_blocks\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Safeguard calibration and escalation plan\n\nCase: The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Safeguard calibration and escalation plan\nRole: AI safety / safeguards engineer\nCase: The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | false_blocks (out of ten permitted tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 7 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo configurations overblock legitimate tasks beyond the agreed pilot tolerance.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nInspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai safety / safeguards engineer.\nBefore release: Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine allowed and prohibited behavior together; keep a review path for ambiguous cases.\nMore aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSafeguard evaluation pack: Report false positives by legitimate task type.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"d9a11baa8f235265c677ce725c8e10bbe0b04ac4e2ed2bb7e3cd3c9699208f71","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The safeguard blocks legitimate requests","scenario":"The trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\nid,false_blocks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_blocks >= 8\nRequired contract: Review when false_blocks >= 3 (out of ten permitted tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two configurations overblock legitimate tasks beyond the agreed pilot tolerance.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Two configurations overblock legitimate tasks beyond the agreed pilot tolerance. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The safeguard blocks legitimate requests","scenario":"The trial gate flags more than two false blocks in ten clearly permitted tasks.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The safeguard blocks legitimate requests\nid,false_blocks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,7\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: false_blocks >= 8\nRequired contract: Review when false_blocks >= 3 (out of ten permitted tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Inspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples.","correct":true,"feedback":"Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 8 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Inspect policy interpretation and calibrate thresholds with allowed neighboring examples. Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI safety / safeguards engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten permitted tasks as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Define allowed and prohibited behavior together; keep a review path for ambiguous cases.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Define allowed and prohibited behavior together; keep a review path for ambiguous cases. More aggressive blocking can reduce some harms while excluding legitimate users; report the trade-off. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your safeguard calibration and escalation plan is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the safeguard calibration and escalation plan with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Measure both missed violations and false blocks across relevant languages and tasks. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"ai-governance":{"revision":1,"title":"The inventory says approved, evidence is missing","brief":"A fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_controls","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_controls":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_controls":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_controls":2},{"id":"case-4","missing_controls":0},{"id":"case-5","missing_controls":3}],"policy":{"field":"missing_controls","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"missing_controls","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_controls >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three pilot records are marked ready despite missing required governance evidence.","repair":"Change their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts.","verify":"Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision.","deliverable":"Governance assessment memo and risk register","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and governance assessment memo and risk register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_controls in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Change their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as governance assessment memo and risk register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI inventory and intake kit — Define inventory fields.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Governance assessment memo and risk register\n\nCase: The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Governance assessment memo and risk register\nRole: AI governance / responsible AI specialist\nCase: The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_controls (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree pilot records are marked ready despite missing required governance evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nChange their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai governance / responsible ai specialist.\nBefore release: Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate vendor assertions from verified controls; record accountable owners and conditional approvals.\nA lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI inventory and intake kit: Each use case has an accountable owner and review path.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI governance / responsible AI specialist — The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nA fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_controls >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and governance assessment memo and risk register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_controls in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Change their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as governance assessment memo and risk register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReview the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI inventory and intake kit — Define inventory fields.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics: https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_controls\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_controls\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_controls\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Governance assessment memo and risk register\n\nCase: The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Governance assessment memo and risk register\nRole: AI governance / responsible AI specialist\nCase: The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nA fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_controls (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree pilot records are marked ready despite missing required governance evidence.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nChange their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai governance / responsible ai specialist.\nBefore release: Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate vendor assertions from verified controls; record accountable owners and conditional approvals.\nA lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI inventory and intake kit: Each use case has an accountable owner and review path.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"5f0eaf8f6d90b053290830e6b285607ab592923a39a15e3255ce7e2d60ca080f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The inventory says approved, evidence is missing","scenario":"A fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\nid,missing_controls\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_controls >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_controls >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three pilot records are marked ready despite missing required governance evidence.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three pilot records are marked ready despite missing required governance evidence. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The inventory says approved, evidence is missing","scenario":"A fictional organization requires an owner, a data-use review and an incident contact before approving an AI pilot.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The inventory says approved, evidence is missing\nid,missing_controls\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_controls >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_controls >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Change their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts.","correct":true,"feedback":"Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Change their status to pending review, assign owners and request the missing artifacts. Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI governance / responsible AI specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Separate vendor assertions from verified controls; record accountable owners and conditional approvals.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Separate vendor assertions from verified controls; record accountable owners and conditional approvals. A lightweight pilot process can be proportionate, but unresolved high-impact controls must remain visible. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your governance assessment memo and risk register is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the governance assessment memo and risk register with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Review the evidence register, approval history and the conditions for reopening a decision. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]}]},"ai-assurance":{"revision":1,"title":"The validator relies on the builder’s examples","brief":"The independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unsupported_claims","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unsupported_claims":0},{"id":"case-2","unsupported_claims":1},{"id":"case-3","unsupported_claims":3},{"id":"case-4","unsupported_claims":0},{"id":"case-5","unsupported_claims":2}],"policy":{"field":"unsupported_claims","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unsupported_claims","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unsupported_claims >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three submissions contain claims without independent evidence, so validation cannot close.","repair":"Map each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence.","verify":"Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner.","deliverable":"Assurance findings report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and assurance findings report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_claims in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Map each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as assurance findings report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Additional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Independent model review — Obtain a public or synthetic model card.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Assurance findings report\n\nCase: The validator relies on the builder’s examples\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Assurance findings report\nRole: AI assurance / model validation specialist\nCase: The validator relies on the builder’s examples\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_claims (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree submissions contain claims without independent evidence, so validation cannot close.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMap each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai assurance / model validation specialist.\nBefore release: Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMaintain independent challenge and test selection; distinguish evidence absence from confirmed failure.\nAdditional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nIndependent model review: Conclusions include data limitations and unresolved findings.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI assurance / model validation specialist — The validator relies on the builder’s examples\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unsupported_claims >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and assurance findings report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_claims in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Map each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as assurance findings report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Additional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Independent model review — Obtain a public or synthetic model card.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,unsupported_claims\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unsupported_claims\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unsupported_claims\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Assurance findings report\n\nCase: The validator relies on the builder’s examples\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Assurance findings report\nRole: AI assurance / model validation specialist\nCase: The validator relies on the builder’s examples\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_claims (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree submissions contain claims without independent evidence, so validation cannot close.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMap each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai assurance / model validation specialist.\nBefore release: Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMaintain independent challenge and test selection; distinguish evidence absence from confirmed failure.\nAdditional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nIndependent model review: Conclusions include data limitations and unresolved findings.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"23a4d41cc0a5d3d64001f5f5b26eae964788c553a0ffe19ddba4c32f422e4d98","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The validator relies on the builder’s examples","scenario":"The independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The validator relies on the builder’s examples\nid,unsupported_claims\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_claims >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_claims >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three submissions contain claims without independent evidence, so validation cannot close.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three submissions contain claims without independent evidence, so validation cannot close. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The validator relies on the builder’s examples","scenario":"The independent review protocol requires zero unreviewed material claims in the release evidence pack.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The validator relies on the builder’s examples\nid,unsupported_claims\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_claims >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_claims >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Map each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence.","correct":true,"feedback":"Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Map each claim to a reproducible test or explicit limitation and request missing evidence. Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI assurance / model validation specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Additional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Maintain independent challenge and test selection; distinguish evidence absence from confirmed failure.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Maintain independent challenge and test selection; distinguish evidence absence from confirmed failure. Additional validation costs time; prioritize material claims without silently treating unchecked claims as true. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your assurance findings report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the assurance findings report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Reproduce key results and record unresolved findings with severity and owner. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"ai-privacy":{"revision":1,"title":"Deletion misses the derived store","brief":"The fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"active_copy_age","unit":"hours after deletion request","rows":[{"id":"case-1","active_copy_age":0},{"id":"case-2","active_copy_age":20},{"id":"case-3","active_copy_age":25},{"id":"case-4","active_copy_age":72},{"id":"case-5","active_copy_age":4}],"policy":{"field":"active_copy_age","operator":">","threshold":96},"expected":{"field":"active_copy_age","operator":">","threshold":24},"rule":"Review when active_copy_age > 24 (hours after deletion request). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two deleted records remain in active derived stores beyond the defined policy window.","repair":"Trace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies.","verify":"Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review.","deliverable":"Deletion evidence ledger","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and deletion evidence ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using active_copy_age in hours after deletion request. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 96 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Trace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as deletion evidence ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Deleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI data-flow register — Model a synthetic support assistant.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Deletion evidence ledger\n\nCase: Deletion misses the derived store\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Deletion evidence ledger\nRole: AI privacy / data-provenance specialist\nCase: Deletion misses the derived store\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | active_copy_age (hours after deletion request) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 20 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 25 | review |\n| case-4 | 72 | review |\n| case-5 | 4 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo deleted records remain in active derived stores beyond the defined policy window.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nTrace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai privacy / data-provenance specialist.\nBefore release: Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack lineage and responsible owners across derived copies; minimize unnecessary sensitive logging.\nDeleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI data-flow register: Derived embeddings, logs and backups are considered explicitly.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI privacy / data-provenance specialist — Deletion misses the derived store\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.\n\n## Contract\nReview when active_copy_age > 24 (hours after deletion request). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and deletion evidence ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using active_copy_age in hours after deletion request. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 96 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Trace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as deletion evidence ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Deleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRecord active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI data-flow register — Model a synthetic support assistant.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n","evidence.csv":"id,active_copy_age\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,20\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,72\r\ncase-5,4\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"active_copy_age\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 96\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"active_copy_age\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 24\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Deletion evidence ledger\n\nCase: Deletion misses the derived store\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Deletion evidence ledger\nRole: AI privacy / data-provenance specialist\nCase: Deletion misses the derived store\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | active_copy_age (hours after deletion request) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 20 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 25 | review |\n| case-4 | 72 | review |\n| case-5 | 4 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo deleted records remain in active derived stores beyond the defined policy window.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nTrace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai privacy / data-provenance specialist.\nBefore release: Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack lineage and responsible owners across derived copies; minimize unnecessary sensitive logging.\nDeleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI data-flow register: Derived embeddings, logs and backups are considered explicitly.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"7cd63fb95815bc793d5db5e260693e2a72f050b3f125506d50653e0c3b7b082f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Deletion misses the derived store","scenario":"The fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Deletion misses the derived store\nid,active_copy_age\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,20\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,72\r\ncase-5,4\r\n\nCurrent review rule: active_copy_age > 96\nRequired contract: Review when active_copy_age > 24 (hours after deletion request). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two deleted records remain in active derived stores beyond the defined policy window.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two deleted records remain in active derived stores beyond the defined policy window. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Deletion misses the derived store","scenario":"The fictional retention policy requires deletion from active derived stores within 24 hours; backups are reviewed separately.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Deletion misses the derived store\nid,active_copy_age\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,20\r\ncase-3,25\r\ncase-4,72\r\ncase-5,4\r\n\nCurrent review rule: active_copy_age > 96\nRequired contract: Review when active_copy_age > 24 (hours after deletion request). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 96 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Trace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies.","correct":true,"feedback":"Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Trace source IDs through chunks, embeddings and logs, then remove or quarantine residual copies. Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI privacy / data-provenance specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 24 hours after deletion request as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Track lineage and responsible owners across derived copies; minimize unnecessary sensitive logging.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Deleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Track lineage and responsible owners across derived copies; minimize unnecessary sensitive logging. Deleting a source row does not establish deletion from embeddings, caches or backups; document each boundary. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your deletion evidence ledger is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the deletion evidence ledger with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Record active-store checks, backup handling and any retention exceptions for qualified review. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"}]}]},"ai-policy":{"revision":1,"title":"A policy claim loses its jurisdiction","brief":"The research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_context","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_context":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_context":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_context":2},{"id":"case-4","missing_context":0},{"id":"case-5","missing_context":3}],"policy":{"field":"missing_context","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"missing_context","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_context >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three claims lack the context needed to evaluate whether the source applies.","repair":"Annotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law.","verify":"A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text.","deliverable":"Policy options brief and source register","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and policy options brief and source register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_context in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Annotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as policy options brief and source register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Comparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI public-service policy brief — Choose a fictional public-service use case.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Policy options brief and source register\n\nCase: A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Policy options brief and source register\nRole: AI policy researcher / programme specialist\nCase: A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_context (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree claims lack the context needed to evaluate whether the source applies.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAnnotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai policy researcher / programme specialist.\nBefore release: A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate empirical findings, legal requirements and recommendations; include implementation owners.\nComparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI public-service policy brief: Distinguish recommendations from applicable law.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI policy researcher / programme specialist — A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_context >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and policy options brief and source register template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_context in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Annotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as policy options brief and source register. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Comparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nA reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI public-service policy brief — Choose a fictional public-service use case.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics: https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- Google Technical Writing: https://developers.google.com/tech-writing\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_context\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_context\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_context\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Policy options brief and source register\n\nCase: A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Policy options brief and source register\nRole: AI policy researcher / programme specialist\nCase: A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_context (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree claims lack the context needed to evaluate whether the source applies.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAnnotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai policy researcher / programme specialist.\nBefore release: A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate empirical findings, legal requirements and recommendations; include implementation owners.\nComparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI public-service policy brief: Distinguish recommendations from applicable law.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"8a3155d477f4a0afaa5680b1937c13df79c21096b8812df746bc83547e483860","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A policy claim loses its jurisdiction","scenario":"The research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\nid,missing_context\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_context >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_context >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three claims lack the context needed to evaluate whether the source applies.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three claims lack the context needed to evaluate whether the source applies. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A policy claim loses its jurisdiction","scenario":"The research brief requires every legal or institutional claim to record jurisdiction and publication date.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A policy claim loses its jurisdiction\nid,missing_context\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_context >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when missing_context >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Annotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law.","correct":true,"feedback":"A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Annotate primary sources and mark unsupported claims as unresolved rather than presenting them as current law. A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI policy researcher / programme specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate empirical findings, legal requirements and recommendations; include implementation owners.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Comparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Separate empirical findings, legal requirements and recommendations; include implementation owners. Comparative policy analysis is useful only when differences in institutions and scope remain explicit. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your policy options brief and source register is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the policy options brief and source register with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"A reviewer can trace every material claim to the relevant dated primary text. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"UNESCO Recommendation on AI Ethics","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"}]}]},"ai-product-manager":{"revision":1,"title":"Usage grows while the task fails","brief":"The pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"failed_tasks","unit":"out of ten attempts","rows":[{"id":"case-1","failed_tasks":0},{"id":"case-2","failed_tasks":2},{"id":"case-3","failed_tasks":3},{"id":"case-4","failed_tasks":6},{"id":"case-5","failed_tasks":1}],"policy":{"field":"failed_tasks","operator":">=","threshold":7},"expected":{"field":"failed_tasks","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when failed_tasks >= 3 (out of ten attempts). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two cohorts fail the product’s task-quality gate despite increased engagement.","repair":"Hold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow.","verify":"Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline.","deliverable":"Product launch decision memo","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and product launch decision memo template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using failed_tasks in out of ten attempts. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Hold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as product launch decision memo. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI product discovery dossier — Choose a narrow user workflow.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Product launch decision memo\n\nCase: Usage grows while the task fails\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Product launch decision memo\nRole: AI product manager\nCase: Usage grows while the task fails\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | failed_tasks (out of ten attempts) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cohorts fail the product’s task-quality gate despite increased engagement.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nHold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai product manager.\nBefore release: Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine outcome and guardrail metrics before the pilot; include evidence that would stop the launch.\nMore interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI product discovery dossier: Claims are traceable and assumptions are separated from research.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI product manager — Usage grows while the task fails\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.\n\n## Contract\nReview when failed_tasks >= 3 (out of ten attempts). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and product launch decision memo template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using failed_tasks in out of ten attempts. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Hold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as product launch decision memo. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCompare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI product discovery dossier — Choose a narrow user workflow.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- Figma learning resources: https://help.figma.com/\n","evidence.csv":"id,failed_tasks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"failed_tasks\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 7\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"failed_tasks\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Product launch decision memo\n\nCase: Usage grows while the task fails\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Product launch decision memo\nRole: AI product manager\nCase: Usage grows while the task fails\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | failed_tasks (out of ten attempts) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cohorts fail the product’s task-quality gate despite increased engagement.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nHold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai product manager.\nBefore release: Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine outcome and guardrail metrics before the pilot; include evidence that would stop the launch.\nMore interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI product discovery dossier: Claims are traceable and assumptions are separated from research.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"fe96b9c582f5e3cf6b2c6201916f3b9bc914eb4e6df99600e180d6d49059ac93","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Usage grows while the task fails","scenario":"The pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Usage grows while the task fails\nid,failed_tasks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: failed_tasks >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when failed_tasks >= 3 (out of ten attempts). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two cohorts fail the product’s task-quality gate despite increased engagement.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two cohorts fail the product’s task-quality gate despite increased engagement. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Usage grows while the task fails","scenario":"The pilot gate flags more than two failed critical tasks in ten observed attempts, regardless of message volume.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Usage grows while the task fails\nid,failed_tasks\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: failed_tasks >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when failed_tasks >= 3 (out of ten attempts). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Hold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow.","correct":true,"feedback":"Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 7 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Hold expansion, investigate failure types and revise the product hypothesis or workflow. Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI product manager. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Define outcome and guardrail metrics before the pilot; include evidence that would stop the launch.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten attempts as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Define outcome and guardrail metrics before the pilot; include evidence that would stop the launch. More interaction can indicate confusion rather than value; include qualitative task observation. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your product launch decision memo is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the product launch decision memo with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Compare task success, review effort, latency and operating cost against the manual baseline. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"}]}]},"ai-business-analyst":{"revision":1,"title":"Automation moves work into exceptions","brief":"The process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"rework_cases","unit":"out of ten cases","rows":[{"id":"case-1","rework_cases":0},{"id":"case-2","rework_cases":2},{"id":"case-3","rework_cases":3},{"id":"case-4","rework_cases":5},{"id":"case-5","rework_cases":1}],"policy":{"field":"rework_cases","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"rework_cases","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when rework_cases >= 3 (out of ten cases). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two batches exceed the exception allowance; claimed time savings omitted rework.","repair":"Map the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation.","verify":"Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations.","deliverable":"Process map and benefit-assumption worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and process map and benefit-assumption worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using rework_cases in out of ten cases. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Map the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as process map and benefit-assumption worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Automating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Automation opportunity assessment — Map five fictional business processes.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Process map and benefit-assumption worksheet\n\nCase: Automation moves work into exceptions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Process map and benefit-assumption worksheet\nRole: AI business / process analyst\nCase: Automation moves work into exceptions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | rework_cases (out of ten cases) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the exception allowance; claimed time savings omitted rework.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMap the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai business / process analyst.\nBefore release: Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDocument normal and exception flows; distinguish required capability from a chosen vendor feature.\nAutomating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAutomation opportunity assessment: Ranking exposes assumptions and data gaps.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI business / process analyst — Automation moves work into exceptions\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.\n\n## Contract\nReview when rework_cases >= 3 (out of ten cases). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and process map and benefit-assumption worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using rework_cases in out of ten cases. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Map the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as process map and benefit-assumption worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Automating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRecalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Automation opportunity assessment — Map five fictional business processes.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Microsoft Power Automate: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,rework_cases\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"rework_cases\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"rework_cases\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Process map and benefit-assumption worksheet\n\nCase: Automation moves work into exceptions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Process map and benefit-assumption worksheet\nRole: AI business / process analyst\nCase: Automation moves work into exceptions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | rework_cases (out of ten cases) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the exception allowance; claimed time savings omitted rework.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMap the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai business / process analyst.\nBefore release: Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDocument normal and exception flows; distinguish required capability from a chosen vendor feature.\nAutomating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAutomation opportunity assessment: Ranking exposes assumptions and data gaps.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"296b4246c9e0bd8f338aa985c0ab3996ccda0f30b74db0cdc467fbfa988cbdc9","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Automation moves work into exceptions","scenario":"The process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Automation moves work into exceptions\nid,rework_cases\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: rework_cases >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when rework_cases >= 3 (out of ten cases). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two batches exceed the exception allowance; claimed time savings omitted rework.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Two batches exceed the exception allowance; claimed time savings omitted rework. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Automation moves work into exceptions","scenario":"The process pilot flags more than two reworked cases in each ten-case batch.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Automation moves work into exceptions\nid,rework_cases\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: rework_cases >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when rework_cases >= 3 (out of ten cases). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Map the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation.","correct":true,"feedback":"Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Map the failed handoffs and include exception handling in the process and benefit calculation. Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI business / process analyst. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Automating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten cases as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Document normal and exception flows; distinguish required capability from a chosen vendor feature.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Document normal and exception flows; distinguish required capability from a chosen vendor feature. Automating a frequent step may not improve the whole process if downstream exceptions become expensive. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your process map and benefit-assumption worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the process map and benefit-assumption worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Recalculate total staff time, including review, corrections and escalations. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}]}]},"ai-adoption":{"revision":1,"title":"Attendance is mistaken for capability","brief":"The workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missed_seeded_errors","unit":"out of ten learners","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missed_seeded_errors":0},{"id":"case-2","missed_seeded_errors":2},{"id":"case-3","missed_seeded_errors":3},{"id":"case-4","missed_seeded_errors":6},{"id":"case-5","missed_seeded_errors":1}],"policy":{"field":"missed_seeded_errors","operator":">=","threshold":7},"expected":{"field":"missed_seeded_errors","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when missed_seeded_errors >= 3 (out of ten learners). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two cohorts need follow-up despite attending the training; attendance did not establish verification skill.","repair":"Add task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use.","verify":"Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers.","deliverable":"Enablement plan and learning assessment","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and enablement plan and learning assessment template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missed_seeded_errors in out of ten learners. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as enablement plan and learning assessment. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Role-based AI workshop — Choose one team and three tasks.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Enablement plan and learning assessment\n\nCase: Attendance is mistaken for capability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Enablement plan and learning assessment\nRole: AI adoption / enablement specialist\nCase: Attendance is mistaken for capability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missed_seeded_errors (out of ten learners) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cohorts need follow-up despite attending the training; attendance did not establish verification skill.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai adoption / enablement specialist.\nBefore release: Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTeach role-specific workflows and escalation; measure task competence after training.\nA short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRole-based AI workshop: Assessment measures task competence rather than tool enthusiasm.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI adoption / enablement specialist — Attendance is mistaken for capability\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missed_seeded_errors >= 3 (out of ten learners). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and enablement plan and learning assessment template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missed_seeded_errors in out of ten learners. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as enablement plan and learning assessment. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRepeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Role-based AI workshop — Choose one team and three tasks.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- Google Technical Writing: https://developers.google.com/tech-writing\n- Microsoft Power Automate: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/\n","evidence.csv":"id,missed_seeded_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missed_seeded_errors\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 7\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missed_seeded_errors\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Enablement plan and learning assessment\n\nCase: Attendance is mistaken for capability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Enablement plan and learning assessment\nRole: AI adoption / enablement specialist\nCase: Attendance is mistaken for capability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missed_seeded_errors (out of ten learners) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cohorts need follow-up despite attending the training; attendance did not establish verification skill.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai adoption / enablement specialist.\nBefore release: Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTeach role-specific workflows and escalation; measure task competence after training.\nA short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nRole-based AI workshop: Assessment measures task competence rather than tool enthusiasm.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"08a59440a10b6fae617baebc488659adc910fe7d781f1003575bd27d68c413c9","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Attendance is mistaken for capability","scenario":"The workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Attendance is mistaken for capability\nid,missed_seeded_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missed_seeded_errors >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when missed_seeded_errors >= 3 (out of ten learners). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two cohorts need follow-up despite attending the training; attendance did not establish verification skill.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two cohorts need follow-up despite attending the training; attendance did not establish verification skill. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Attendance is mistaken for capability","scenario":"The workshop gate flags more than two participants in ten unable to verify a seeded AI error.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Attendance is mistaken for capability\nid,missed_seeded_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missed_seeded_errors >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when missed_seeded_errors >= 3 (out of ten learners). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 7 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use.","correct":true,"feedback":"Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Add task-specific verification exercises and coaching before broadening independent use. Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI adoption / enablement specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten learners as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Teach role-specific workflows and escalation; measure task competence after training.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Teach role-specific workflows and escalation; measure task competence after training. A short workshop can build awareness, but sustained adoption requires practice, support and measurable outcomes. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your enablement plan and learning assessment is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the enablement plan and learning assessment with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Repeat a new task with seeded errors and record reasoning, not copied answers. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]}]},"ai-ux":{"revision":1,"title":"Users cannot undo an AI edit","brief":"The usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unrecoverable_edits","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unrecoverable_edits":0},{"id":"case-2","unrecoverable_edits":1},{"id":"case-3","unrecoverable_edits":2},{"id":"case-4","unrecoverable_edits":0},{"id":"case-5","unrecoverable_edits":1}],"policy":{"field":"unrecoverable_edits","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"unrecoverable_edits","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unrecoverable_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three tasks lack a usable recovery path after an incorrect suggestion.","repair":"Add reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction.","verify":"Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs.","deliverable":"Interaction-state specification and usability findings","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and interaction-state specification and usability findings template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unrecoverable_edits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as interaction-state specification and usability findings. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Extra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI review interface — Prototype editable suggestions with source evidence.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Interaction-state specification and usability findings\n\nCase: Users cannot undo an AI edit\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Interaction-state specification and usability findings\nRole: AI UX / product designer\nCase: Users cannot undo an AI edit\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unrecoverable_edits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree tasks lack a usable recovery path after an incorrect suggestion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai ux / product designer.\nBefore release: Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDesign correction and recovery alongside the happy path; distinguish suggestion from approved content.\nExtra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI review interface: Users distinguish model suggestions from verified facts.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI UX / product designer — Users cannot undo an AI edit\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unrecoverable_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and interaction-state specification and usability findings template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unrecoverable_edits in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as interaction-state specification and usability findings. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Extra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI review interface — Prototype editable suggestions with source evidence.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Figma learning resources: https://help.figma.com/\n- W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials: https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,unrecoverable_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unrecoverable_edits\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unrecoverable_edits\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Interaction-state specification and usability findings\n\nCase: Users cannot undo an AI edit\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Interaction-state specification and usability findings\nRole: AI UX / product designer\nCase: Users cannot undo an AI edit\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unrecoverable_edits (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree tasks lack a usable recovery path after an incorrect suggestion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai ux / product designer.\nBefore release: Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDesign correction and recovery alongside the happy path; distinguish suggestion from approved content.\nExtra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI review interface: Users distinguish model suggestions from verified facts.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"afe2bfc2aefdf38e9cb997797c71f07e1ed2066600723ce3d0f819e4f4a30cbd","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Users cannot undo an AI edit","scenario":"The usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Users cannot undo an AI edit\nid,unrecoverable_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unrecoverable_edits >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when unrecoverable_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three tasks lack a usable recovery path after an incorrect suggestion.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three tasks lack a usable recovery path after an incorrect suggestion. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Users cannot undo an AI edit","scenario":"The usability pilot flags any task where an incorrect generated edit cannot be reversed through the interface.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Users cannot undo an AI edit\nid,unrecoverable_edits\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unrecoverable_edits >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when unrecoverable_edits >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Add reviewable suggestions, version history and an accessible undo interaction. Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI UX / product designer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Design correction and recovery alongside the happy path; distinguish suggestion from approved content.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Extra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Design correction and recovery alongside the happy path; distinguish suggestion from approved content. Extra review controls can add friction, so measure task completion and error recovery together. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your interaction-state specification and usability findings is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the interaction-state specification and usability findings with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Test keyboard and assistive-technology recovery with intentionally wrong outputs. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"conversation-designer":{"revision":1,"title":"Corrections are heard but not applied","brief":"The scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"ignored_corrections","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","ignored_corrections":0},{"id":"case-2","ignored_corrections":1},{"id":"case-3","ignored_corrections":0},{"id":"case-4","ignored_corrections":2},{"id":"case-5","ignored_corrections":3}],"policy":{"field":"ignored_corrections","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"ignored_corrections","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when ignored_corrections >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three dialogues confirm stale values after the user has corrected them.","repair":"Add an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment.","verify":"Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances.","deliverable":"Conversation flow and repair transcript","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and conversation flow and repair transcript template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using ignored_corrections in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as conversation flow and repair transcript. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Repeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Service conversation pack — Define a booking task.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Conversation flow and repair transcript\n\nCase: Corrections are heard but not applied\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Conversation flow and repair transcript\nRole: Conversation designer\nCase: Corrections are heard but not applied\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | ignored_corrections (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree dialogues confirm stale values after the user has corrected them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in conversation designer.\nBefore release: Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep tentative and confirmed slots separate; design repair paths for each critical field.\nRepeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nService conversation pack: Every branch preserves confirmed state and explains the next action.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Conversation designer — Corrections are heard but not applied\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.\n\n## Contract\nReview when ignored_corrections >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and conversation flow and repair transcript template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using ignored_corrections in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as conversation flow and repair transcript. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Repeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRole-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Service conversation pack — Define a booking task.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Figma learning resources: https://help.figma.com/\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n- W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials: https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/\n","evidence.csv":"id,ignored_corrections\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"ignored_corrections\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"ignored_corrections\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Conversation flow and repair transcript\n\nCase: Corrections are heard but not applied\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Conversation flow and repair transcript\nRole: Conversation designer\nCase: Corrections are heard but not applied\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | ignored_corrections (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree dialogues confirm stale values after the user has corrected them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in conversation designer.\nBefore release: Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep tentative and confirmed slots separate; design repair paths for each critical field.\nRepeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nService conversation pack: Every branch preserves confirmed state and explains the next action.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"97dcdf6d780d263fe74f49820ab08254fd137d970b146d9c24b6951949642431","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Corrections are heard but not applied","scenario":"The scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Corrections are heard but not applied\nid,ignored_corrections\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: ignored_corrections >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when ignored_corrections >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three dialogues confirm stale values after the user has corrected them.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three dialogues confirm stale values after the user has corrected them. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Corrections are heard but not applied","scenario":"The scripted booking assistant must update every explicitly corrected critical field before confirmation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Corrections are heard but not applied\nid,ignored_corrections\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: ignored_corrections >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when ignored_corrections >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment.","correct":true,"feedback":"Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Add an explicit correction transition and read back the updated field before commitment. Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Conversation designer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Keep tentative and confirmed slots separate; design repair paths for each critical field.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Repeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Keep tentative and confirmed slots separate; design repair paths for each critical field. Repeated confirmations can frustrate users; focus them on ambiguity and high-cost errors. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your conversation flow and repair transcript is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the conversation flow and repair transcript with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Role-play correction, interruption, ambiguity and human handoff using new utterances. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"}]}]},"generative-media":{"revision":1,"title":"A shot has no rights record","brief":"The fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unresolved_assets","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unresolved_assets":0},{"id":"case-2","unresolved_assets":1},{"id":"case-3","unresolved_assets":3},{"id":"case-4","unresolved_assets":0},{"id":"case-5","unresolved_assets":2}],"policy":{"field":"unresolved_assets","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unresolved_assets","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unresolved_assets >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three shots contain assets with unresolved provenance; visual quality does not clear delivery.","repair":"Replace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export.","verify":"Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio.","deliverable":"Production breakdown and asset-rights ledger","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and production breakdown and asset-rights ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unresolved_assets in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Replace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as production breakdown and asset-rights ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Generative short-film sequence — Storyboard a rights-cleared 20-second scene.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Production breakdown and asset-rights ledger\n\nCase: A shot has no rights record\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Production breakdown and asset-rights ledger\nRole: Generative-media producer / technical artist\nCase: A shot has no rights record\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unresolved_assets (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree shots contain assets with unresolved provenance; visual quality does not clear delivery.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReplace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in generative-media producer / technical artist.\nBefore release: Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack original, licensed and generated ingredients; preserve human edits and approval history.\nA fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGenerative short-film sequence: Deliver an edit breakdown and asset provenance ledger.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Generative-media producer / technical artist — A shot has no rights record\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unresolved_assets >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and production breakdown and asset-rights ledger template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unresolved_assets in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Replace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as production breakdown and asset-rights ledger. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReview the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Generative short-film sequence — Storyboard a rights-cleared 20-second scene.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Runway developer documentation: https://dev.runwayml.com/\n- Blender manual: https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/\n- C2PA specifications: https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/\n","evidence.csv":"id,unresolved_assets\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unresolved_assets\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unresolved_assets\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Production breakdown and asset-rights ledger\n\nCase: A shot has no rights record\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Production breakdown and asset-rights ledger\nRole: Generative-media producer / technical artist\nCase: A shot has no rights record\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unresolved_assets (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree shots contain assets with unresolved provenance; visual quality does not clear delivery.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nReplace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in generative-media producer / technical artist.\nBefore release: Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTrack original, licensed and generated ingredients; preserve human edits and approval history.\nA fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGenerative short-film sequence: Deliver an edit breakdown and asset provenance ledger.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"83e8e38a93a77afaf0b8e13166bfc4352014ba0236b5c4c270447e25e3698932","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A shot has no rights record","scenario":"The fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A shot has no rights record\nid,unresolved_assets\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unresolved_assets >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unresolved_assets >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three shots contain assets with unresolved provenance; visual quality does not clear delivery.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three shots contain assets with unresolved provenance; visual quality does not clear delivery. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Runway developer documentation","url":"https://dev.runwayml.com/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A shot has no rights record","scenario":"The fictional production requires a documented source and permitted use for every visible asset before delivery.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A shot has no rights record\nid,unresolved_assets\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unresolved_assets >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unresolved_assets >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Replace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export.","correct":true,"feedback":"Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Replace, license or hold those assets and update the production ledger before export. Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Runway developer documentation","url":"https://dev.runwayml.com/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Generative-media producer / technical artist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Track original, licensed and generated ingredients; preserve human edits and approval history.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Track original, licensed and generated ingredients; preserve human edits and approval history. A fast generative pipeline still needs art direction, continuity and rights review; tool output alone is not clearance. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Runway developer documentation","url":"https://dev.runwayml.com/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your production breakdown and asset-rights ledger is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the production breakdown and asset-rights ledger with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Review the final edit against the asset ledger, including references, likenesses and audio. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Runway developer documentation","url":"https://dev.runwayml.com/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"}]}]},"ai-localization":{"revision":1,"title":"Fluency hides a changed meaning","brief":"The localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"critical_meaning_errors","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","critical_meaning_errors":0},{"id":"case-2","critical_meaning_errors":1},{"id":"case-3","critical_meaning_errors":2},{"id":"case-4","critical_meaning_errors":0},{"id":"case-5","critical_meaning_errors":1}],"policy":{"field":"critical_meaning_errors","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"critical_meaning_errors","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when critical_meaning_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three fluent outputs alter a consequential instruction, so fluency scoring alone is insufficient.","repair":"Revise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication.","verify":"Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately.","deliverable":"Bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_meaning_errors in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Revise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Bilingual assistant evaluation — Choose a language pair and support domain.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide\n\nCase: Fluency hides a changed meaning\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide\nRole: AI localization / language specialist\nCase: Fluency hides a changed meaning\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_meaning_errors (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree fluent outputs alter a consequential instruction, so fluency scoring alone is insufficient.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRevise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai localization / language specialist.\nBefore release: Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nUse locally grounded examples and a calibrated rubric; record dialect scope and reviewer disagreement.\nA literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBilingual assistant evaluation: Publish terminology, error categories and sample limitations.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI localization / language specialist — Fluency hides a changed meaning\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.\n\n## Contract\nReview when critical_meaning_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using critical_meaning_errors in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Revise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nUse unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Bilingual assistant evaluation — Choose a language pair and support domain.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n","evidence.csv":"id,critical_meaning_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"critical_meaning_errors\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"critical_meaning_errors\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide\n\nCase: Fluency hides a changed meaning\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide\nRole: AI localization / language specialist\nCase: Fluency hides a changed meaning\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | critical_meaning_errors (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree fluent outputs alter a consequential instruction, so fluency scoring alone is insufficient.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRevise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai localization / language specialist.\nBefore release: Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nUse locally grounded examples and a calibrated rubric; record dialect scope and reviewer disagreement.\nA literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBilingual assistant evaluation: Publish terminology, error categories and sample limitations.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"70a6b9a3762ef56b49de4bae0b2f0b139027897b0ecbbd90484b1eddfd0657ec","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Fluency hides a changed meaning","scenario":"The localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Fluency hides a changed meaning\nid,critical_meaning_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_meaning_errors >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when critical_meaning_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three fluent outputs alter a consequential instruction, so fluency scoring alone is insufficient.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three fluent outputs alter a consequential instruction, so fluency scoring alone is insufficient. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Fluency hides a changed meaning","scenario":"The localization brief treats any mistranslated critical instruction as a release blocker.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Fluency hides a changed meaning\nid,critical_meaning_errors\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: critical_meaning_errors >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when critical_meaning_errors >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Revise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication.","correct":true,"feedback":"Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Revise terminology and examples with qualified language reviewers; preserve disputed items for adjudication. Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI localization / language specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Use locally grounded examples and a calibrated rubric; record dialect scope and reviewer disagreement.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Use locally grounded examples and a calibrated rubric; record dialect scope and reviewer disagreement. A literal translation can preserve words while losing task intent; context and audience determine acceptability. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the bilingual evaluation sheet and terminology guide with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Use unseen local tasks and report meaning, register and cultural-fit errors separately. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"}]}]},"domain-evaluator":{"revision":1,"title":"A plausible answer has no support","brief":"The annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unsupported_statements","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unsupported_statements":0},{"id":"case-2","unsupported_statements":1},{"id":"case-3","unsupported_statements":0},{"id":"case-4","unsupported_statements":3},{"id":"case-5","unsupported_statements":2}],"policy":{"field":"unsupported_statements","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unsupported_statements","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unsupported_statements >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three accepted answers include unsupported statements; polished wording influenced the ratings.","repair":"Separate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric.","verify":"Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements.","deliverable":"Annotated answer set and calibration note","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and annotated answer set and calibration note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_statements in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Separate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as annotated answer set and calibration note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Agreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Domain evaluation set — Choose a field you know.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Annotated answer set and calibration note\n\nCase: A plausible answer has no support\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Annotated answer set and calibration note\nRole: Domain evaluator / AI trainer\nCase: A plausible answer has no support\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_statements (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree accepted answers include unsupported statements; polished wording influenced the ratings.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nSeparate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in domain evaluator / ai trainer.\nBefore release: Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nWrite clear positive and negative examples; keep uncertain judgments visible for domain review.\nAgreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDomain evaluation set: Another qualified reviewer can apply the rubric.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Domain evaluator / AI trainer — A plausible answer has no support\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unsupported_statements >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and annotated answer set and calibration note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_statements in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Separate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as annotated answer set and calibration note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Agreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nHave another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Domain evaluation set — Choose a field you know.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n- Google Technical Writing: https://developers.google.com/tech-writing\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,unsupported_statements\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unsupported_statements\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unsupported_statements\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Annotated answer set and calibration note\n\nCase: A plausible answer has no support\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Annotated answer set and calibration note\nRole: Domain evaluator / AI trainer\nCase: A plausible answer has no support\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_statements (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree accepted answers include unsupported statements; polished wording influenced the ratings.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nSeparate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in domain evaluator / ai trainer.\nBefore release: Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nWrite clear positive and negative examples; keep uncertain judgments visible for domain review.\nAgreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDomain evaluation set: Another qualified reviewer can apply the rubric.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"26104ee141059db7f699dd49f5c67f02b58f55f274765ef01b8028bc6d365b26","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A plausible answer has no support","scenario":"The annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A plausible answer has no support\nid,unsupported_statements\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_statements >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_statements >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three accepted answers include unsupported statements; polished wording influenced the ratings.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three accepted answers include unsupported statements; polished wording influenced the ratings. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A plausible answer has no support","scenario":"The annotation rubric requires every accepted domain answer to have a checked factual basis.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A plausible answer has no support\nid,unsupported_statements\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_statements >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_statements >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Separate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric.","correct":true,"feedback":"Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Separate correctness from style and adjudicate evidence against the rubric. Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Domain evaluator / AI trainer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Write clear positive and negative examples; keep uncertain judgments visible for domain review.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Agreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Write clear positive and negative examples; keep uncertain judgments visible for domain review. Agreement is useful but does not prove truth when reviewers share the same mistaken assumption. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your annotated answer set and calibration note is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the annotated answer set and calibration note with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Have another qualified reviewer independently assess a new sample and resolve disagreements. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"human-data-ops":{"revision":1,"title":"A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes","brief":"The labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unresolved_disputes","unit":"out of ten items","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unresolved_disputes":0},{"id":"case-2","unresolved_disputes":2},{"id":"case-3","unresolved_disputes":3},{"id":"case-4","unresolved_disputes":5},{"id":"case-5","unresolved_disputes":1}],"policy":{"field":"unresolved_disputes","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"unresolved_disputes","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when unresolved_disputes >= 3 (out of ten items). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two batches exceed the agreed unresolved-dispute allowance despite high throughput.","repair":"Pause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator.","verify":"Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration.","deliverable":"Annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unresolved_disputes in out of ten items. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Pause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Speed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Annotation operations playbook — Define a synthetic labeling task.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook\n\nCase: A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook\nRole: Human-data operations / annotation-quality lead\nCase: A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unresolved_disputes (out of ten items) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the agreed unresolved-dispute allowance despite high throughput.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in human-data operations / annotation-quality lead.\nBefore release: Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nVersion guidelines and calibration sets; separate reviewer errors from ambiguous instructions.\nSpeed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAnnotation operations playbook: Includes quality gates, escalation and workload assumptions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Human-data operations / annotation-quality lead — A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unresolved_disputes >= 3 (out of ten items). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unresolved_disputes in out of ten items. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Pause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Speed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nAudit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Annotation operations playbook — Define a synthetic labeling task.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,unresolved_disputes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unresolved_disputes\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unresolved_disputes\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook\n\nCase: A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook\nRole: Human-data operations / annotation-quality lead\nCase: A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unresolved_disputes (out of ten items) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo batches exceed the agreed unresolved-dispute allowance despite high throughput.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nPause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in human-data operations / annotation-quality lead.\nBefore release: Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nVersion guidelines and calibration sets; separate reviewer errors from ambiguous instructions.\nSpeed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAnnotation operations playbook: Includes quality gates, escalation and workload assumptions.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"ec0eca83c917c2dee14be3ad9208e181311699f9dd03459678bd31682104da6c","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes","scenario":"The labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\nid,unresolved_disputes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unresolved_disputes >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when unresolved_disputes >= 3 (out of ten items). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two batches exceed the agreed unresolved-dispute allowance despite high throughput.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two batches exceed the agreed unresolved-dispute allowance despite high throughput. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes","scenario":"The labeling pilot flags more than two unresolved disputed items in a ten-item audit batch.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A fast queue accumulates unresolved disputes\nid,unresolved_disputes\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unresolved_disputes >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when unresolved_disputes >= 3 (out of ten items). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Pause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator.","correct":true,"feedback":"Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Pause acceptance for disputed labels, clarify instructions and assign an adjudicator. Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Human-data operations / annotation-quality lead. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Version guidelines and calibration sets; separate reviewer errors from ambiguous instructions.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Speed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten items as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Version guidelines and calibration sets; separate reviewer errors from ambiguous instructions. Speed targets can worsen quality if difficult cases are hidden or rushed; report rework and case mix. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the annotation operations scorecard and escalation playbook with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Audit a fresh sample for error types and guideline adherence after recalibration. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"ai-consultant":{"revision":1,"title":"The demo promises unsupported capability","brief":"The proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unsupported_promises","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unsupported_promises":0},{"id":"case-2","unsupported_promises":1},{"id":"case-3","unsupported_promises":2},{"id":"case-4","unsupported_promises":0},{"id":"case-5","unsupported_promises":3}],"policy":{"field":"unsupported_promises","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"unsupported_promises","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when unsupported_promises >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three proposals promise outcomes that the demonstration has not established.","repair":"Revise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment.","verify":"Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites.","deliverable":"Discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_promises in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Revise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI discovery and demo kit — Create a fictional customer brief.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal\n\nCase: The demo promises unsupported capability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal\nRole: AI solutions consultant / sales engineer\nCase: The demo promises unsupported capability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_promises (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree proposals promise outcomes that the demonstration has not established.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRevise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai solutions consultant / sales engineer.\nBefore release: Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nQualify use-case fit before showing features; separate observed behavior from roadmap promises.\nA narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI discovery and demo kit: Demo claims are supported by observable behavior.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI solutions consultant / sales engineer — The demo promises unsupported capability\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unsupported_promises >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unsupported_promises in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Revise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nImplementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI discovery and demo kit — Create a fictional customer brief.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Amazon Bedrock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html\n- Microsoft Foundry: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,unsupported_promises\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unsupported_promises\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unsupported_promises\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal\n\nCase: The demo promises unsupported capability\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal\nRole: AI solutions consultant / sales engineer\nCase: The demo promises unsupported capability\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unsupported_promises (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree proposals promise outcomes that the demonstration has not established.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRevise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai solutions consultant / sales engineer.\nBefore release: Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nQualify use-case fit before showing features; separate observed behavior from roadmap promises.\nA narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI discovery and demo kit: Demo claims are supported by observable behavior.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"add0bb79c2161ec26e0969ef61bb79a9a373b40b0269d5c058818e5901c81f4b","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The demo promises unsupported capability","scenario":"The proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The demo promises unsupported capability\nid,unsupported_promises\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_promises >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_promises >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three proposals promise outcomes that the demonstration has not established.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three proposals promise outcomes that the demonstration has not established. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The demo promises unsupported capability","scenario":"The proof-of-value proposal must contain zero material capability claims lacking demo evidence or an explicit dependency.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The demo promises unsupported capability\nid,unsupported_promises\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unsupported_promises >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when unsupported_promises >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Revise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment.","correct":true,"feedback":"Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Revise scope, mark assumptions and agree customer acceptance criteria before commitment. Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI solutions consultant / sales engineer. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Qualify use-case fit before showing features; separate observed behavior from roadmap promises.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Qualify use-case fit before showing features; separate observed behavior from roadmap promises. A narrower credible proposal may lose superficial breadth but prevents an unimplementable handoff. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the discovery brief and proof-of-value proposal with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Implementation owners can trace each promised capability to evidence and prerequisites. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Amazon Bedrock","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/what-is-bedrock.html"},{"title":"Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/what-is-foundry"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"ai-customer-success":{"revision":1,"title":"Seat activation hides failed onboarding","brief":"The onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"missing_onboarding_owners","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","missing_onboarding_owners":0},{"id":"case-2","missing_onboarding_owners":1},{"id":"case-3","missing_onboarding_owners":0},{"id":"case-4","missing_onboarding_owners":2},{"id":"case-5","missing_onboarding_owners":1}],"policy":{"field":"missing_onboarding_owners","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"missing_onboarding_owners","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when missing_onboarding_owners >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three activated accounts lack required ownership and escalation readiness.","repair":"Complete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use.","verify":"Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data.","deliverable":"Account success plan and escalation map","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and account success plan and escalation map template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_onboarding_owners in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Complete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as account success plan and escalation map. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: High activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI customer onboarding pack — Define a fictional account.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Account success plan and escalation map\n\nCase: Seat activation hides failed onboarding\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Account success plan and escalation map\nRole: AI customer-success / implementation specialist\nCase: Seat activation hides failed onboarding\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_onboarding_owners (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree activated accounts lack required ownership and escalation readiness.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nComplete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai customer-success / implementation specialist.\nBefore release: Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nConnect adoption to customer outcomes; separate training gaps from product defects.\nHigh activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI customer onboarding pack: Account owners and product limits are clear.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI customer-success / implementation specialist — Seat activation hides failed onboarding\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.\n\n## Contract\nReview when missing_onboarding_owners >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and account success plan and escalation map template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using missing_onboarding_owners in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Complete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as account success plan and escalation map. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: High activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nRun a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI customer onboarding pack — Define a fictional account.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google Technical Writing: https://developers.google.com/tech-writing\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- Microsoft Power Automate: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/\n","evidence.csv":"id,missing_onboarding_owners\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"missing_onboarding_owners\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"missing_onboarding_owners\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Account success plan and escalation map\n\nCase: Seat activation hides failed onboarding\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Account success plan and escalation map\nRole: AI customer-success / implementation specialist\nCase: Seat activation hides failed onboarding\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | missing_onboarding_owners (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree activated accounts lack required ownership and escalation readiness.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nComplete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai customer-success / implementation specialist.\nBefore release: Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nConnect adoption to customer outcomes; separate training gaps from product defects.\nHigh activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI customer onboarding pack: Account owners and product limits are clear.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"88a68624f27be153a45db5914d3065961f68b0af31e930d99804b9fd03b8054f","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Seat activation hides failed onboarding","scenario":"The onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Seat activation hides failed onboarding\nid,missing_onboarding_owners\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_onboarding_owners >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when missing_onboarding_owners >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three activated accounts lack required ownership and escalation readiness.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three activated accounts lack required ownership and escalation readiness. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Seat activation hides failed onboarding","scenario":"The onboarding gate flags any account missing an approved data owner or support escalation contact.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Seat activation hides failed onboarding\nid,missing_onboarding_owners\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: missing_onboarding_owners >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when missing_onboarding_owners >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Complete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use.","correct":true,"feedback":"Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Complete the success plan and resolve ownership before encouraging broader data use. Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI customer-success / implementation specialist. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Connect adoption to customer outcomes; separate training gaps from product defects.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: High activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Connect adoption to customer outcomes; separate training gaps from product defects. High activity without task value or support readiness is not a reliable renewal signal. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your account success plan and escalation map is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the account success plan and escalation map with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Run a sample support escalation and confirm the customer can identify allowed data. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"},{"title":"Microsoft Power Automate","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"}]}]},"ai-educator":{"revision":1,"title":"The tutorial works only for its author","brief":"The learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"hidden_prerequisites","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","hidden_prerequisites":0},{"id":"case-2","hidden_prerequisites":1},{"id":"case-3","hidden_prerequisites":2},{"id":"case-4","hidden_prerequisites":0},{"id":"case-5","hidden_prerequisites":3}],"policy":{"field":"hidden_prerequisites","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"hidden_prerequisites","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when hidden_prerequisites >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three tutorial runs depend on unstated setup, so the published steps are not reproducible.","repair":"Make prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch.","verify":"Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point.","deliverable":"Tested lesson and learner-observation log","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and tested lesson and learner-observation log template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using hidden_prerequisites in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Make prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as tested lesson and learner-observation log. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Concise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI fundamentals workshop — Choose a specific audience.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Tested lesson and learner-observation log\n\nCase: The tutorial works only for its author\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Tested lesson and learner-observation log\nRole: AI educator / technical writer / developer advocate\nCase: The tutorial works only for its author\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | hidden_prerequisites (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree tutorial runs depend on unstated setup, so the published steps are not reproducible.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMake prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai educator / technical writer / developer advocate.\nBefore release: Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTeach toward an observable task; verify examples and document limitations.\nConcise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI fundamentals workshop: Assessment measures understanding rather than memorized terminology.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI educator / technical writer / developer advocate — The tutorial works only for its author\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.\n\n## Contract\nReview when hidden_prerequisites >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and tested lesson and learner-observation log template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using hidden_prerequisites in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Make prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as tested lesson and learner-observation log. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Concise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nAsk a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: AI fundamentals workshop — Choose a specific audience.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google Technical Writing: https://developers.google.com/tech-writing\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n","evidence.csv":"id,hidden_prerequisites\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"hidden_prerequisites\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"hidden_prerequisites\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Tested lesson and learner-observation log\n\nCase: The tutorial works only for its author\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Tested lesson and learner-observation log\nRole: AI educator / technical writer / developer advocate\nCase: The tutorial works only for its author\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | hidden_prerequisites (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree tutorial runs depend on unstated setup, so the published steps are not reproducible.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMake prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai educator / technical writer / developer advocate.\nBefore release: Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTeach toward an observable task; verify examples and document limitations.\nConcise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAI fundamentals workshop: Assessment measures understanding rather than memorized terminology.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"5142e10ddb397bcd32d65c26508e35d5f85a9be3535349120d8af0fc8eea4ae5","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The tutorial works only for its author","scenario":"The learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The tutorial works only for its author\nid,hidden_prerequisites\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: hidden_prerequisites >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when hidden_prerequisites >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three tutorial runs depend on unstated setup, so the published steps are not reproducible.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three tutorial runs depend on unstated setup, so the published steps are not reproducible. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The tutorial works only for its author","scenario":"The learning pilot flags any hidden setup dependency discovered by a new learner.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The tutorial works only for its author\nid,hidden_prerequisites\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: hidden_prerequisites >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when hidden_prerequisites >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Make prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch.","correct":true,"feedback":"Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Make prerequisites, versions and expected outputs explicit and add a troubleshooting branch. Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI educator / technical writer / developer advocate. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Teach toward an observable task; verify examples and document limitations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Concise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Teach toward an observable task; verify examples and document limitations. Concise tutorials are useful only if they do not omit the steps learners need to reproduce the result. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your tested lesson and learner-observation log is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the tested lesson and learner-observation log with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Ask a new learner to follow the revised instructions from a clean starting point. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google Technical Writing","url":"https://developers.google.com/tech-writing"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]}]},"agent-identity":{"revision":1,"title":"Approval is reused for another action","brief":"The agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"changed_arguments","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","changed_arguments":0},{"id":"case-2","changed_arguments":1},{"id":"case-3","changed_arguments":2},{"id":"case-4","changed_arguments":0},{"id":"case-5","changed_arguments":1}],"policy":{"field":"changed_arguments","operator":">=","threshold":3},"expected":{"field":"changed_arguments","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when changed_arguments >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three actions reuse approval after changing resource or action parameters.","repair":"Bind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record.","verify":"Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once.","deliverable":"Authorization matrix and approval integrity report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and authorization matrix and approval integrity report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using changed_arguments in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Bind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as authorization matrix and approval integrity report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Broad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Delegated tool gateway — Define two users and three actions.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Authorization matrix and approval integrity report\n\nCase: Approval is reused for another action\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Authorization matrix and approval integrity report\nRole: Agent identity & authorization\nCase: Approval is reused for another action\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | changed_arguments (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree actions reuse approval after changing resource or action parameters.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nBind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent identity & authorization.\nBefore release: Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate authority outside the model; retain a trace from user approval to actual resource action.\nBroad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDelegated tool gateway: Unauthorized actions have zero side effects.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Agent identity & authorization — Approval is reused for another action\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.\n\n## Contract\nReview when changed_arguments >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and authorization matrix and approval integrity report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using changed_arguments in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 3 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Bind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as authorization matrix and approval integrity report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Broad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nChanged, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Delegated tool gateway — Define two users and three actions.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Open Policy Agent: https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project: https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n","evidence.csv":"id,changed_arguments\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"changed_arguments\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 3\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"changed_arguments\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Authorization matrix and approval integrity report\n\nCase: Approval is reused for another action\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Authorization matrix and approval integrity report\nRole: Agent identity & authorization\nCase: Approval is reused for another action\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | changed_arguments (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 1 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree actions reuse approval after changing resource or action parameters.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nBind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent identity & authorization.\nBefore release: Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nValidate authority outside the model; retain a trace from user approval to actual resource action.\nBroad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nDelegated tool gateway: Unauthorized actions have zero side effects.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"53c045b84260dfae5a7869f12c985251617ba20b4a57216340f581876bbe5abe","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Approval is reused for another action","scenario":"The agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Approval is reused for another action\nid,changed_arguments\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: changed_arguments >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when changed_arguments >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three actions reuse approval after changing resource or action parameters.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three actions reuse approval after changing resource or action parameters. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Approval is reused for another action","scenario":"The agent gateway rejects every action whose arguments differ from the human-approved action digest.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Approval is reused for another action\nid,changed_arguments\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: changed_arguments >= 3\nRequired contract: Review when changed_arguments >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Bind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record.","correct":true,"feedback":"Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 3 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Bind approval to identity, exact action scope, expiry and a replay-resistant request record. Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Agent identity & authorization. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Validate authority outside the model; retain a trace from user approval to actual resource action.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Broad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Validate authority outside the model; retain a trace from user approval to actual resource action. Broad approvals reduce friction but expand blast radius; scope must reflect the consequence of the action. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your authorization matrix and approval integrity report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the authorization matrix and approval integrity report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Changed, expired and revoked approvals fail without effects; the original approved action can complete once. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Open Policy Agent","url":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]}]},"agent-sandbox":{"revision":1,"title":"A completed run leaves writable state behind","brief":"The sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"shared_writable_artifacts","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","shared_writable_artifacts":0},{"id":"case-2","shared_writable_artifacts":1},{"id":"case-3","shared_writable_artifacts":0},{"id":"case-4","shared_writable_artifacts":3},{"id":"case-5","shared_writable_artifacts":2}],"policy":{"field":"shared_writable_artifacts","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"shared_writable_artifacts","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when shared_writable_artifacts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three runs leave mutable artifacts that another tenant could encounter.","repair":"Use separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle.","verify":"Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets.","deliverable":"Sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_writable_artifacts in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Use separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Bounded code runner — Run benign generated code in a disposable environment.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist\n\nCase: A completed run leaves writable state behind\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist\nRole: Agent sandbox & execution infrastructure\nCase: A completed run leaves writable state behind\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_writable_artifacts (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree runs leave mutable artifacts that another tenant could encounter.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nUse separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent sandbox & execution infrastructure.\nBefore release: Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nConstrain filesystem, network and resources; treat generated code as untrusted.\nA container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBounded code runner: Timeouts release resources and output is size-limited.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Agent sandbox & execution infrastructure — A completed run leaves writable state behind\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.\n\n## Contract\nReview when shared_writable_artifacts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_writable_artifacts in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Use separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Bounded code runner — Run benign generated code in a disposable environment.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Kubernetes: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project: https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n","evidence.csv":"id,shared_writable_artifacts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"shared_writable_artifacts\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"shared_writable_artifacts\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist\n\nCase: A completed run leaves writable state behind\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist\nRole: Agent sandbox & execution infrastructure\nCase: A completed run leaves writable state behind\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_writable_artifacts (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 3 | review |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree runs leave mutable artifacts that another tenant could encounter.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nUse separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in agent sandbox & execution infrastructure.\nBefore release: Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nConstrain filesystem, network and resources; treat generated code as untrusted.\nA container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nBounded code runner: Timeouts release resources and output is size-limited.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"fcabffbcf444b6a86591e341cfe37e5de2d0bdb5a0b6a4987ca575d9f6b867b0","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A completed run leaves writable state behind","scenario":"The sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A completed run leaves writable state behind\nid,shared_writable_artifacts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_writable_artifacts >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_writable_artifacts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three runs leave mutable artifacts that another tenant could encounter.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three runs leave mutable artifacts that another tenant could encounter. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A completed run leaves writable state behind","scenario":"The sandbox lifecycle contract permits zero writable artifacts shared between isolated tenant runs.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A completed run leaves writable state behind\nid,shared_writable_artifacts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,3\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_writable_artifacts >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_writable_artifacts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Use separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Use separate per-run storage and a verified cleanup or quarantine lifecycle. Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Agent sandbox & execution infrastructure. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Constrain filesystem, network and resources; treat generated code as untrusted.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Constrain filesystem, network and resources; treat generated code as untrusted. A container boundary alone does not establish strong tenant isolation; document the mechanism and threat model. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the sandbox isolation and lifecycle checklist with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Test concurrent runs, timeout cleanup and safe artifact export without host secrets. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"}]}]},"evaluation-integrity":{"revision":1,"title":"An impressive score uses leaked tasks","brief":"The benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"known_overlaps","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","known_overlaps":0},{"id":"case-2","known_overlaps":1},{"id":"case-3","known_overlaps":3},{"id":"case-4","known_overlaps":0},{"id":"case-5","known_overlaps":2}],"policy":{"field":"known_overlaps","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"known_overlaps","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when known_overlaps >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three benchmark subsets have known overlap and cannot support the claimed uncontaminated comparison.","repair":"Quarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks.","verify":"Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations.","deliverable":"Benchmark provenance and contamination report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and benchmark provenance and contamination report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using known_overlaps in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Quarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as benchmark provenance and contamination report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: No detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: False-success benchmark — Seed three deceptive completion artifacts.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Benchmark provenance and contamination report\n\nCase: An impressive score uses leaked tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Benchmark provenance and contamination report\nRole: Evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis\nCase: An impressive score uses leaked tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | known_overlaps (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree benchmark subsets have known overlap and cannot support the claimed uncontaminated comparison.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nQuarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis.\nBefore release: Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep evaluation provenance separate and reviewable; use independent outcome verifiers.\nNo detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFalse-success benchmark: Publish a reproducible false-pass reduction.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis — An impressive score uses leaked tasks\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.\n\n## Contract\nReview when known_overlaps >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and benchmark provenance and contamination report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using known_overlaps in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Quarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as benchmark provenance and contamination report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: No detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: False-success benchmark — Seed three deceptive completion artifacts.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n- GitHub Actions: https://docs.github.com/en/actions\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n","evidence.csv":"id,known_overlaps\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"known_overlaps\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"known_overlaps\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Benchmark provenance and contamination report\n\nCase: An impressive score uses leaked tasks\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Benchmark provenance and contamination report\nRole: Evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis\nCase: An impressive score uses leaked tasks\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | known_overlaps (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree benchmark subsets have known overlap and cannot support the claimed uncontaminated comparison.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nQuarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis.\nBefore release: Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nKeep evaluation provenance separate and reviewable; use independent outcome verifiers.\nNo detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nFalse-success benchmark: Publish a reproducible false-pass reduction.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"1fe9b18b8e81567502d97861fe8b68e6b29b0c497c3dbd54b3358f8d7fcc1e29","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"An impressive score uses leaked tasks","scenario":"The benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — An impressive score uses leaked tasks\nid,known_overlaps\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: known_overlaps >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when known_overlaps >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three benchmark subsets have known overlap and cannot support the claimed uncontaminated comparison.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three benchmark subsets have known overlap and cannot support the claimed uncontaminated comparison. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: An impressive score uses leaked tasks","scenario":"The benchmark protocol forbids exact task identifiers shared with the disclosed tuning corpus.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — An impressive score uses leaked tasks\nid,known_overlaps\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: known_overlaps >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when known_overlaps >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Quarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Quarantine overlaps, report affected results and evaluate on independently sourced held-out tasks. Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Evaluation integrity & reward-hacking analysis. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Keep evaluation provenance separate and reviewable; use independent outcome verifiers.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: No detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Keep evaluation provenance separate and reviewable; use independent outcome verifiers. No detected overlap is not proof of a clean benchmark; similarity tests have blind spots. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your benchmark provenance and contamination report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the benchmark provenance and contamination report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Test seeded overlaps and paraphrases, stating detector limitations. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"},{"title":"GitHub Actions","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"}]}]},"data-contamination":{"revision":1,"title":"Near duplicates cross the split","brief":"The training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"cross_split_families","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","cross_split_families":0},{"id":"case-2","cross_split_families":1},{"id":"case-3","cross_split_families":2},{"id":"case-4","cross_split_families":0},{"id":"case-5","cross_split_families":4}],"policy":{"field":"cross_split_families","operator":">=","threshold":5},"expected":{"field":"cross_split_families","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when cross_split_families >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three partitions contain families spread across training and evaluation.","repair":"Group duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest.","verify":"Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives.","deliverable":"Data contamination audit and split manifest","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and data contamination audit and split manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using cross_split_families in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Group duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as data contamination audit and split manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Aggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Contamination scanner — Seed exact and near-duplicate records.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Data contamination audit and split manifest\n\nCase: Near duplicates cross the split\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Data contamination audit and split manifest\nRole: Training-data quality & contamination detection\nCase: Near duplicates cross the split\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | cross_split_families (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 4 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree partitions contain families spread across training and evaluation.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nGroup duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in training-data quality & contamination detection.\nBefore release: Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine provenance, grouping and sampled review; record both missed and overflagged pairs.\nAggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nContamination scanner: Report detector errors and group-level split integrity.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Training-data quality & contamination detection — Near duplicates cross the split\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.\n\n## Contract\nReview when cross_split_families >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and data contamination audit and split manifest template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using cross_split_families in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 5 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Group duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as data contamination audit and split manifest. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Aggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCheck exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Contamination scanner — Seed exact and near-duplicate records.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Apache Spark: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,cross_split_families\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,4\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"cross_split_families\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 5\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"cross_split_families\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Data contamination audit and split manifest\n\nCase: Near duplicates cross the split\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Data contamination audit and split manifest\nRole: Training-data quality & contamination detection\nCase: Near duplicates cross the split\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | cross_split_families (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 4 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree partitions contain families spread across training and evaluation.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nGroup duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in training-data quality & contamination detection.\nBefore release: Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine provenance, grouping and sampled review; record both missed and overflagged pairs.\nAggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nContamination scanner: Report detector errors and group-level split integrity.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"03c204f459a74eae93c848ac62c783d5cd47acac9d5b5cd3f113e2ca7ac7ba6c","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Near duplicates cross the split","scenario":"The training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Near duplicates cross the split\nid,cross_split_families\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,4\r\n\nCurrent review rule: cross_split_families >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when cross_split_families >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three partitions contain families spread across training and evaluation.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three partitions contain families spread across training and evaluation. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Near duplicates cross the split","scenario":"The training protocol requires duplicate-family groups to stay within a single split.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Near duplicates cross the split\nid,cross_split_families\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,4\r\n\nCurrent review rule: cross_split_families >= 5\nRequired contract: Review when cross_split_families >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Group duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest.","correct":true,"feedback":"Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 5 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Group duplicate families before splitting and retain a traceable manifest. Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Training-data quality & contamination detection. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Combine provenance, grouping and sampled review; record both missed and overflagged pairs.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Aggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Combine provenance, grouping and sampled review; record both missed and overflagged pairs. Aggressive deduplication may remove legitimate diversity; report the precision/recall trade-off. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your data contamination audit and split manifest is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the data contamination audit and split manifest with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Check exact and approximate overlap on seeded cases and review suspected false positives. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Apache Spark","url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"knowledge-architecture":{"revision":1,"title":"Two policies claim to be current","brief":"The knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"active_conflicts","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","active_conflicts":0},{"id":"case-2","active_conflicts":1},{"id":"case-3","active_conflicts":2},{"id":"case-4","active_conflicts":0},{"id":"case-5","active_conflicts":3}],"policy":{"field":"active_conflicts","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"active_conflicts","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when active_conflicts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three topics have unresolved authority conflicts; retrieval cannot decide which policy governs.","repair":"Assign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions.","verify":"Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction.","deliverable":"Knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using active_conflicts in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Assign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Better embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Knowledge governance map — Inventory fictional departmental sources.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan\n\nCase: Two policies claim to be current\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan\nRole: Enterprise AI knowledge architect\nCase: Two policies claim to be current\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | active_conflicts (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree topics have unresolved authority conflicts; retrieval cannot decide which policy governs.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAssign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in enterprise ai knowledge architect.\nBefore release: Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCarry authority, effective date and owner metadata into retrieval; expose unresolved conflicts.\nBetter embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nKnowledge governance map: Conflicts have an escalation and retirement process.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Enterprise AI knowledge architect — Two policies claim to be current\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.\n\n## Contract\nReview when active_conflicts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using active_conflicts in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Assign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Better embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nSearch for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Knowledge governance map — Inventory fictional departmental sources.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,active_conflicts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"active_conflicts\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"active_conflicts\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan\n\nCase: Two policies claim to be current\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan\nRole: Enterprise AI knowledge architect\nCase: Two policies claim to be current\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | active_conflicts (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree topics have unresolved authority conflicts; retrieval cannot decide which policy governs.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAssign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in enterprise ai knowledge architect.\nBefore release: Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCarry authority, effective date and owner metadata into retrieval; expose unresolved conflicts.\nBetter embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nKnowledge governance map: Conflicts have an escalation and retirement process.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"ce2b76996954e77625fac0a0954dc6de81d54d2502b677cd6bc327d30c41a59b","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Two policies claim to be current","scenario":"The knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Two policies claim to be current\nid,active_conflicts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: active_conflicts >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when active_conflicts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three topics have unresolved authority conflicts; retrieval cannot decide which policy governs.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three topics have unresolved authority conflicts; retrieval cannot decide which policy governs. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Two policies claim to be current","scenario":"The knowledge workflow flags any topic with more than one conflicting active authoritative policy.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Two policies claim to be current\nid,active_conflicts\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: active_conflicts >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when active_conflicts >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Assign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Assign an owner, resolve effective dates and retire or clearly scope conflicting versions. Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Enterprise AI knowledge architect. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Carry authority, effective date and owner metadata into retrieval; expose unresolved conflicts.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Better embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Carry authority, effective date and owner metadata into retrieval; expose unresolved conflicts. Better embeddings cannot resolve an organizational authority dispute without source governance. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the knowledge authority register and lifecycle plan with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Search for the same question under different dates and permissions after the correction. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"semantic-layer":{"revision":1,"title":"Revenue doubles after a join","brief":"The toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"duplicate_contributions","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","duplicate_contributions":0},{"id":"case-2","duplicate_contributions":1},{"id":"case-3","duplicate_contributions":2},{"id":"case-4","duplicate_contributions":0},{"id":"case-5","duplicate_contributions":3}],"policy":{"field":"duplicate_contributions","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"duplicate_contributions","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when duplicate_contributions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three queries inflate revenue through a grain-mismatched join.","repair":"Define grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level.","verify":"Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items.","deliverable":"Metric contract and reconciliation worksheet","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and metric contract and reconciliation worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using duplicate_contributions in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Define grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as metric contract and reconciliation worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: An apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Governed metrics catalog — Create synthetic commerce data.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Metric contract and reconciliation worksheet\n\nCase: Revenue doubles after a join\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Metric contract and reconciliation worksheet\nRole: Semantic-layer engineering for AI analytics\nCase: Revenue doubles after a join\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | duplicate_contributions (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree queries inflate revenue through a grain-mismatched join.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nDefine grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in semantic-layer engineering for ai analytics.\nBefore release: Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine metric semantics before natural-language access; restrict queries to tested governed models.\nAn apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGoverned metrics catalog: Two dashboards return the same result for the same question.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Semantic-layer engineering for AI analytics — Revenue doubles after a join\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.\n\n## Contract\nReview when duplicate_contributions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and metric contract and reconciliation worksheet template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using duplicate_contributions in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Define grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as metric contract and reconciliation worksheet. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: An apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCompare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Governed metrics catalog — Create synthetic commerce data.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- dbt: https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction\n- PostgreSQL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html\n- Hugging Face Agents Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction\n","evidence.csv":"id,duplicate_contributions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"duplicate_contributions\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"duplicate_contributions\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Metric contract and reconciliation worksheet\n\nCase: Revenue doubles after a join\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Metric contract and reconciliation worksheet\nRole: Semantic-layer engineering for AI analytics\nCase: Revenue doubles after a join\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | duplicate_contributions (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree queries inflate revenue through a grain-mismatched join.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nDefine grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in semantic-layer engineering for ai analytics.\nBefore release: Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine metric semantics before natural-language access; restrict queries to tested governed models.\nAn apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGoverned metrics catalog: Two dashboards return the same result for the same question.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"bab9effaf61d119847b06ac8433c7467362b34664a9c7bd22d3cb6c62d12d40a","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Revenue doubles after a join","scenario":"The toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Revenue doubles after a join\nid,duplicate_contributions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: duplicate_contributions >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when duplicate_contributions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three queries inflate revenue through a grain-mismatched join.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three queries inflate revenue through a grain-mismatched join. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"dbt","url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Revenue doubles after a join","scenario":"The toy semantic model permits no duplicate contribution of an order to the revenue metric.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Revenue doubles after a join\nid,duplicate_contributions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: duplicate_contributions >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when duplicate_contributions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Define grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level.","correct":true,"feedback":"Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Define grain and join keys explicitly and aggregate at the correct business level. Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"dbt","url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Semantic-layer engineering for AI analytics. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: An apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Define metric semantics before natural-language access; restrict queries to tested governed models.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Define metric semantics before natural-language access; restrict queries to tested governed models. An apparently fluent analytics answer can still be wrong when the underlying metric definition is inconsistent. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"dbt","url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your metric contract and reconciliation worksheet is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the metric contract and reconciliation worksheet with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Compare reference SQL totals and edge cases for dates, refunds and repeated line items. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"dbt","url":"https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction"},{"title":"PostgreSQL","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction"}]}]},"multilingual-evals":{"revision":1,"title":"A language average omits dialect failures","brief":"The pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"dialect_failures","unit":"out of ten tasks","rows":[{"id":"case-1","dialect_failures":0},{"id":"case-2","dialect_failures":2},{"id":"case-3","dialect_failures":3},{"id":"case-4","dialect_failures":6},{"id":"case-5","dialect_failures":1}],"policy":{"field":"dialect_failures","operator":">=","threshold":7},"expected":{"field":"dialect_failures","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when dialect_failures >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two dialect slices fail the declared gate while the pooled language score looks acceptable.","repair":"Add locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices.","verify":"Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit.","deliverable":"Multilingual evaluation and coverage report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and multilingual evaluation and coverage report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using dialect_failures in out of ten tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as multilingual evaluation and coverage report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Low-resource task bank — Choose a language you know.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Multilingual evaluation and coverage report\n\nCase: A language average omits dialect failures\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Multilingual evaluation and coverage report\nRole: Multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation\nCase: A language average omits dialect failures\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | dialect_failures (out of ten tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo dialect slices fail the declared gate while the pooled language score looks acceptable.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation.\nBefore release: Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine dialect and task coverage before sampling; avoid assuming translated English tasks cover local needs.\nMore slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nLow-resource task bank: Sources, dialect scope and rubric are documented.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation — A language average omits dialect failures\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.\n\n## Contract\nReview when dialect_failures >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and multilingual evaluation and coverage report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using dialect_failures in out of ten tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 7 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Add locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as multilingual evaluation and coverage report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nUse new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Low-resource task bank — Choose a language you know.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Label Studio: https://labelstud.io/guide/\n- Hugging Face Audio Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction\n- Hugging Face LLM Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1\n","evidence.csv":"id,dialect_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"dialect_failures\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 7\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"dialect_failures\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Multilingual evaluation and coverage report\n\nCase: A language average omits dialect failures\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Multilingual evaluation and coverage report\nRole: Multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation\nCase: A language average omits dialect failures\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | dialect_failures (out of ten tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 6 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo dialect slices fail the declared gate while the pooled language score looks acceptable.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nAdd locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation.\nBefore release: Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDefine dialect and task coverage before sampling; avoid assuming translated English tasks cover local needs.\nMore slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nLow-resource task bank: Sources, dialect scope and rubric are documented.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"2f42c2d00dda2c3d313cba45a80a7b1740b47402c9ff4255cd968d7ddd7ccca8","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A language average omits dialect failures","scenario":"The pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A language average omits dialect failures\nid,dialect_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: dialect_failures >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when dialect_failures >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Two dialect slices fail the declared gate while the pooled language score looks acceptable.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Two dialect slices fail the declared gate while the pooled language score looks acceptable. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A language average omits dialect failures","scenario":"The pilot flags more than two critical failures per ten tasks in each represented dialect.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A language average omits dialect failures\nid,dialect_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,6\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: dialect_failures >= 7\nRequired contract: Review when dialect_failures >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 7 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Add locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices.","correct":true,"feedback":"Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Add locally grounded tasks and qualified raters for the failing slices. Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Multilingual & low-resource-language evaluation. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten tasks as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Define dialect and task coverage before sampling; avoid assuming translated English tasks cover local needs.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Define dialect and task coverage before sampling; avoid assuming translated English tasks cover local needs. More slices need more evaluation effort; small samples cannot justify broad population claims. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your multilingual evaluation and coverage report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the multilingual evaluation and coverage report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Use new tasks, report sample sizes and keep disagreement and unrepresented populations explicit. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Label Studio","url":"https://labelstud.io/guide/"},{"title":"Hugging Face Audio Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/audio-course/chapter0/introduction"},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1"}]}]},"ai-accessibility":{"revision":1,"title":"Streaming output traps focus","brief":"The assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"focus_losses","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","focus_losses":0},{"id":"case-2","focus_losses":1},{"id":"case-3","focus_losses":0},{"id":"case-4","focus_losses":2},{"id":"case-5","focus_losses":3}],"policy":{"field":"focus_losses","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"focus_losses","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when focus_losses >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three tasks lose focus during updates, preventing keyboard users from completing them.","repair":"Keep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context.","verify":"Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology.","deliverable":"Accessibility findings report and interaction specification","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and accessibility findings report and interaction specification template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using focus_losses in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Keep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as accessibility findings report and interaction specification. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Automated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Accessible AI reading assistant — Prototype adjustable summaries and source links.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Accessibility findings report and interaction specification\n\nCase: Streaming output traps focus\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Accessibility findings report and interaction specification\nRole: AI accessibility & assistive interaction\nCase: Streaming output traps focus\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | focus_losses (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree tasks lose focus during updates, preventing keyboard users from completing them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nKeep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai accessibility & assistive interaction.\nBefore release: Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDesign status updates and recovery with affected users; combine technical and usability checks.\nAutomated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAccessible AI reading assistant: Updates do not overwhelm focus or hide source information.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI accessibility & assistive interaction — Streaming output traps focus\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.\n\n## Contract\nReview when focus_losses >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and accessibility findings report and interaction specification template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using focus_losses in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Keep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as accessibility findings report and interaction specification. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Automated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Accessible AI reading assistant — Prototype adjustable summaries and source links.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials: https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/\n- Figma learning resources: https://help.figma.com/\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n","evidence.csv":"id,focus_losses\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"focus_losses\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"focus_losses\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Accessibility findings report and interaction specification\n\nCase: Streaming output traps focus\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Accessibility findings report and interaction specification\nRole: AI accessibility & assistive interaction\nCase: Streaming output traps focus\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | focus_losses (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree tasks lose focus during updates, preventing keyboard users from completing them.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nKeep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai accessibility & assistive interaction.\nBefore release: Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nDesign status updates and recovery with affected users; combine technical and usability checks.\nAutomated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nAccessible AI reading assistant: Updates do not overwhelm focus or hide source information.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"37c35a6c7c71b87baa20d462fc0ced3ec12862d509b5fe795b29f341407d46d2","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Streaming output traps focus","scenario":"The assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Streaming output traps focus\nid,focus_losses\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: focus_losses >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when focus_losses >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three tasks lose focus during updates, preventing keyboard users from completing them.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three tasks lose focus during updates, preventing keyboard users from completing them. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Streaming output traps focus","scenario":"The assistive-interaction pilot treats any task with an unrecoverable keyboard-focus loss as a blocker.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Streaming output traps focus\nid,focus_losses\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: focus_losses >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when focus_losses >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Keep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Keep focus under user control and announce updates without replacing the active interaction context. Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI accessibility & assistive interaction. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Design status updates and recovery with affected users; combine technical and usability checks.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Automated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Design status updates and recovery with affected users; combine technical and usability checks. Automated accessibility checks find only part of the problem; real interaction can expose different barriers. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your accessibility findings report and interaction specification is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the accessibility findings report and interaction specification with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Test streaming, cancellation and correction with keyboard and relevant assistive technology. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials","url":"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/"},{"title":"Figma learning resources","url":"https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"NIST AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}]}]},"model-routing":{"revision":1,"title":"The cheapest route fails the task","brief":"The router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"unacceptable_outputs","unit":"out of ten tasks","rows":[{"id":"case-1","unacceptable_outputs":0},{"id":"case-2","unacceptable_outputs":2},{"id":"case-3","unacceptable_outputs":3},{"id":"case-4","unacceptable_outputs":5},{"id":"case-5","unacceptable_outputs":1}],"policy":{"field":"unacceptable_outputs","operator":">=","threshold":6},"expected":{"field":"unacceptable_outputs","operator":">=","threshold":3},"rule":"Review when unacceptable_outputs >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two cheaper-route buckets violate the quality constraint, so lower token cost is not enough.","repair":"Route difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost.","verify":"Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints.","deliverable":"Routing evaluation and unit-economics memo","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and routing evaluation and unit-economics memo template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unacceptable_outputs in out of ten tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Route difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as routing evaluation and unit-economics memo. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Routing adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Quality-constrained router — Label simple and difficult tasks.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Routing evaluation and unit-economics memo\n\nCase: The cheapest route fails the task\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Routing evaluation and unit-economics memo\nRole: Model routing & AI unit economics\nCase: The cheapest route fails the task\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unacceptable_outputs (out of ten tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cheaper-route buckets violate the quality constraint, so lower token cost is not enough.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRoute difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in model routing & ai unit economics.\nBefore release: Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure useful outcomes and routing errors; bound fallback and overload behavior.\nRouting adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nQuality-constrained router: Savings claims include fallback and quality-review costs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Model routing & AI unit economics — The cheapest route fails the task\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.\n\n## Contract\nReview when unacceptable_outputs >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and routing evaluation and unit-economics memo template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using unacceptable_outputs in out of ten tasks. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 6 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Route difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as routing evaluation and unit-economics memo. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Routing adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCompare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Quality-constrained router — Label simple and difficult tasks.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- FinOps Foundation: AI: https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/\n- MLflow: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/\n- vLLM: https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/\n","evidence.csv":"id,unacceptable_outputs\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"unacceptable_outputs\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 6\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"unacceptable_outputs\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 3\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Routing evaluation and unit-economics memo\n\nCase: The cheapest route fails the task\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Routing evaluation and unit-economics memo\nRole: Model routing & AI unit economics\nCase: The cheapest route fails the task\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | unacceptable_outputs (out of ten tasks) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 2 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 5 | review |\n| case-5 | 1 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo cheaper-route buckets violate the quality constraint, so lower token cost is not enough.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRoute difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in model routing & ai unit economics.\nBefore release: Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nMeasure useful outcomes and routing errors; bound fallback and overload behavior.\nRouting adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nQuality-constrained router: Savings claims include fallback and quality-review costs.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"af0d57764d36bd49dff73486136f88d9ed6fc202209e4079d92d35d0383523e5","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The cheapest route fails the task","scenario":"The router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The cheapest route fails the task\nid,unacceptable_outputs\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unacceptable_outputs >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when unacceptable_outputs >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two cheaper-route buckets violate the quality constraint, so lower token cost is not enough.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Two cheaper-route buckets violate the quality constraint, so lower token cost is not enough. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The cheapest route fails the task","scenario":"The router gate flags more than two unacceptable outputs in a ten-task evaluation bucket.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The cheapest route fails the task\nid,unacceptable_outputs\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,2\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,5\r\ncase-5,1\r\n\nCurrent review rule: unacceptable_outputs >= 6\nRequired contract: Review when unacceptable_outputs >= 3 (out of ten tasks). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Route difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost.","correct":true,"feedback":"Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 6 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Route difficult cases to a verified fallback and include retries and review in outcome cost. Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Model routing & AI unit economics. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Measure useful outcomes and routing errors; bound fallback and overload behavior.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Routing adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 3 out of ten tasks as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Measure useful outcomes and routing errors; bound fallback and overload behavior. Routing adds complexity and classification errors; simple policies may be more reliable than an unvalidated learned router. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your routing evaluation and unit-economics memo is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the routing evaluation and unit-economics memo with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Compare against a fixed-model baseline on identical tasks with quality and latency constraints. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"FinOps Foundation: AI","url":"https://www.finops.org/framework/technology-categories/ai/"},{"title":"MLflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/"},{"title":"vLLM","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/"}]}]},"edge-ai":{"revision":1,"title":"A model fits memory but drains the device","brief":"The device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"energy_per_task","unit":"millijoules","rows":[{"id":"case-1","energy_per_task":200},{"id":"case-2","energy_per_task":500},{"id":"case-3","energy_per_task":510},{"id":"case-4","energy_per_task":900},{"id":"case-5","energy_per_task":350}],"policy":{"field":"energy_per_task","operator":">","threshold":1000},"expected":{"field":"energy_per_task","operator":">","threshold":500},"rule":"Review when energy_per_task > 500 (millijoules). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Two configurations exceed the toy energy budget despite fitting device memory.","repair":"Profile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider.","verify":"Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware.","deliverable":"On-device performance and quality matrix","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and on-device performance and quality matrix template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using energy_per_task in millijoules. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 1000 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Profile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as on-device performance and quality matrix. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Offline image classifier — Choose a compact licensed model.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — On-device performance and quality matrix\n\nCase: A model fits memory but drains the device\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — On-device performance and quality matrix\nRole: Edge / on-device AI\nCase: A model fits memory but drains the device\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | energy_per_task (millijoules) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 200 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 500 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 510 | review |\n| case-4 | 900 | review |\n| case-5 | 350 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo configurations exceed the toy energy budget despite fitting device memory.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nProfile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in edge / on-device ai.\nBefore release: Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTreat hardware, operator support and update recovery as design constraints; preserve an offline fallback.\nA smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nOffline image classifier: Results name the hardware and accuracy/latency trade-off.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Edge / on-device AI — A model fits memory but drains the device\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.\n\n## Contract\nReview when energy_per_task > 500 (millijoules). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and on-device performance and quality matrix template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using energy_per_task in millijoules. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 1000 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Profile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as on-device performance and quality matrix. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: A smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReport task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Offline image classifier — Choose a compact licensed model.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 2 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- ONNX Runtime: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n- NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,energy_per_task\r\ncase-1,200\r\ncase-2,500\r\ncase-3,510\r\ncase-4,900\r\ncase-5,350\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"energy_per_task\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 1000\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"energy_per_task\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 500\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — On-device performance and quality matrix\n\nCase: A model fits memory but drains the device\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — On-device performance and quality matrix\nRole: Edge / on-device AI\nCase: A model fits memory but drains the device\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | energy_per_task (millijoules) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 200 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 500 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-3 | 510 | review |\n| case-4 | 900 | review |\n| case-5 | 350 | within_demo_bound |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n2/5 cases need review: case-3, case-4.\nTwo configurations exceed the toy energy budget despite fitting device memory.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nProfile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in edge / on-device ai.\nBefore release: Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nTreat hardware, operator support and update recovery as design constraints; preserve an offline fallback.\nA smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nOffline image classifier: Results name the hardware and accuracy/latency trade-off.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"dc7e17d0c1c1d9b6d4f18c948f1cfac38376258bf2a784ac5a1b7799e447ea60","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A model fits memory but drains the device","scenario":"The device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A model fits memory but drains the device\nid,energy_per_task\r\ncase-1,200\r\ncase-2,500\r\ncase-3,510\r\ncase-4,900\r\ncase-5,350\r\n\nCurrent review rule: energy_per_task > 1000\nRequired contract: Review when energy_per_task > 500 (millijoules). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Two configurations exceed the toy energy budget despite fitting device memory.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-3, case-4. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Two configurations exceed the toy energy budget despite fitting device memory. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A model fits memory but drains the device","scenario":"The device trial flags sustained inference consuming more than 500 mJ per accepted task under the stated measurement boundary.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A model fits memory but drains the device\nid,energy_per_task\r\ncase-1,200\r\ncase-2,500\r\ncase-3,510\r\ncase-4,900\r\ncase-5,350\r\n\nCurrent review rule: energy_per_task > 1000\nRequired contract: Review when energy_per_task > 500 (millijoules). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Profile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider.","correct":true,"feedback":"Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 1000 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-3 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Profile on the intended device and compare model size, precision and execution provider. Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Edge / on-device AI. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: A smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 500 millijoules as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Treat hardware, operator support and update recovery as design constraints; preserve an offline fallback.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Treat hardware, operator support and update recovery as design constraints; preserve an offline fallback. A smaller model may save energy while lowering quality; measure cost per accepted task rather than inference alone. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your on-device performance and quality matrix is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the on-device performance and quality matrix with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Report task quality, warm/cold latency, memory and sustained energy on named hardware. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"ONNX Runtime","url":"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"},{"title":"NVIDIA CUDA Programming Guide","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html"}]}]},"robotics-simulation":{"revision":1,"title":"Simulation success hides collisions","brief":"The simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"collisions","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","collisions":0},{"id":"case-2","collisions":1},{"id":"case-3","collisions":0},{"id":"case-4","collisions":2},{"id":"case-5","collisions":3}],"policy":{"field":"collisions","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"collisions","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when collisions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three simulated successes violate the collision condition and cannot count as valid completion.","repair":"Make collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal.","verify":"Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct.","deliverable":"Simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using collisions in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Make collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Simulated pick-and-place evaluation — Define a simple scene and task.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan\n\nCase: Simulation success hides collisions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan\nRole: Robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering\nCase: Simulation success hides collisions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | collisions (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree simulated successes violate the collision condition and cannot count as valid completion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMake collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering.\nBefore release: Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nEvaluate safety and completion separately; stage any physical validation with qualified supervision.\nMore realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSimulated pick-and-place evaluation: Publish seeds, simulator settings and failure cases.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering — Simulation success hides collisions\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.\n\n## Contract\nReview when collisions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using collisions in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Make collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: More realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReplay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Simulated pick-and-place evaluation — Define a simple scene and task.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Hugging Face Robotics Course: https://huggingface.co/learn/robotics-course/unit0/1\n- ROS 2 tutorials: https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,collisions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"collisions\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"collisions\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan\n\nCase: Simulation success hides collisions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan\nRole: Robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering\nCase: Simulation success hides collisions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | collisions (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree simulated successes violate the collision condition and cannot count as valid completion.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nMake collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering.\nBefore release: Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nEvaluate safety and completion separately; stage any physical validation with qualified supervision.\nMore realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nSimulated pick-and-place evaluation: Publish seeds, simulator settings and failure cases.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"c7adfc4aacd0da46ed3ccb05387c48f42ba5bbabfdec90566029e533ae679fb1","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Simulation success hides collisions","scenario":"The simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Simulation success hides collisions\nid,collisions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: collisions >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when collisions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three simulated successes violate the collision condition and cannot count as valid completion.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three simulated successes violate the collision condition and cannot count as valid completion. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Robotics Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/robotics-course/unit0/1"},{"title":"ROS 2 tutorials","url":"https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Simulation success hides collisions","scenario":"The simulated task protocol rejects any run with a collision, even if the object reaches its target.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Simulation success hides collisions\nid,collisions\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: collisions >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when collisions >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Make collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal.","correct":true,"feedback":"Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Make collision and control-limit checks independent of the learned success signal. Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Robotics Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/robotics-course/unit0/1"},{"title":"ROS 2 tutorials","url":"https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Robotics simulation & sim-to-real engineering. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Evaluate safety and completion separately; stage any physical validation with qualified supervision.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: More realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Evaluate safety and completion separately; stage any physical validation with qualified supervision. More realistic simulation reduces some gaps but cannot establish safe real-world behavior by itself. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Robotics Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/robotics-course/unit0/1"},{"title":"ROS 2 tutorials","url":"https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the simulation test matrix and supervised transfer plan with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Replay varied friction, sensor noise and latency; keep simulated and hardware evidence distinct. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Robotics Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/robotics-course/unit0/1"},{"title":"ROS 2 tutorials","url":"https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]}]},"industrial-ai":{"revision":1,"title":"Maintenance alerts arrive too late","brief":"For this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"lead_time_shortfall","unit":"minutes","rows":[{"id":"case-1","lead_time_shortfall":0},{"id":"case-2","lead_time_shortfall":5},{"id":"case-3","lead_time_shortfall":0},{"id":"case-4","lead_time_shortfall":20},{"id":"case-5","lead_time_shortfall":40}],"policy":{"field":"lead_time_shortfall","operator":">","threshold":45},"expected":{"field":"lead_time_shortfall","operator":">","threshold":0},"rule":"Review when lead_time_shortfall > 0 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three warnings miss the required maintenance window; event detection alone is insufficient.","repair":"Evaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow.","verify":"Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment.","deliverable":"Maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using lead_time_shortfall in minutes. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 45 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Evaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Earlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Predictive-maintenance demo — Generate or license equipment time series.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note\n\nCase: Maintenance alerts arrive too late\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note\nRole: Industrial AI / digital twins\nCase: Maintenance alerts arrive too late\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nFor this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | lead_time_shortfall (minutes) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 5 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 20 | review |\n| case-5 | 40 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree warnings miss the required maintenance window; event detection alone is insufficient.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEvaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in industrial ai / digital twins.\nBefore release: Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine domain constraints, sensor validation and human decisions; separate prediction from physical control.\nEarlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPredictive-maintenance demo: Report detection lead time and false alarms per operating hour.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# Industrial AI / digital twins — Maintenance alerts arrive too late\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nFor this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.\n\n## Contract\nReview when lead_time_shortfall > 0 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using lead_time_shortfall in minutes. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 45 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Evaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Earlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReplay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Predictive-maintenance demo — Generate or license equipment time series.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Siemens digital twin overview: https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/digital-twin/\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- Prometheus: https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\n","evidence.csv":"id,lead_time_shortfall\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,5\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,20\r\ncase-5,40\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"lead_time_shortfall\",\n  \"operator\": \">\",\n  \"threshold\": 45\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"lead_time_shortfall\",\n    \"operator\": \">\",\n    \"threshold\": 0\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note\n\nCase: Maintenance alerts arrive too late\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note\nRole: Industrial AI / digital twins\nCase: Maintenance alerts arrive too late\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nFor this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | lead_time_shortfall (minutes) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 5 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 20 | review |\n| case-5 | 40 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree warnings miss the required maintenance window; event detection alone is insufficient.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nEvaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in industrial ai / digital twins.\nBefore release: Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nCombine domain constraints, sensor validation and human decisions; separate prediction from physical control.\nEarlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nPredictive-maintenance demo: Report detection lead time and false alarms per operating hour.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"b391c75fa4d9bacf69643b193b569f93a8ab2c36b77a5b4fdc0524a154949211","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Maintenance alerts arrive too late","scenario":"For this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Maintenance alerts arrive too late\nid,lead_time_shortfall\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,5\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,20\r\ncase-5,40\r\n\nCurrent review rule: lead_time_shortfall > 45\nRequired contract: Review when lead_time_shortfall > 0 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three warnings miss the required maintenance window; event detection alone is insufficient.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."}],"model":"Three warnings miss the required maintenance window; event detection alone is insufficient. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Siemens digital twin overview","url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/digital-twin/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Maintenance alerts arrive too late","scenario":"For this synthetic process, a warning needs at least 30 minutes of useful lead time; measure the shortfall from that target.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Maintenance alerts arrive too late\nid,lead_time_shortfall\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,5\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,20\r\ncase-5,40\r\n\nCurrent review rule: lead_time_shortfall > 45\nRequired contract: Review when lead_time_shortfall > 0 (minutes). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Evaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow.","correct":true,"feedback":"Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 45 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."}],"model":"Evaluate event-level lead time and false alarms against the maintenance workflow. Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Siemens digital twin overview","url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/digital-twin/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of Industrial AI / digital twins. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 0 minutes as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Combine domain constraints, sensor validation and human decisions; separate prediction from physical control.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Earlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."}],"model":"Combine domain constraints, sensor validation and human decisions; separate prediction from physical control. Earlier alerts may increase false alarms; the useful trade-off depends on maintenance cost and response time. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Siemens digital twin overview","url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/digital-twin/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the maintenance alert evaluation and operating-envelope note with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."}],"model":"Replay temporal holdouts and changed operating regimes without controlling real equipment. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Siemens digital twin overview","url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/digital-twin/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"Prometheus","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/"}]}]},"ai-biology":{"revision":1,"title":"A scaffold appears in both partitions","brief":"The molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"shared_scaffolds","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","shared_scaffolds":0},{"id":"case-2","shared_scaffolds":1},{"id":"case-3","shared_scaffolds":2},{"id":"case-4","shared_scaffolds":0},{"id":"case-5","shared_scaffolds":3}],"policy":{"field":"shared_scaffolds","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"shared_scaffolds","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when shared_scaffolds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three evaluations leak scaffold families and overstate out-of-family generalization.","repair":"Rebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models.","verify":"Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer.","deliverable":"Molecular data audit and applicability report","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and molecular data audit and applicability report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_scaffolds in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Rebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as molecular data audit and applicability report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Molecular property benchmark — Use a permitted public dataset.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Molecular data audit and applicability report\n\nCase: A scaffold appears in both partitions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Molecular data audit and applicability report\nRole: AI for biology / drug discovery\nCase: A scaffold appears in both partitions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_scaffolds (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree evaluations leak scaffold families and overstate out-of-family generalization.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai for biology / drug discovery.\nBefore release: Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nPreserve assay units and provenance; distinguish prediction from experimental validation.\nStrong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMolecular property benchmark: Report leakage checks and performance outside familiar scaffolds.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI for biology / drug discovery — A scaffold appears in both partitions\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.\n\n## Contract\nReview when shared_scaffolds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and molecular data audit and applicability report template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using shared_scaffolds in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Rebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as molecular data audit and applicability report. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Strong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nReport performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Molecular property benchmark — Use a permitted public dataset.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- RDKit documentation: https://www.rdkit.org/docs/\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,shared_scaffolds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"shared_scaffolds\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"shared_scaffolds\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Molecular data audit and applicability report\n\nCase: A scaffold appears in both partitions\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Molecular data audit and applicability report\nRole: AI for biology / drug discovery\nCase: A scaffold appears in both partitions\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | shared_scaffolds (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 2 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree evaluations leak scaffold families and overstate out-of-family generalization.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nRebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai for biology / drug discovery.\nBefore release: Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nPreserve assay units and provenance; distinguish prediction from experimental validation.\nStrong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMolecular property benchmark: Report leakage checks and performance outside familiar scaffolds.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"233bd43c504c24dbc93f7b75acc51414db2c3526092098dbfa35c9bb698ab781","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"A scaffold appears in both partitions","scenario":"The molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A scaffold appears in both partitions\nid,shared_scaffolds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_scaffolds >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_scaffolds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three evaluations leak scaffold families and overstate out-of-family generalization.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."}],"model":"Three evaluations leak scaffold families and overstate out-of-family generalization. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"RDKit documentation","url":"https://www.rdkit.org/docs/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: A scaffold appears in both partitions","scenario":"The molecular-property protocol forbids the same scaffold family in training and held-out evaluation.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — A scaffold appears in both partitions\nid,shared_scaffolds\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,2\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: shared_scaffolds >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when shared_scaffolds >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Rebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models.","correct":true,"feedback":"Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."}],"model":"Rebuild scaffold-aware splits before fitting preprocessing and comparing models. Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"RDKit documentation","url":"https://www.rdkit.org/docs/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI for biology / drug discovery. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"design","text":"Preserve assay units and provenance; distinguish prediction from experimental validation.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Strong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."}],"model":"Preserve assay units and provenance; distinguish prediction from experimental validation. Strong computational scores do not establish efficacy or safety and may not transfer to new chemistry. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"RDKit documentation","url":"https://www.rdkit.org/docs/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your molecular data audit and applicability report is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the molecular data audit and applicability report with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."}],"model":"Report performance by chemical domain and assay conditions; involve a qualified scientific reviewer. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"RDKit documentation","url":"https://www.rdkit.org/docs/"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]}]},"scientific-ai":{"revision":1,"title":"Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation","brief":"The geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Python replay + evidence workbook","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"overlapping_tiles","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","overlapping_tiles":0},{"id":"case-2","overlapping_tiles":1},{"id":"case-3","overlapping_tiles":3},{"id":"case-4","overlapping_tiles":0},{"id":"case-5","overlapping_tiles":2}],"policy":{"field":"overlapping_tiles","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"overlapping_tiles","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when overlapping_tiles >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three spatial splits overlap and undermine the claim of generalization to new regions.","repair":"Use geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries.","verify":"Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions.","deliverable":"Spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":["python3 --version"]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using overlapping_tiles in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":["python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Use geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":["python3 verify.py","python3 analyze.py"]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Geographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":["python3 verify.py"]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Geospatial change study — Select licensed satellite imagery.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table\n\nCase: Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table\nRole: AI for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis\nCase: Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | overlapping_tiles (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree spatial splits overlap and undermine the claim of generalization to new regions.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nUse geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis.\nBefore release: Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nChoose domain-valid splits and physical plausibility checks; retain uncertainty and provenance.\nGeographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGeospatial change study: Report cloud masking, spatial leakage and uncertainty.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis — Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.\n\n## Contract\nReview when overlapping_tiles >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Download and extract the pack into a new folder; open a terminal in that folder. Python 3.9+ is sufficient and there are no external dependencies.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n```sh\npython3 --version\n```\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using overlapping_tiles in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n```sh\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Use geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\npython3 analyze.py\n```\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Geographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nCompare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n```sh\npython3 verify.py\n```\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Geospatial change study — Select licensed satellite imagery.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- Google Earth Engine tutorials: https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/tutorials\n- scikit-learn: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html\n- PyTorch: https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html\n","evidence.csv":"id,overlapping_tiles\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"overlapping_tiles\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"overlapping_tiles\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table\n\nCase: Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table\nRole: AI for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis\nCase: Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | overlapping_tiles (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 3 | review |\n| case-4 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-5 | 2 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-3, case-5.\nThree spatial splits overlap and undermine the claim of generalization to new regions.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nUse geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis.\nBefore release: Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nChoose domain-valid splits and physical plausibility checks; retain uncertainty and provenance.\nGeographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nGeospatial change study: Report cloud masking, spatial leakage and uncertainty.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"d1643af8a4397686aa02ba50da2e176265b3e9f09b71ac80ab10905fa7c3c5dc","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation","scenario":"The geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\nid,overlapping_tiles\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: overlapping_tiles >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when overlapping_tiles >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."},{"id":"supported","text":"Three spatial splits overlap and undermine the claim of generalization to new regions.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-3, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."}],"model":"Three spatial splits overlap and undermine the claim of generalization to new regions. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"Google Earth Engine tutorials","url":"https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/tutorials"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation","scenario":"The geospatial protocol requires no sampled tile overlap between training and evaluation regions.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — Spatial neighbors leak into evaluation\nid,overlapping_tiles\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,3\r\ncase-4,0\r\ncase-5,2\r\n\nCurrent review rule: overlapping_tiles >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when overlapping_tiles >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"repair","text":"Use geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries.","correct":true,"feedback":"Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."},{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."}],"model":"Use geographically separated holdouts and document resolution, masks and time boundaries. Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"Google Earth Engine tutorials","url":"https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/tutorials"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI for materials, climate, energy & geospatial analysis. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Geographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."},{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Choose domain-valid splits and physical plausibility checks; retain uncertainty and provenance.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."}],"model":"Choose domain-valid splits and physical plausibility checks; retain uncertainty and provenance. Geographic separation can make scores lower but is more informative for deployment in an unfamiliar region. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"Google Earth Engine tutorials","url":"https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/tutorials"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."},{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the spatial-validation report and scientific assumptions table with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."}],"model":"Compare physical or statistical baselines and report uncertainty in unseen regions. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"Google Earth Engine tutorials","url":"https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/tutorials/tutorials"},{"title":"scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"PyTorch","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"}]}]},"content-provenance":{"revision":1,"title":"The asset changed after signing","brief":"The provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.","scope":"Local synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.","mode":"Browser / spreadsheet workbook; Python optional","estimate":"Allow 60–120 minutes for the guided exercise, plus prerequisite study and peer review. Editorial planning allowance.","field":"binding_failures","unit":"count","rows":[{"id":"case-1","binding_failures":0},{"id":"case-2","binding_failures":1},{"id":"case-3","binding_failures":0},{"id":"case-4","binding_failures":2},{"id":"case-5","binding_failures":3}],"policy":{"field":"binding_failures","operator":">=","threshold":4},"expected":{"field":"binding_failures","operator":">=","threshold":1},"rule":"Review when binding_failures >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","finding":"Three assets no longer match their recorded binding; the UI must not present them as verified.","repair":"Display valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context.","verify":"Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification.","deliverable":"Provenance inspection report and status-language guide","steps":[{"title":"Set up and define the contract","action":"Read the brief and provenance inspection report and status-language guide template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.","check":"You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.","commands":[]},{"title":"Inspect the evidence before checking the answer","action":"Read evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using binding_failures in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.","check":"All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.","commands":[]},{"title":"Reproduce and repair the broken review rule","action":"The supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Display valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.","check":"Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.","commands":[]},{"title":"Write the role-specific deliverable","action":"Complete the draft template as provenance inspection report and status-language guide. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Valid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification.","check":"A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.","commands":[]},{"title":"Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption","action":"Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.","check":"Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.","commands":[]},{"title":"Compare, revise and present","action":"Reveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Media provenance inspector — Use public specification examples.","check":"Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.","commands":[]}],"template":"# WORKING DRAFT — Provenance inspection report and status-language guide\n\nCase: The asset changed after signing\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample":"# SAMPLE — Provenance inspection report and status-language guide\nRole: AI content provenance & authenticity\nCase: The asset changed after signing\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | binding_failures (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree assets no longer match their recorded binding; the UI must not present them as verified.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nDisplay valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai content provenance & authenticity.\nBefore release: Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate provenance integrity from factual truth; preserve ingredient and transformation history.\nValid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMedia provenance inspector: UI distinguishes unknown provenance from detected tampering.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n","files":{"README.md":"# AI content provenance & authenticity — The asset changed after signing\n\nLocal synthetic evidence-and-decision lab. The Python replay tests a numeric contract; it does not call a model, run a GPU workload, deploy cloud infrastructure or validate a real business decision.\n\n## Brief\nThe provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.\n\n## Contract\nReview when binding_failures >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.\n\n## Setup\nExtract this ZIP into a new empty folder. Open a terminal there for the optional Python replay. On macOS/Linux use python3; on Windows use py -3 instead. Python 3.9+; standard library only. No installation commands or paid resources. Spreadsheet/browser learners can complete the same table and memo without Python.\n\n## 1. Set up and define the contract\nRead the brief and provenance inspection report and status-language guide template. Restate the acceptance rule in your own words, including its units. Use this browser, a text editor or any spreadsheet. No coding or paid account is required. Download the pack if you want to work offline in a spreadsheet.\n\nExit check: You can distinguish the case contract from a general industry rule.\n\n## 2. Inspect the evidence before checking the answer\nRead evidence.csv. Make a five-row decision table using binding_failures in count. Mark each record as review or within_demo_bound. Explain what a value exactly on the threshold means.\n\nExit check: All five records have a decision and an evidence-based reason; keep this first attempt.\n\n## 3. Reproduce and repair the broken review rule\nThe supplied rule uses 4 and misses the case failures. Use the threshold and comparison controls, or edit policy.json locally, to match the stated contract. Do not edit the evidence or answer contract to make a test pass. Display valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context. The threshold edit repairs only this replay, not the underlying system.\n\nExit check: Run Check policy. The fixture and boundary cases must agree with the contract.\n\n## 4. Write the role-specific deliverable\nComplete the draft template as provenance inspection report and status-language guide. Cite record IDs, state your decision, name a fictional accountable owner and distinguish observations from causal hypotheses. Address this trade-off: Valid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification.\n\nExit check: A reviewer can trace the recommendation to evidence and identify who must act.\n\n## 5. Rehearse verification and challenge an assumption\nTest altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification. Add one new example on each side of the boundary and one exactly at it. Explain how missing or unreliable evidence should be handled. For this lab, an absent numeric field is an error, not an automatic pass.\n\nExit check: Document expected results, one rejected alternative and a condition that reopens the finding.\n\n## 6. Compare, revise and present\nReveal the sample only after drafting. Compare the evidence table, decision, trade-off and limitations; revise your own artifact rather than copying it. Present a two-minute explanation. Then extend the work toward: Media provenance inspector — Use public specification examples.\n\nExit check: Export your notes and compare with a peer. Self-checkmarks do not certify the written deliverable.\n\n## Expected execution\nThe original policy deliberately reports zero reviews; verify.py exits nonzero on the fixture and boundary checks. After repair, the report lists 3 review cases and all four tests pass. The report prints to standard output and does not overwrite your files.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Missing file: open the terminal in the extracted pack directory.\n- Python missing: use the in-browser exercise; install Python only from its official distribution if you choose the local route.\n- JSON error: use double quotes, numeric threshold and no trailing comma.\n- Fixture passes but boundary fails: distinguish > from >= and keep the stated units.\n- Missing evidence: reject the malformed record for review; do not silently treat missing as zero.\n\n## Files and answer disclosure\npolicy.json is the intentionally broken rule; contract.json contains the check oracle. sample-deliverable.md is the worked answer. Keep them closed until you have made an attempt. learner-deliverable.md is your editable template. In-browser notes are separate from these local files.\n\n## Sources\n- C2PA specifications: https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/\n- Blender manual: https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project: https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\n","evidence.csv":"id,binding_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n","policy.json":"{\n  \"field\": \"binding_failures\",\n  \"operator\": \">=\",\n  \"threshold\": 4\n}\n","contract.json":"{\n  \"policy\": {\n    \"field\": \"binding_failures\",\n    \"operator\": \">=\",\n    \"threshold\": 1\n  },\n  \"decisions\": [\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"within_demo_bound\",\n    \"review\",\n    \"review\"\n  ]\n}\n","analyze.py":"\"\"\"Local synthetic evidence replay. No model calls, network or paid resources.\"\"\"\nimport argparse,csv,json,math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef validate(policy):\n    if not isinstance(policy,dict) or set(policy)!={'field','operator','threshold'}:\n        raise ValueError('Use exactly field, operator and threshold')\n    if not isinstance(policy['field'],str) or policy['operator'] not in ('>=','>'):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid field or operator')\n    if isinstance(policy['threshold'],bool) or not isinstance(policy['threshold'],(int,float)) or not math.isfinite(policy['threshold']):\n        raise ValueError('Threshold must be a finite number')\n    return policy\n\ndef classify(rows,policy):\n    validate(policy)\n    result=[]\n    for row in rows:\n        value=float(row[policy['field']])\n        if not math.isfinite(value): raise ValueError('Evidence values must be finite')\n        flag=value>=policy['threshold'] if policy['operator']=='>=' else value>policy['threshold']\n        result.append({'id':row['id'],'value':value,'decision':'review' if flag else 'within_demo_bound'})\n    return result\n\nif __name__=='__main__':\n    parser=argparse.ArgumentParser();parser.add_argument('--policy',default='policy.json');parser.add_argument('--data',default='evidence.csv');args=parser.parse_args()\n    with open(args.data,newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n    policy=json.loads(Path(args.policy).read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    report=classify(rows,policy)\n    print(json.dumps({'synthetic':True,'records':report,'review_count':sum(r['decision']=='review' for r in report)},indent=2))\n","verify.py":"\"\"\"Run from the extracted pack directory. Intentionally fails until policy.json is repaired.\"\"\"\nimport csv,json,unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom analyze import classify,validate\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent\ncontract=json.loads((ROOT/'contract.json').read_text())\npolicy=json.loads((ROOT/'policy.json').read_text())\nclass ContractTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_fixture_decisions(self):\n        with (ROOT/'evidence.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f: rows=list(csv.DictReader(f))\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],contract['decisions'])\n    def test_boundary(self):\n        expected=contract['policy']; t=expected['threshold']; delta=max(abs(t)*0.001,0.000001)\n        rows=[{'id':str(i),expected['field']:v} for i,v in enumerate([t-delta,t,t+delta])]\n        target=['within_demo_bound','review' if expected['operator']=='>=' else 'within_demo_bound','review']\n        self.assertEqual([r['decision'] for r in classify(rows,policy)],target)\n    def test_bad_threshold_rejected(self):\n        for value in [True,'1',float('nan'),float('inf')]:\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError): validate({**policy,'threshold':value})\n    def test_missing_evidence_rejected(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(KeyError): classify([{'id':'missing'}],policy)\nif __name__=='__main__': unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n","learner-deliverable.md":"# WORKING DRAFT — Provenance inspection report and status-language guide\n\nCase: The asset changed after signing\n\n## Decision and scope\n[What is being decided? What is outside the lab?]\n\n## Evidence\n[Record IDs, values, units and expected treatment; do not hide contrary evidence.]\n\n## Finding and alternative explanation\n[Separate observation from hypothesis.]\n\n## Proposed action and accountable owner\n[Name a role, a bounded action and a review checkpoint.]\n\n## Verification and reopening condition\n[State positive, negative and boundary checks.]\n\n## Trade-off and limitations\n[Compare an alternative and state what is still untested.]\n","sample-deliverable.md":"# SAMPLE — Provenance inspection report and status-language guide\nRole: AI content provenance & authenticity\nCase: The asset changed after signing\nStatus: Educational example using five synthetic records; not real deployment or user-study evidence.\n\n## Decision and scope\nHold the affected cases for review. Continue only cases within this one demonstration bound; that label does not establish full system quality or safety.\nThe provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.\n\n## Evidence table\n| Record | binding_failures (count) | Decision |\n|---|---:|---|\n| case-1 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-2 | 1 | review |\n| case-3 | 0 | within_demo_bound |\n| case-4 | 2 | review |\n| case-5 | 3 | review |\n\n## Finding and reasoning\n3/5 cases need review: case-2, case-4, case-5.\nThree assets no longer match their recorded binding; the UI must not present them as verified.\nThe initial demo rule hides these violations. The evidence identifies a contract failure; it does not establish every underlying causal mechanism.\n\n## Proposed action\nDisplay valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context.\nOwner (fictional): pilot lead; reviewer: a practitioner qualified in ai content provenance & authenticity.\nBefore release: Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification.\n\n## Design choice and trade-off\nSeparate provenance integrity from factual truth; preserve ingredient and transformation history.\nValid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification.\n\n## Acceptance and reopening conditions\nRe-run all five records and values immediately below, at and above the boundary. Any expected review case that passes reopens the finding. Inspect a fresh representative sample before a real decision; do not generalize from these five records.\n\n## Next deliverable\nMedia provenance inspector: UI distinguishes unknown provenance from detected tampering.\nKeep original evidence, revised rule, replay output and this decision record together. A report alone is not evidence that the proposed operational repair was implemented.\n"},"zip":"","sha256":"e273f1faa261e50ae2b837c1edb98c6f937bc16c8bb050454e70f112aae66ed3","questions":[{"id":"diagnosis","area":"diagnosis","kind":"single","title":"The asset changed after signing","scenario":"The provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The asset changed after signing\nid,binding_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: binding_failures >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when binding_failures >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?","options":[{"id":"supported","text":"Three assets no longer match their recorded binding; the UI must not present them as verified.","correct":true,"feedback":"The required rule identifies case-2, case-4, case-5. The current higher threshold hides these cases. This is a bounded conclusion from the fixture, not a general production diagnosis."},{"id":"current","text":"All five cases are acceptable because the current replay rule reports no reviews.","correct":false,"feedback":"The implementation is the object under test. Its output cannot override the separately stated acceptance contract."},{"id":"all","text":"All five cases must be rejected because one observed failure invalidates every record.","correct":false,"feedback":"Apply the contract to each row. Some rows meet this particular bound; that does not establish all other quality requirements."},{"id":"none","text":"No conclusion at all can be drawn because the evidence is synthetic.","correct":false,"feedback":"Synthetic evidence can establish behavior within a declared exercise. It cannot establish real-world frequency or deployment performance."}],"model":"Three assets no longer match their recorded binding; the UI must not present them as verified. Cite the violating records, distinguish the implemented rule from the required contract, and state what further evidence would establish the underlying cause.","writing":"Name two observations, a competing explanation and the evidence you would request next.","sources":[{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"}]},{"id":"recovery","area":"recovery","kind":"single","title":"Repair and verify: The asset changed after signing","scenario":"The provenance viewer must flag any asset-binding validation failure; missing credentials are a separate unknown state.","evidence":"SYNTHETIC EVIDENCE — The asset changed after signing\nid,binding_failures\r\ncase-1,0\r\ncase-2,1\r\ncase-3,0\r\ncase-4,2\r\ncase-5,3\r\n\nCurrent review rule: binding_failures >= 4\nRequired contract: Review when binding_failures >= 1 (count). This is a declared toy-case contract, not an industry standard.","prompt":"Which next action best addresses the observed contract failure?","options":[{"id":"relax","text":"Raise the acceptance boundary to 4 so that all existing cases pass.","correct":false,"feedback":"Changing the requirement to match a faulty implementation conceals the failure instead of addressing it."},{"id":"one","text":"Fix only case-2 and close the issue after that single case passes.","correct":false,"feedback":"A one-case check misses other affected cases and boundary behavior. Replay the entire suite and a fresh representative sample."},{"id":"replace","text":"Replace the tool or provider immediately and skip verification because the implementation is new.","correct":false,"feedback":"A replacement can reproduce the same design or process flaw. It still needs evidence against the agreed contract."},{"id":"repair","text":"Display valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context.","correct":true,"feedback":"Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification. Treat a repaired replay as a local check, not proof of the proposed operational change."}],"model":"Display valid, invalid and unknown provenance distinctly and retain trust-chain context. Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification. Preserve before/after evidence and an accountable owner; state the condition that would reverse the decision.","writing":"Order mitigation, durable correction and verification. Explain what the local exercise cannot prove.","sources":[{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"}]},{"id":"architecture","area":"architecture","kind":"multi","title":"Design the next version","scenario":"You own the next iteration of this pilot in the role of AI content provenance & authenticity. Keep the stated contract while making the workflow reviewable.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two defensible design decisions.","options":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide the failing records from the published results so the overall score remains easy to explain.","correct":false,"feedback":"Selective reporting makes the decision unreviewable and hides the cases the contract was intended to catch."},{"id":"universal","text":"Treat 1 count as a universal industry threshold for every future customer.","correct":false,"feedback":"The number belongs to a synthetic brief. Real requirements depend on workload, affected users, domain and evidence."},{"id":"design","text":"Separate provenance integrity from factual truth; preserve ingredient and transformation history.","correct":true,"feedback":"This targets the actual responsibility boundary rather than relying on a successful-looking demo."},{"id":"tradeoff","text":"Document this constraint in the decision record: Valid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification.","correct":true,"feedback":"A design review should expose the cost and limits of the chosen approach, including what would change the decision."}],"model":"Separate provenance integrity from factual truth; preserve ingredient and transformation history. Valid credentials can describe a fabricated scene; provenance supports origin review, not truth certification. Sketch the actor → evidence → decision → action → review path. Keep permissions and ownership explicit.","writing":"Draw a system or process map, compare an alternative and explain the main trade-off.","sources":[{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"}]},{"id":"operations","area":"operations","kind":"multi","title":"Handover and sustained quality","scenario":"A teammate will own this pilot after your provenance inspection report and status-language guide is accepted.","evidence":"","prompt":"Select the two strongest handover decisions.","options":[{"id":"score","text":"Use a perfect five-row replay score as the sole authorization for an unrestricted real-world launch.","correct":false,"feedback":"This tiny synthetic exercise cannot establish domain coverage, security, real-user outcomes or deployment readiness."},{"id":"verify","text":"Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification.","correct":true,"feedback":"This verifies the relevant user or review boundary instead of a superficial success signal."},{"id":"owner","text":"Deliver the provenance inspection report and status-language guide with source records, rule version, review owner and reopening conditions.","correct":true,"feedback":"Evidence and ownership allow another person to challenge, reproduce and revisit the decision."},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete the initial failed examples after the revised rule passes to keep the handover concise.","correct":false,"feedback":"Keep representative failures as regression evidence; remove sensitive data appropriately rather than erasing the failure history."}],"model":"Test altered files, stripped metadata and unknown signers with the selected specification. Retain the evidence and the change history. Name an owner, define when to stop or reopen, and assess a new representative sample before expansion.","writing":"Give two measurable review signals, an accountable role and an explicit stop or rollback condition.","sources":[{"title":"C2PA specifications","url":"https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/"},{"title":"Blender manual","url":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/"},{"title":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"}]}]}}};
